Oho, what an open invitation!  Though this will certainly risk
sounding like Monday morning quarterbacking, I think that many
of us can think of more than just three major examples of dumb
corporate strategy.

I'll limit my own comments to just one:  Digital's long-running
antipathy to UNIX, starting at least as far back as the late 70s
and continuing, to one degree or another, until the early 90s at
least (and many would opine that it continues to this day, though
I'd admit that once the Alpha+OSF1 combination was on the market,
Digital was finally serious about providing a decent UNIX).

I remember when Bill Munson, Armando Stettner, and a few dedicated
others did their best to make a case that Digital should *support*
UNIX, not fight it.  By 1979 or thereabouts, it was clear to many
people that UNIX is what a lot of customers would want to use, and
it *should* have been clear to Digital's management that it would
be wisest to jump on the bandwagon right from the start.  Instead,
though, they persisted in trying to resist an unstoppable force.

It's interesting to speculate how different things might have been
if Digital had taken a strong, pro-active approach with regard to
UNIX support, and UNIX-based products, to serve the needs of those
customers who ended up dumping Digital for Sun, and later for SGI,
HP, and even -- yes even -- IBM.

Instead, Digital's management tended, for years, to look at their
own UNIX group in much the same way that the FBI views Fox Mulder.

Sigh.

