Stan Lass wrote:
> 
> In article <34D02C2C.131E@EasyInternet.net>, PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net
> says...
> >   snip
> >The architectural and compiler advantages that Merced enjoys over
> >classical RISC ISAs will really start to shine as high end
> >implementations reach 6 issue width and head towards 12+ issue width.
> 
> Implied, I think, in what you're saying and in IA-64 hype is that highly
> superscalar processors make economic sense. I see it differently.
> 
> In a simple task, e.g. adding two numbers, there's little opportunity for
> parallelism. In a large task, e.g. designing the followon to the Boeing 777,
> there's is much opportunity for parallelism.
> 
> Similarly, in small programs,there is little opportunity for parallelism.
> The faster that single processors become, the larger the tasks that can be
> completed, and on average, the more and coarser the parallelism there
> will be in the task.

   Nonsense.  Read "Compiler Technology for Future Microprocessors" by W.W. Hwu
   et al in December 1995 Proceedings of the IEEE.  With compiler techniques
   for a predicated instruction set, enough instruction level parallelism can be
   extracted from SPEC92 programs and Unix utilies like lex and grep to operate
   an eight issue wide processor at an average of roughly 5 times the performance
   of a single pipe scalar CPU at the same frequency.

> 
> Supercomputers, as I recall, went to multiprocessors somewhere
> around 500mips (+ or - 300 mips) in the early 80's. Apparently, it was
> the path of least cost to higher performance.

   They had to.  With the low level of integration possible with the technology
   used to build these beasts they couldn't increase the clock rate much due to
   interchip communications delay and they couldn't really go to very wide issue
   due to economic restrictions on gate count.

   It is the modern 0.25 um or smaller CMOS processes permitting tens of millions
   of transistors at clock rates approaching 1 GHz that makes an EPIC style wide
   issue CPU economically and technically feasible to consider.

> 
> The primary compute intensive application that most consumers will care
> about is graphics, e.g. 3D animation, MPEG decoding, etc., and there is
> plenty of parallelism in graphics to enable effective use of the multiple
> processors.

   Most of the "media processors" designed for this kind of stuff are built
   as VLIW machines to avoid the programming and cost problems of MP systems.
   Why do you think TI has stopped development of MP-style 'C80 DSP family in
   favour of 'C6X VLIW DSP family?

[snip]

> Stan Lass    http://www.netins.net/showcase/stanlass
> 
> (515) 275-2135

All opinions strictly my own.

