> > > Todd
> >
> > DE435 is only 10Mbps. If you want a 10/100Mbps card, you need a DE500
> > (there are 3 or 4 variants of this, so make sure you get the current
> > one - the most recent I've seen referred-to is the DE500-BA). Certainly
> > avoid the -XA version, it has problems under heavy network or system
> > load.
> > Regards,
> > John Hill
> > --
> > 

> Are the problems with the DE500-XA documented? I just installed the XA
> version in my 2100A in part because of the ton of traffic from users
> pulling data from Sybase with Buisness Objects. We are planning to
> switch
> over from the DE435 tomorrow morning (Saturday). DEC did not mention
> any problems with this card, only that the AA version was not supported
> in
> VMS 6.2 I was under the impression that the limitation with the XA was
> that
> it was not auto sensing, which for us is fine because we want it to stay
> in 100 Mbs full duplex mode. I would be interested in any information
> you
> have on this issue. Thanks!
> 
> Jim McKinney
> System Programmer
> Group Health Northwest
> jkmckinn@ghnw.ghc.org
Hi,
  The problems are documented in that they come up on the news every
few months (I posted a request for information myself a few weeks ago).
My experiences with the DE500-XA are on a AlphaServer 1000A 4/266
running DIGITAL UNIX (V3.2G and V4.0B), so I cannot comment on its use
under OpenVMS. I'd thought the -XA had been superceded by first the
-AA and then the -BA. 
  What I see is that occasionally (once every few weeks) the network
connection is lost for ~30 seconds and then recovered. It seems to be
related to heavy network traffic. It only (in my experience) occurs
at 100Mbps (we ran the card at 10Mbps for several months without any
problem). On my D.U. system there is a message at the same time:

transmit FIFO underflow: threshold raised to: 256 bytes

which lends weight to the idea that the -XA doesn't have the on-board
resources to handle sustained 100Mbps traffic.
Regards,
John Hill
