>   extracted from SPEC92 programs and Unix utilies like lex and grep to 
operate
>   an eight issue wide processor at an average of roughly 5 times the 
performance
>   of a single pipe scalar CPU at the same frequency.
>
If the eight issue wide processor had eight pipes worth of logic, then 
the average utilization per pipe would be ~62.5%. A standalone single 
pipe would have 100% utilization (by definition). The question then, 
I think, is can multiple processors beat the ~62.5% utilization of pipes. 
With coming graphics programs requiring thousands of mips, I'd estimate 
the utilization of multiple processors to be over 90%.

More generally, each time you increase the issue rate by one, you must 
add logic to support the increased issue rate, and the overall average 
utilization of the logic falls off. Similarly, each time you add a 
processor in a multiple processor system, the overall average utilization 
falls off. However, it's easier to find exploitable parallelism in a 
program than in a single thread, and so the fall off should be less rapid 
in a multiple processor system, given suitable applications. For this 
reason, I think that processors beyond a few instructions issued per 
clock will be less than optimum.

>> 
>> Supercomputers, as I recall, went to multiprocessors somewhere
>> around 500mips (+ or - 300 mips) in the early 80's. Apparently, it was
>> the path of least cost to higher performance.
>
>   They had to.  With the low level of integration possible with the 
technology
>   used to build these beasts they couldn't increase the clock rate much 
due to
>   interchip communications delay and they couldn't really go to very wide 
issue
>   due to economic restrictions on gate count.
>
>   It is the modern 0.25 um or smaller CMOS processes permitting tens of 
millions
>   of transistors at clock rates approaching 1 GHz that makes an EPIC style 
wide
>   issue CPU economically and technically feasible to consider.

As CMOS processes shrink, wiring delay is becoming a larger part of the 
clock delay. This tends to reduce the performance of wide issue processors. 

>> 
>> The primary compute intensive application that most consumers will care
>> about is graphics, e.g. 3D animation, MPEG decoding, etc., and there is
>> plenty of parallelism in graphics to enable effective use of the multiple
>> processors.
>
>   Most of the "media processors" designed for this kind of stuff are built
>   as VLIW machines to avoid the programming and cost problems of MP 
systems.
>   Why do you think TI has stopped development of MP-style 'C80 DSP family 
in
>   favour of 'C6X VLIW DSP family?
>
No doubt the MP-style didn't fit their customers' applications very well. 
It seems that a certain threshold must be crossed before multiple 
processors are the better choice. Supercomputers crossed that threshold 
in the early 80's. I surmise that microprocessors will soon cross it.

As the amount of logic that can be put on a chip increases, it's only a 
matter of time before multiple processors become more viable than a 
really wide issue single processor. And then it's a matter of optimizing 
the issue width versus productivity within the single processors used in 
a multiple processor system. 

Again, I'm surmising that there will be increased exploitable parallelism 
in the computationally demanding and economically interesting problems of 
the future.

>Paul W. DeMone                 

