Bryan Jensen wrote:
> 
> I think we underestimate the resources required to make an OS viable.
> Everybody seems to be doing poorly.

I think one could make a good case that although the scope of operating
systems has grown considerably between (say) VMS and NT, factors such as

	the productivity of modern languages and tools

	the fact that everything you have to do is very well understood
	and can basically be copied out of textbooks

	the availability of ever-increasing numbers of skilled people over
	the Internet

make it actually easier than it has ever been to make a "state of the
art" system.

> Linux of course was created by a large group of people.  But there's
> no quality control.  It's been said that Linux is not very robust.

Quality and robustness are not by and large big issues with Linux.  More
of a problem has been the lack of fancy GUI utilities and admin tools,
but that is steadily coming into place now (Red Hat, KDE), and on
current trends Linux may actually catch up with NT 4 before NT 5 really
settles down.  I'm sure the same could be done for VMS without too much
effort.

The trouble, of course, is that to "make an OS viable" also means
endowing it with enough actual applications, and that seems to be a
great deal more difficult than getting the OS itself working well.  In
particular there are very few examples of really polished desktop apps
which have not come out of well-staffed commercial shops, and they are
not going to get ported to a new OS unless and until it becomes
viable---catch 22.



