Organization: AOL http://www.aol.com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


What will Compaq do with Alpha?  To me this is the big question.  I propose two
oppose scenarios:

1. Dump Alpha:

Compaq has been very good at using, not pioneering technology.  They have done
this by simply selling the dominant technology, i.e. Wintel.   The do an
exellent job of efficiently churning out mainstream product. 

Why try to play in the micro market when it costs more every year, and when
Intel has the whole market locked up? Alpha has miniscule market share.   Dump
the expensive alpha development and just go with the (Wintel) flow.  Plus Intel
is supposed to soon conquer the last remaining market, the high end, with
Merced.


2. Push Alpha:

Alpha is technically superior, it lacks to things:  market acceptance and
marketign savvy.

Compaq is famous and has a large share.  If they say Alpha is standard, people
will believe them. Presto, self-fullfilling prophecy, Alpha *is* standard.  
Also, Compaq is very good at rapidly developing inexpensive, functional boxes. 
Alpha becomes cheap, plentiful, and accepted.

The 21264 will push the Alpha speed advantage from 2.5X to 5X.  NT takes over
the desktop, pushing more and more Unix boxes out.  If you want a truly fast
box, and you want NT, you buy Alpha.  Sun, HP, SG, and IBM can't get NT ported
to their hardware.  Compaq finally gets to enjoy the top of the computer food
chain, taking some market from Intel.


*****

What will happen?   Option 1 is much lower risk, but option 2 has much higher
potential rewards.  I think the decision also depends on timing, specifically
how long the alpha performance window is open:

1. When will the 21264 ship?!  (i've been waiting foreve, it seems)
2. When will the Merced ship (could be late)

My uninformed ranting, Any comments?   

Andrew



