Rob Young wrote:

>         So, anyone still believe Alpha is dead after today?
>
> http://biz.yahoo.com/prnews/980128/ca_microso_1.html
>
> "Initial Digital [Wildfire] systems will use 32, 64 or more Alpha processors and
> are expected to debut in early 1999. Microsoft and Digital will contribute
> engineering and management resources for this joint development effort out of
> company facilities in Washington and New England."
>
>         As the PR relates, "the first 64-bit systems to ship" will be
>         shipping on Alpha.  Also:
>
> "The companies will cooperate on a single programming model and a single API for
> 64-bit Windows NT running on Intel and Alpha systems. This cooperation will
> provide source code compatibility between Intel and Alpha systems for Windows
> NT application developers."
>
>         You create for both platforms by default.  May the best architecture
>         win.  Looks like 64-bit NT arrives on Alpha first.  10 months or
>         more before the first Merced boxes.
>
>                                 Rob

64 bit NT is to my understanding based on NT 5. If this is correct then I
am afraid that for the market that Wildfire is supposed to address there
is not going to be a 10 month Alpha advantage over Merced.

The reasons are simple, no one is recommending that people use NT 5.0
for anything mission critical until it has been available for some time and
Microsoft have fixed the Bugs. Gartner are recommending a 12 month
wait after the first NT 5 shipments for first business critical NT 5
implementations. Given the bad NT 5 beta test reports, the massive
ammounts of new code in NT 5 and Microsofts pitifull record with NT 4 Gartner
are probably being optimistic.

In other words, by the time the pottential customers for NT/Wildfire
systems get arround to doing deployments of NT 5 Wildfire systems
Merced based systems will be available. In all probability a few
brave NT/Alpha customers will have done all of the testing which
will benefit the NT/Merced systems.


This has nothing to do with Alpha and everything to do with Microsoft
and NT.

It does of course raise the question about the wisdom of Digitals NT
strategy.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK






