>
>The above configuration is definitely legal and bootable via DUP AFTER
>the system has first been booted (let us say on some other device such
>as an RL02 or a RX02).

True.

>In summary, setting up a device with a MSCP mapping table that has
>entries where the logical and the physical units are different (but
>at least the partition numbers are zero) will allow DUP to boot
>the device, but DEC BOOT ROMs (at least from my understanding and
>very limited testing to verify that understanding) can't boot an
>MSCP device when the PHYSICAL  and  the  LOGICAL device mapping
>is NOT  IDENTICAL  UNLESS the BOOT block has been set up in a
>manner which is able to accommodate this situation being different.

This agrees with the 5.6 release notes (page 3-3):

  For an MSCP device to be hard-bootable, the MSCP unit number for 
  that device must be the same as the RT-11 unit number.  Further,
  only partition 0 is hardware bootable.  All partitions are software
  bootable.

As you and Tim have both pointed out, by cleverly modifying the boot
block or by having bootable media present in multiple drives you can
get around this restriction.  Understandably, the "restrictions"
in the release notes don't tell you that a particular method won't work,
it's just warning you that it's not supported.

I, though, am opposed to such "work-arounds" because they don't
make anything work more clearly.  For example, suppose I've mapped
the hard drives (MSCP units 0 and 1) to DU units 2 and 3, then I map
the floppy drives (MSCP units 2 and 3) to DU units 0 and 1.  Then
I SYSGEN a new system on the hard drives and boot it.  The new "virgin"
DU driver will have default mappings.  If I don't carefully take this
into account, and I try to INIT a floppy by saying INIT DU0: (which
usually worked on my floppy because of my custom mapping), I'll
have INITed my hard drive.  Oops!

In summary: DU -> MSCP mapping is a powerful feature of RT-11.
It's plenty powerful enough to shoot yourself in the foot with it.
(And I think most of us have, at some point!)  If I want the same
name for my floppy drives on all systems, I use logical assignments.

Tim.



