Philip Brown (phil.s1@bolthole.com.no-bots) wrote:

: >> It's not the cost of running the OS itself, it's the cost of running
: >> third party application software that pushes NT.  NT licenses are often
: >> *much* cheaper than Unix licenses for the same product.  For sites with
: >> hundreds or thousands of users relying on a number of third party
: >> applications, this is a major issue that actually dictates the choice
: >> of NT.

One of the mitigating factors is that Unix
applications are much more likely to come with network
licensing schemes that allow one to buy licenses for
the number of copies actually in use, rather than the
number of people who might at some point use the
application.  If you have a thousand users but not
more than, say, 150, use the application at any given
time, Unix vendors generally let you get 150 licenses
while PC vendors require 1000.  For large sites, this
is indeed a major issue; but it does not necessarily
dictate the choice of OS either way.

	mike

