In article <6ao76r$7dp@gap.cco.caltech.edu>,
   mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu writes (about compaq):
>The only thing that they're really missing is a few key pieces of software.
>Better business in the long run to subsidize the development of a few key
>pieces of desktop and small server software to run on Compaq's own OS's
>than to cede the high end OS market to Microsoft 5-7 years from now.

I think we underestimate the resources required to make an OS viable.
Everybody seems to be doing poorly.

The guy at the next desk tells me that BSDI is the best OS there is
(BSDI is a tiny little company that produces yet another non-compatible
UNIX variant).  I pointed to the BSDI manual where it says that shared
libraries are mapped into addresses fixed when the library is built
(thus making them useless for 3rd party vendors).  There is just too
much that goes into a modern system for a small group to produce,
they have to cut corners somewhere, even, as with BSDI, at the most
basic level.

Linux of course was created by a large group of people.  But there's
no quality control.  It's been said that Linux is not very robust.

VMS, while the OS itself is quite functional and robust, lacks a
tremendous amount of add-on software when compared to most other
OS's.  I'd guess Digital just plain doesn't have the manpower to
make all the layered products work.

I got in a new AlphaStation 500/500 last week.  I brought it up with
the factory installed software (no additional software and no network
connections).  I logged into SYSTEM, selected cycling of all the screen 
savers, then locked the screen.  Within a week, the window manager
access violated and died.  That's better than my VMS 6.2 systems, 
some of which crash multiple times a day.  I look forward to upgrading 
to the latest software if it results in only weekly crashes.  I don't 
expect perfection.

WordPerfect for VMS lags years behind other platforms.  It's like there
is no such thing as portable software.

Our MIS department is currently switching from VMS to Solaris so they
can run current versions of Oracle.  I hear Oracle for VMS lags about
a year and a half behind other platforms.  I hope nobody really plans
to market VMS as a backoffice server.  Why pay more for a system that
only runs outdated software?

MIS is also moving applications from VMS to Windows NT.  This is going
very poorly.  Seems very foolish to me.  MicroSloth has the manpower,
but they lack experience in a multi-vendor environment.



