Around here the situation has been confusing for the past few years.
Before that, Digital sold servers and workstations directly to our
university. Once in a while we got a visit from a Digital salesman,
armed with catalogues, etc...

Then one day someone at Digital decided that some things (workstations,
maybe small servers also) would be sold via VARs. Our usual vendor
appeared accompanied by someone from a VAR, who was supposed to be
our contact from then on.

I suspected that that arrangement didn't work very well, since what
has been happening since is that we talk with the Digital vendor, but
then we buy from a VAR (and the VAR has changed once or twice). And
when we have problems with the VAR (like waiting months for a fast
ethernet board) we solve them by phoning the Digital salesman.

The idea I got (seeing the situation from the outside) is that someone
invented rules/strategies which looked good on paper (or maybe in the
situation on other countries), but which were not realistic. And the
people in contact with the costumers recognised that and found ways
to keep a direct contact.

BTW (and the true intention of this post), that Digital salesman has
been here today (it might have been a coincidence, but I suspect he is
making the rounds of their customers to appease fears). Some things he
said:

- these are good news for Alpha, since one of the things Compaq is
buying is the Alpha tecnology. So they will want to use it.

- Digital and Compaq are a good match since, apart from Digital's PC
business (which was not profitable), there is very little overlap.

- Compaq's support here in Portugal is already being done by Digital
people.

