    The funny thing is that I hope you are correct and that my prediction doesn't
    come to pass.  My heart wishes that Alpha thrives and there is competition in
    high end CPUs to keep Intel honest on price and pace of innovation.  I also wish
    Alpha would succeed much better than it has as I have been a "RISC bigot" since
    1982 and enjoyed seeing lumbering CISC ISAs succumb one by one with the pivotal
    exception of x86.

    However, my cold, dettached prediction is based upon keen observation of the CPU
    marketplace since before the RISC era.  I have seen the various campaigns waged
    by RISC vendors to overthrow the x86 in the desktop.  The ACE consortium rallied
    around the MIPS R4000 to take on Intel's vaporous promise of the 586 (Pentium).
    The merits of the R4000 couldn't overcome 1) ACE internal disunity, 2) Intel's
    leverage over PC vendors (the sole sourced 486 was on allocation), and 3) Intel's
    outrageously exaggerated claims of performance and schedule for the 586 ("faster
    than R4000, available shortly after R4000).  Any momentum MIPS had in the NT market
    was lost when MIPS Technologies financially stumbled from problems with the bipolar
    R6000 and was acquired by SGI (which staunchly opposed NT until their recent deathbed
    conversion ;-).  At one point Sun made noise about taking on PCs with low cost SPARC
    desktops with a GUI based OS.  These so-called SPARCintoshes never did materialize.
    IBM, Motorola, and Apple went after Wintel with the PowerPC with huge fanfare and hype.
    The good start the PPC guys had rapidly degenerateded into a stalemate situation
    (which Wintel wins by default) by Apple's ridiculously high margins, lack of clones,
    and the failure of PPC to stay ahead of x86 as Intel pushed Pentium into 0.6 um and
    later 0.35 um processes and cranked up the clock rate.  DEC looked like it really had
    a chance with the 21164 processor until Intel shocked the marketplace with the 0.35 um,
    200 MHz P6 a good year ahead of its roadmap schedule and suprisingly fast.   The P6
    took the integer performance lead for only a fleeting instant and it was still far
    behind on FP but the damage to the RISC campaign against the x86 for the hearts and
    minds of ISVs and desktop computer builders was long lasting and profound.  The 21164PC
    gives Alpha access to new price points but there is no compelling reason for most
    desktop users to buy it considering the aggressive Pentium II roadmap.

    Ironically Merced is basically Intel's admission that the RISC approach was correct
    after all and x86 baggage cannot be propped up indefinitely, especially when pushing
    into the 64 bit realm.   The participation of HP along with inclusion of a decade's
    worth of advancements in computer architecture and compiler technology virtually
    ensures that IA-64 will be technologically successful and scalable (unlike the iAPX432
    and i860 for example).  More importantly, on the business side Intel takes in the
    lion's share of uP revenue and profit that is needed to sustain the ever increasing
    costs of high end CPU design and advanced logic process semi development while the
    Wintel business model appears to be irrestible.  When a company like HP says it cannot
    afford to keep developing competitive CPUs and their associated fabs you have to sit up
    and pay at least a bit of attention.

    Under the circumstances of the DEC-Compaq deal, it is completely expected that officials
    of both companies will swear up and down that Alpha is of strategic importance and there
    will be continued funding of new development yada yada yada.  It makes no sense at all
    to undermine one of the product lines of your new acquisition regardless of your true
    intentions.

> 
>         Given that, and the fact that in a press release on the 26th Samsung
>         revealed 21264 will be at 1 GHz and 100 SpecInt95 (no time frame
