    How can this be?  Despite its x86 mode, the statically scheduled Merced is still likely a
    simpler device than the complex 21264 so it potentially could have a smaller die size in
    the same 0.18 um process.   Meanwhile Merced will in all probability enjoy a much higher
    production rate and lifetime unit volume.

> 
>         Merced certainly is no danger to Alpha centric customers and no
>         threat to folks that need high-performance.   I really don't see
>         the Merced sex appeal.  Given that Alpha <-> Intel int95 differential
>         today is in the 60% neighborhood and all indications will grow to
>         200-300% difference.

    Unlike virtually every other RISC vendor, DEC has managed to keep its Alpha CPUs ahead
    of Intel x86 integer performance by between 1.5 to 2.0 X.   But by transitioning to
    IA-64, Intel is able to finally throw off the chains of x86 baggage, at least for native
    code.  To say that Alpha will be able to maintain, let alone extend, its performance
    advantage over high end Intel CPUs seems unreasonable.   The architectural and compiler
    advantages that Merced enjoys over classical RISC ISAs will really start to shine as high
    end implementations reach 6 issue width and head towards 12+ issue width.

> 
>         OpenVMS Technologies?  Hummm.  Galaxies.  Yes.  No one touches
>         Galaxies in performance.
> 
>         By the way, you are a great CPU historian.  Please do us a favor
>         and flesh out this statement:
> 
> > Compaq never adopted RISC chips despite consistent two to
> >   one or more performance advantages over x86 CPUs.
> 
>         Follow-up multi-part question regarding that statement:
> 
>                     When did this performance advantage exist, who had it,
>                     and how long did it last?

    Here is a rough comparison over time of x86 versus RISC.  Remember that
    for most of the timeline shown here RISC was *much* more expensive than
    x86 due to the small volumes and special system level requirements.


             Intel CPU           RISC  CPU           Approximate Integer
                                                     performance advantage

               386               MIPS R2000             1.5 - 2 x
               486               MIPS R3000A            1.5 x
               486DX2            MIPS R4000             1.5 x
               Pentium P5        MIPS R4400             1.5 x
               Pentium P54       Alpha 21064A           1.25 x
               Pentium P54s      Alpha 21164            2 x
               P Pro P6          Alpha 21164A           1.25 - 2 x

speculate:     Deschutes P6      Alpha 21264            2 - 2.5 x
               Merced P7         Alpha 21264A              ?
               Merced-II P7      Alpha 21364 ?             ?

    It is not worth trying to predict Alpha vs Merced at this point because of the
    architectural unknowns, Intel predilection for springing nasty suprises, and most
    importantly, IA-64 compiler maturity.  Remember that RISC compilers continued to
    advance significantly (10-20% integer performance bump on major new releases) five
    years or more after the ISA was in general use.  IA-64 is even more dependent on
    compiler quality than RISC so the performance of Merced will strongly depend on how
    much prep work using architectural simulators Intel and HP put into compilers ahead
    of silicon availability. 

> 
>         The reason I ask is because it appears that Digital will have a
>         2-3 times performance advantage over Merced that should from all
>         indications last AT LEAST 4 years.  By the way, don't muddle
>         the 21364 . . . you been reading Linley Gwennap's stuff?  The
>         21364 will happen, why pass up a 150 SpecInt95 part?  Besides,
>         Nova says it will happen.... that makes it true doesn't it?

    I don't see any technical reasons to believe such grandiose predictions of
    Alpha superiority over Merced/IA-64.  The clock rate crank becomes ever so
    harder to turn as 1 GHz is crossed.  Also, the potentially simpler dispatch
    logic of Merced CPUs due to its static scheduling approach will likely reduce
    or even eliminate the clock rate advantages Alpha has historically enjoyed.
    Similarly, attempts to build very wide issue machines will likely play to IA-64's
    strengths as the dependency checking logic and out-of-order support mechanisms
    needed for dynamically scheduled CPUs like the 21264 experience exponential
    complexity increases with issue width.

    Regardless of the technical issues of Merced vs Alpha is the business aspect.
    Each new generation of CPU is ever more expensive to conceptualize, design,
    implement, and verify.  Is the Alpha development process so "lean and mean"
    that it can continue on the technology treadmill given its rather mediocre
    commercial success so far?


