References: <34CC8D06.3359@e-farm.com> <34CD1D6D.AD958F7D@primary.net> <34CD35C9.572@videotron.ca> <borsom-2601982116540001@bos-ma11-17.ix.netcom.com> <34CDF124.2A74@bigfoot.com> <34CD79ED.4ADB@videotron.ca>
Organization: MindSpring Enterprises
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms


My 2 cents:
VMS has been over priced for years.  DEC attitude has been that VMS is such
a high performance OS that there is no reason to provide VMS at a
reasonable price.  However, this attitude has
only led VMS into obscurity, since most developers can not afford a
VMS/Alpha with the necessary licenses and compilers or will make due with
inferior products.   

VMS success is its own downfall.  VMS is not scalable in the sense that it
pretty much runs the same whether VMS is on a small box or large.  I feel
DEC really controlled this on the VAXes
but not really on the AXPs.  I think DEC knows this, so they keep the low
end very expensive.  I know of companies that still run their business off
of small workstation very sucessfully.

Don't get me wrong, I make my living off VMS, but I am very glad to see
someone else ultimately in charge of VMS.  I have the feeling that Compaq
with dump VMS or make the prices very
competitive, I certainly hope for the latter.



