 I feel Josh's reply here is inaccurate.  I try not
to get involved in philosophical debates, but I couldn't resist posting
my feelings about this:

Sun NC's are based on JavaOS and JavaVM, and as you know, the Java
language itself cross-platform compatible.  Java is a object oriented
language and and adheres to the principle, "write once, run anywhere".

Java is young and it's moving along just fine.  Network Computers are
also relatively new technologies and are also advancing nicely.  If you
examine some software companies, you will find there are Java ports in
progress everywhere.

Sometimes trade publications, etc., don't consider that technologies
evolve.  The possibilities with Java and NC's are almost endless - who
says from all this there might finally be a OS that competes with the
Microsoft sleech?  What about UNIX innovations?  We're moving into the
64-bit zone rapidly and the next UNIX "standard" (I hope it's tighter
this time) is in the works (UNIX SysVII?).  What's going on with the HP
UNIX-3D project, or whatever?

NC's will be given away free and Java software will become widely
available and accepted, I believe.  NC's are not XTerminals with
different names either.

Yet another opinion...

