In article <6anm5b$8ka$1@csnews.cs.colorado.edu>, fedrick@dendrite.cs.colorado.edu (Kelvin Fedrick) writes:
>>Phillip Helbig wrote:
>>
>>> X-windows is not the same thing as a GUI.  X-windows is just a cheaper
>>> way of having several VTs in your room.  A GUI means clicking 5 times
>>> and navigating through menus to get the same effect---with much more
>>> overhead---of typing a few keystrokes.
>
>I have used many X-window GUI's that have nothing to do with providing
>VTs, and which fit your description of a GUI. I have also written a couple
>of my own. Now if you are saying that X-windows itself is not a GUI, you
>are correct - it's the set of programs and libraries used to produce and
>use GUI interfaces. But if you are saying that it has nothing more to offer
>than providing VT's, then you clearly are not familiar with X-windows.

While it is true that you can do a lot more with Xwindows than just run 
terminal emulators, at least in my experience that is still their primary 
use.  Around here a typical X11 user has 3 or 4 terminal windows open at
any one time, and usually one to at most three graphical programs running.
(Not counting fluff like Xclock or Xeyes.)  Interestingly, even when the
nominally more "integrated" and Windows-like CDE is installed, they STILL
do things pretty much the same way, usually covering up all of that pretty,
but largely unused, desktop.  I tend to agree with the first poster - if
you know what you're doing, and do the same tasks frequently, the 
command line free GUI approach of Windows or the Mac is simply less
efficient than typing - especially if you have command line recall and
cut and paste to cut down on typos (cut and paste between 
windows is especially valuable).  Conversely, if you do a task very
infrequently, so that you don't recall the names of the commands, let alone
their syntax, the GUI will usually be faster.  Relevant to that point, it
would be very nice if OpenVMS were enhanced/standardized so that 

 $ command/help

triggered the equivalent of 

 $ help command

Yeah, I know that it is the exact same number of keystrokes for the
commands which are installed in DCLTABLES, but for other software it often 
is not, ie

 $ help @unziphelp unzip

The one caveat to the "frequent tasks, command line is best" observation is
that younger users, who cut their teeth on GUIS, seem to develop a
constitutive inability to use typed commands.  I'd say that most of these
folks never learn the keystroke shortcuts in programs like Microsoft Word
either, even though they use them all the time. 

And as long as we're on the subject of X11, I'm shipping the OpenVMS port
of Xforms 0.88 back to the author today, so it should be on his web site
soon at:

  http://bloch.phys.uwm.edu/xforms

Xforms is a nice GUI toolkit based on Xlib, complete with an interactive
interface design program.  The shared libraries produced by this version
are upwardly compatible from the 0.86 OpenVMS version.

