Pierre Baron  <pbaron@clear.net.nz> wrote:
>
>This mainly centres around what sort of (free) UNIX operating system I
>can get to run on this.  The board with the CPU mounted on it has the
>part number of M7606 EF stamped on it.

Your best bet is NetBSD if you like UNIX, or if you like VMS and are a
member of DECUS, you can get a free hobbyist VMS license and run VMS 5.5-2. 
The Dallas DECUS user group sells media on CD for $30 with a lot of 
goodies on as well. 
If you're more adventurous, you can run the older Berkeley UNIXes, 
at gatekeeper.dec.com via anonymous ftp you can access /pub/decinfo/BSD
and in the net1 and net2 directories there are two old releases of 4.3BSD. 
There's other goodies in there too, including a lot of documentation
from the Good Ol Days. 

>Also fitted is a couple of memory looking boards with the number NS630
>(not MS630), a CMD SCSI controller and an M7504 adapter.

Good luck with the CMD! theyre not that easy to find!
As for the M7504, thats an ehternet adapter. Theyre not that tough
to find. 

>
>The system has no tape or floppy drive, only a full height 5.25" 760mb
>SCSI drive with nothing on it.

No, no, i will bet you a VAX and a donut that that is defintiely NOT
a scsi drive!
 It is an SMD drive, which has a deceptively familiar looking 50 pin
connector but be assured, it is NOT SCSI! hehehe

>
>Armed with this can anyone advise me on my options.

Find an SMD controller and toss it in. They are or were a rather common
sight since many people no longer had SMD drives when they got their
hands on castawar VAXen, mainly because lots of SMD drives were simply
too big to move. Earlier SMD drives (for examle the RA8x series) weighed
up to several hundred pounds. But the controllers are pretty easy to 
come by.

good luck with it, theyre great little (well little is a relative term)
machines!


happy hacking,
isildur



