Pierre Baron  <pbaron@clear.net.nz> wrote:
>
>This mainly centres around what sort of (free) UNIX operating system I
>can get to run on this.  The board with the CPU mounted on it has the
>part number of M7606 EF stamped on it.
>
>Also fitted is a couple of memory looking boards with the number NS630
>(not MS630), a CMD SCSI controller and an M7504 adapter.

Whoops! (did I just lose a VAX and a donut?) you already _have_ the CMD 
in there?
 you are VERY lucky! 
Well, in that case, the chance is that the drive int here really is scsi. 
*muttering about how someone could happen on a uVAX that already had 
a qbus SCSI board in it!* very very lucky man you are!

well, 
your options look pretty darn good! 
The CMD is a great board, like the Emulex UC07/8 SCSI baords, it emulates 
MSCP transparently and the system does not know or care that there are SCSI
devices attached to it. 
If the system was working before you got it, and everythign is still in 
place, then things should probably work. IIRC, the only difference between a
GPX and a normal uVAX II was the graphics boards. 
Unless you have a DEC monitor of older vinatge (ergo one of the 1024x864
ones, VR290, VR260, etc) you will not be able too see anythign on the console.
If thats the case you might want to remove the graphics board(s) and
boot the system with the console on the console panel/connector on the
bulkhead in the rear. You will need a nonstandard cable for this, but the
signals are normal serial and a terminal or other machine running kermit
or something will work fine. 

As for an OS, I would reocmmend NetBSD. 

happy hacking,
isildur



