From: "Randy Park" <rjpark.nospam@halcyon.com.nospam>
Subject: Digital's 3 big mistakes
Date: 1998/01/29
Message-ID: <01bd2cfc$6ed0cb80$341337a6@rjp>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 320376610
NNTP-Posting-Date: Thu, 29 Jan 1998 16:22:52 EST
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


My career and business has been tied to Digital's coattails for the
past 20 years.  In reflection I see that Digital has made three
strategic mistakes.


1.  Not believing that people wanted a computer on their desk.

Back in the late 70's Digital practically owned 16 bit computing.
Instead of embracing the personal computer revolution, DEC ignored
it.  They had the ability to produce and promote desktop systems
but Ken Olsen didn't believe in them.

In late 1979 or early 1980, they introduced the PDT-130 and PDT-150
desktop computers.  They incorported an LSI-11 computer running the
RT-11/CTS-300 operating system and were housed in a VT100 with 
8 inch external floppy disks for the PDT-150.  The 130 used TU-58 
tapes.  These systems predated the ill-fated VT180 (Robin).

Think what the computing world would be like today if DEC had
believed that desktop computing was the logical extension to
minicomputer revolution of the 1970s.  Where would Intel and
Microsoft be today?  This decision meant that DEC stopped being
an innovator that created new markets.  This leads us to the
second blunder.

2.  Adopting a "Defend the Franchise" business model.

During DEC's period of greatest success, they decided they wanted
all of the "DEC related" business.  One of the big reasons PDP-11s
were so sucessful was that their bus structures, the Unibus and the
Q-bus, were open.  Anyone could attach hardware to them.

With the advent of the BI-bus, DEC decided they knew best how their
computers were to be used.  If you wanted to attach non-DEC hardware
to their BI-bus, you had to get permission.  If you didn't get
permission, you got sued.

At about the same time they decided they didn't need an OEM sales
channel.  They wanted to have all the sales themselves.  Hundreds of
companies that built their businesses selling DEC based solutions
went elsewhere.

This business model worked for several years in the mid 1980s as DEC 
absorbed sales that many of their value added resellers would have 
made.  By the late '80s the growth from devouring their reseller base
was over.  Guess what kind of systems those resellers were now selling?
Can you say Unix?

The history of business is littered with companies that have adopted
the "Defend the Franchise" model.  When a business whose history is
defining and building new markets ceases doing this, their days
become numbered.  The failure to recognize this leads us to the third
major blunder.

3.  Failure to replace the Board of Directors.

Digital's board for years has been primarily populated by old men
that had been high executives at other Forture 500 companies.  None
of them were leaders of businesses that created new industries.  My
personal impression of the board was that it primarily an honorary
appointment.  Digital's board may have worked well for an old line
manufacturing company, like Ford, but it was totally wrong for the
company that created the minicomputer industry.  They needed members
that had an entrepreneureal spirit.



