In article <34CF0E50.275A5CD3@uhrad.com>, Gary Muswick <muswick@uhrad.com> writes:
>  VMS on Intel would lower profit margin  even more - except now
>deal with thousands of different types of hardware out there and IRQ's etc.

On that front I'm a bit more optimistic. Digital would blow off any idea
concerning hardware without a huge gross margin.  Compaq has been making a
lot more money, for a long time, selling a lot of systems with tiny
margins.  While for Digital a gross margin of 30% was too small, for Compaq
that may look lucrative indeed.  Moreover, if Compaq sells inexpensive
OpenVMS or DU machines they pay exactly zero to Microsoft and zero to Intel, which
cuts their costs on making a "pure Compaq" PC versus a regular PC.  They
can amortize the costs of those OS's because come what may, they still need 
to make them run on the big iron; there are only incremental costs to
make them run on smaller machines.  Compaq management has been quoted as
saying their goal was to become number two in the industry, but you can be
pretty sure that they'd much rather be number one.  One way to do it is to
take business away from both Intel and Microsoft, which they are now very
much in a position to do, using their own Alpha chips and their own OS's
(either OpenVMS or DU). 

They just spent 9 Billion dollars to buy one big service company, and a
lot of very expensive technology.  It may be that they will now be penny wise
and pound foolish and not immediately invest enough in one or both OS's
to give WNT some competition, but I don't expect that.  More likely EP is
bright enough to realize that if he wants to keep that nice juicy high end
for any length of time, he'd darn well better give BG a run for the money
in small servers and probably right down to the desktop.  Plus Compaq can 
pretty much have its cake and eat it too.  If Microsoft kills NT on alpha
now it's not going to be the fatal event it would have been for Digital
alone.  Never mind the immediate, and probably ultimately successful
antitrust actions, they can still sell their 20 billion in Intel based
systems, so the loss of maybe a billion a years in Alpha/WNT sales isn't
going to be instantly fatal. Heck, it might not even be all that painful
for Compaq as a company - in the extreme they could just fire everybody who
works on WNT/Alpha (sorry guys, that's what happens when you let a guy
like RP lead you down into the bowels of hell) and so cut their losses to
not very much.  Or they could divert some of those folks to WNT/ Intel
(which Microsoft cannot take away from them) and the rest to the Compaq's
OS's, providing a lot of resources with which to tune them up for the mass
market - and then shove them right down Microsoft's throat. 

The only thing that they're really missing is a few key pieces of software.
Better business in the long run to subsidize the development of a few key
pieces of desktop and small server software to run on Compaq's own OS's
than to cede the high end OS market to Microsoft 5-7 years from now.  Get
the ball rolling, see what happens. Maybe even Oracle would drop its prices
(ok, maybe not) if it saw the opportunity to slow Microsoft's spread into
its database markets.  It isn't the 25000 applications that make or break a
platform, it's the 5 or 10 without which nobody can justify a purchase.  So
if Compaq could get an office suite (Corel, probably), a less expensive
database, a graphics program or two, Autocad, and some animation software
(like lightwave) in place, and competitively priced for one or both of its
OS's, it would make for very interesting times indeed. 

Regards,

