re: Alpha having undeniable speed advantage over first generation IA64
    but press still dismisses Alpha as a "dead".

Ok, lets assume that when IA64 becomes available, it will be
rated at 40 Tpc while Alpha will, at that time be at 100.

Has it ever occured to you that Intel may purposefully produce an IA64
which is slower than it can ? IA64 need be just fast enough to push
folks over the hurdle to convert from 8086 to IA64.

Once folks have made the jump and replaced their fleet of PCs spending
lots of money, then Intel will obsolete the first Ia64s by producing 
faster ones, causing folks to again spend money to replace their PCs.
This slower progress allows software to follow and eat up any speed
advantages obtained.

If Intel were to produce a truly fast chip right away, the software
would be so blazing fast that folks would not see the need to upgrade
for a long time, until the software has caught up and become so bloated
that upgrades are necessary. And during that lull where everyone is
happy, Intel and PC makers would be hurting big time with low sales.

When you target Alpha at a niche market which can instantly eat away any
CPU cycle it can get its hands at (eg: Tinanic using Alpha as batch
rendering machines), you're talking about an insatiable apetite for
horsepower and hence, it doesn't hurt to make giant leaps in performance
because folks will upgrade as soon as you offer more power.

But for home and business, providing too giant a leap can cause too much
peaks and lows in sales figures.



