In article <34CF4F4E.F38CF8DC@qei-trading.com>, Allen Quinn <allen_quinn@qei-trading.com> writes:
:I am searching for the escape sequences for the VT220 keyboard.  I have
:the correct sequences for the new keys added PF and F keys and also the
:keypad.  I simply need the alphabet portion of the keyboard.
:
:KEY     STRING
:
:ESC      <esc>[00q

  ESC is a special-case -- depending on the keyboard and terminal
  settings, ESC can generate the escape character, or an escape
  sequence that is interpreted by the host.  (According to ANSI
  specifications, a lone escape" isn't valid -- software that was
  developed for Microsoft operating systems or that was developed
  prior to the ANSI standards doesn't tend to follow this, however.)

  Other exceptions include <shift>, <control>, and <compose> -- these
  do not generate characters, they modify other keystrokes...

:That is what I am looking for except for the 58 keys.

  Depending on the operating system you have available, you can look in
  /etc/termcap (UNIX) or in sys$system:smgterms.txt (OpenVMS).

  [The main keyboard keys -- the QWERTY keys -- directly generate ASCII
  characters, not escape nor control sequences.]

  I've seen a recent reference to documentation available at the
  DIGITAL website for certain VT terminals, check www.dejanews.com
  for previous discussions.  (I'll see if I can remember to find
  that pesky URL later...  :-)

