"Mark R. Bowyer" <Mark.Bowyer@uk.sun.com> writes:

>Steve Kappel wrote:
>> In article <6a8f39$78e@news1.panix.com>,
>>         Bryan Althaus <bryan@panix.com> writes:
>> >Do you really think 8bit graphics is competing?  I can't remember the
>> >last time I worked with a PC that could only handle 256 colors.

>Sun is aiming to compete on price with PCs to stop Sun low-end users
>from moving over.  So they're not competing with what a PC has, they are
>trying to provide a good Sun at PC prices.  And low end Sun users are
>used to 8-bit framebuffers.  Gods, I only have 8 bit on my Ultra-1,
>though I suppose that's low end now.  16 bit actually - I have 2 of
>them. =O)

>If you look at this as a cheap Sun, the decision to go 8-bit makes
>sense, as long as you support 24-bit and fast 3D as an add-on.  Which
>they do.  But most low-end Sun users don't care, and will be more than
>happy with the speed hike at that price.

I suppose some Sun manager compared SS5 TGX sales with SS5 S24 sales,
and decided low-end Sun buyers didn't want 24-bit graphics.  Of course, the
more likely reason most people chose the TGX over the S24 was that the S24
was unacceptably slow.

Well, even if most low-end Sun users don't care, I do think it's still
short-sighted on Sun's part.  Bumping up the VRAM on the motherboard to 4MB
from 2MB would not have been all that much more expensive, and it would have
permitted the onboard ATI chipset to support 24-bit colour at reasonable
resolutions.  If it's easy to do, and it "buys you a lot", why not do it,
especially when you're trying to compete with PCs that already do it? 

Or at least there should be a video memory slot so that more VRAM can be
added.  NB: I haven't seen an Ultra 5 motherboard yet, so I'm not certain
there isn't one, but I would have expected Sun would have said something
if there was.

In any case, I don't expect this would be a problem that would be hard to
fix, since there are three perfectly good PCI slots in the Ultra 5, and
I don't see any reason why Sun can't support a 24-bit capable PCI framebuffer
in the Ultra 5, especially since they support plenty of such framebuffers
in Solaris x86.

Regards,

John
