In article <34CCD21B.685D@videotron.ca>, jf mezei <"[non-spam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca> writes:
>Not sure this merger is that powerfull. Remember that we are not talking
>about the Digital leader, but rather about a severely downsized Digital
>"also ran". The fact that a relatively young upstart managed to buy a 40
>year old company is a true indication of how insignificant Digital had
>become compared to its former self.

Digital's old mainframes, though in many ways superior, were never even
a threat to IBM's, and IBM's slower, more expensive, and harder to use
Systems 32/34/36/38 beat PDP-11s and MicroVAXes in that market in
Digital's heyday.  Though AT&T and Berkeley brought UNIX to maturity on
PDP-11s and VAXes, Digital refused to acknowledge a market for UNIX, and
let the upstart Sun beat them with Motorola 68000 CPUs long before
RISC.  Digital peaked as a one stop systems vendor despite itself, but
the industry changed.  The economics of running the fab plant recently
sold to Intel is very different from the economics of making 
MicroVAX II and LANCE chips.  They sold off more pieces than most of us
would have liked, but the Alpha, Galaxies, VMS, and (finally) UNIX
lines are hardly insignificant compared to comparable lines of the late
1980s.

Upstart?  I think not.  A Fortune 500 company with worldwide sales of
$26.4 billion last year fails to meet my definition of upstart.  Compaq
invented the PC clone when the industry was very young, grew to be and
still is the number one PC supplier, and has shipped over a million
servers, despite not having a service organization.  Though Digital is
older and IBM and HP older yet, Compaq is one of the most mature
companies in the industry.

Compaq gets a faster CPU than a Pentium II today without waiting for
Merced, and Galaxies.  That looks more to me like leadership, not "also
ran."  They get Digital's agreements with Microsoft and an outstanding
service organization.  Even without VMS, that makes Compaq a lot
stronger.  For VMS, how much worse could they do than Digital?

