>Back in 1984, I had to replace the carborator on my 1984 Chevy Impala.
>If it hadn't been under warranty it would have cost me about $250
>(parts) plus about $25/hr. for labor to install.

Sounds reasonable so far.....

>Today I will pick up my 1984 Chevy from the garage with a brand new
>"re-conditioned" carb ($635) plus labor at $55/hr.

But they are probably referring to the same workshop manual as they
did in 1984.

Seems to me you could have got a carby overhaul kit from a spares
shop, rebuilt the carby yourself, and got out of it for far less than
1984 prices, provided you were willing to learn by doing and do the
labour yourself. If it was really beat up/physically worn in the body,
you could no doubt get a used one from a wrecker and put a kit in
that.. That's all a "reconditioned" carby is.

>> From there I found the pricing, get ready to laugh/cry/vomit :
>>  ZE210-CW    VAX 11/785 CMPLT DIAG-RX01/MT      $12,000.00
>> Does this pricing seem excessive to other people or have I got the
>> wrong figure ?

It IS (IMHO) excessive in January of 1998. Perhaps is was not at the
time the machine was state of the art, but now it's not equatable with
reality.

>Perception is reality.

Yes, but who's perception are we talking about here. I find it
difficult to believe that DEC perceive that any sane person would
actually pay that for something that old in this day and age.

This guy only wanted the INFORMATION, not hardware.  It's schematics
and words on paper (or on computer somewhere), plus some obsolete
diagnostic software. He's not asking Field Service to come and fix it
for him,  or for a new, shiny CPU, HE wants to do the WORK himself,
not get someone else to do it at a premium, and LEARN from the
experience. The ridiculous part here is that the information/diagrams
etc already exist, but DEC want to sell them for what they were worth
at the time the MACHINE was worth more than $400 a ton for scrap
metal.  The value of the machine has become (from a commercial/serious
usage viewpoint) $0 but DEC still price stuff like this at the
original, ridiculous (by todays standards) list price.

>I guess Detroit is trying to tell me to upgrade my car - it's beginning
>to cost too much to maintain (finding old, out-of-date parts). One of
>these days I probably will, but not until the wheels fall off.

What, you don't have wrecking yards in the USA?
My vehicles date from 1974 and 1978, I get major parts from wreckers,
and replace with new only those things that need it (brake linings,
hoses etc). Why put brand new stuff in an old vehicle?? I do my own
work, if I don't know how, I get a workshop manual and find out how,
which is what Mr Piggott intended, but he didn't expect the "manual"
to cost 30 times the present value of the hardware.....  
(What was an 11/785 worth new anyway?? I suspect if they depreciated
the list price of the Diagnostics by the same percentage as the
machine has depreciated in value, plus a bit extra for still keeping
the stuff on file/in stock, that might well be a fair and reasonable
price)

>I guess I just really love old stuff.

Me too, I'm waiting for my employer to retire the VAX 6310 in a few
years time.  When they do, it will come home with me. (I rescued it
from becoming an upright tool chest (which the original owners
intended) once already.

> Alas, times change and this old stuff that I have is becoming obsolete and
> difficult to maintain (not to mention expensive).

Only if you choose to make it difficult and expensive.....There ARE
alternatives to full support from DEC, which is what the original
poster is wishing to explore.
DEC boxes and bits of this vintage can be had for the cost of freight
or free if you pick it up, or at a fraction of their original list
prices from secondhand dealers.  We've all seen posts about it here
and elsewhere.... 

Surely there must be a way, especially since DEC now provide a FREE
VMS license to hobbyists.  Why not do something similar with obsolete
technical information? 
Or do they really think it's still worth $12K???? 
 I really hope not.....

> Oh well, life goes on.

Yes, indeed.
I'm sure someone, somewhere, will help get Mr Piggot what he needs.
If DEC wanted to win some brownie points, maybe they should help out
"little people" like this without expecting to profit hugely from it. 

I intend no heavy criticism of DEC here, it's a niche market at best,
and they have no real interest in it, but good PR is worth its weight
in gold.
Glen, I'd be very interested to hear your, (or any ones) thoughts on
this.


