	Sad to report I don't believe they are teachable.  Very little
	backgrounding is going on here.  Let's cut to the chase:

http://www.news.com/News/Item/0,4,18475,00.html

		"In addition, Intel's next-generation 64-bit Merced
		chip will likely eliminate the performance advantage
                enjoyed by Sparc and other chips, and become the
                last nail in the coffin for many, if not all, competing
                technologies, according to analysts."

	Well well. . . how about this little snippet Brooke and Michael:

	"[Samsung's] 21264 versions of Alpha will be the first 1 GHz 
	processors with 100 SPECint95 range that have compatibility with the 
	latest version of the Windows NT operating system."

	That is from a Samsung press release.  Given that Merced is coming in
	at 40 SpecInt95 and the 21264 will be shipping soon (the part they
	are describing looks like EV68 i.e. the shrink to .18 micron about
	the same time or sooner than Merced I) what are we supposed to think?

	How is Merced going to eliminate the performance advantage?  But
	chew on the fact that FX!32 will be native in NT 5.0 and so 21264
	will run translated Intel binaries MUCH faster than Merced translated
	binaries.  Now do you get it???  Merced loses on translation and
	Merced loses on recompiles, loses BIG.

	But I really want to come back to one "of the many" analysts.  Back
	to yesterday when Linley Gwennap says:

http://www.news.com/News/Item/0,4,18468,00.html

	"While it's possible that Alpha could have some superior performance
	it's about how many R&D dollars Compaq wants to invest in a technology  
	that's not the standard."

	Look at that statement in light of Samsung's numbers above.  Today
	a Pentium II does 11 SpecInt95 with a strong tail wind.  An Alpha
	does 18 or so.  If Linley made the "some superior performance" 
	statement in regards to SHIPPING product, I would whole-heartedly
	agree.  I have made very similar statements.  However, Alpha's
	performance advantage over Intel is going to grow considerably
	given what we and Compaq know.

	Hey Linley, don't worry about the R&D.  From Samsung's perspective
	they are crowing 100 SpecInt95 with a part they really don't have to
	sink a lot of money into.  How many years until Merced hits
	60 SpecInt95?  4 or 5 years?

	By the time Merced I arrives, 21264 should be at .18 micron right 
	along with it.  How much do you think a 21264 will drop to in price 
	then?  400 bucks?  Should as the die size will be very tiny.  How 
	well will those $1000 Merceds at 40 SpecInt95 do against a $400 
	100 SpecInt95 part?

	One final thought that our trade-rag friends haven't touched on.
	Compaq knows both sides of the fence.  They have obviosly been getting
	many Merced details and now all the Digital Alpha details.  If Compaq
	is very postive publically on Alpha, ever wonder why?  Maybe Compaq
	knows who is blowing smoke and who isn't?  Compaq will know pricing
	and performance and what not of both CPUs and Intel will be
	manufacturing both.

