From what I've heard ( see comp.arch on Dejanews, Hot Chips symposium ),
Merced will be a VLIW-like extension to Risc (HP-PA?) - ie a load store architure
but with cunning information on scheduling embedded in the instructions.

The x86 compatibility is only for legacy apps, NT on Merced will 
run native. You'll need to recompile to get performance better
than x86 chips.

As to Pentium III's, Intel have already said that they'll continue
to develop x86 stuff for a few generations past Merced ( which
will initially be expensive, and if previous first generation
Intel designs are anything to judge by no terribly impressive 
