I think that like Java, developers could learn to develop code that's
really portable, ie that the only difference between running on 
x86 and Alpha is speed. Basically they could develop a gut feeling
for which bits of code are likely to be non portable. This lessens
the frequency of testing on Alpha.

> Of course, I'll have a different problem: convincing management that
> we need more machines. This will be especially difficult if people
> continue to buy into the mindset that little testing is necessary.
> 
> 
> Let's just agree to disagree, and let's check back in ten years to see
> who was right!

To be honest I have absolutely no idea about the next 10 years.

My gut feeling is that Java won't take off, Alpha will disappear,
and the PC world will gradually make the transition from x86
to Merced. No-one else will be able to afford to develop a competing
architecture. Intel rule the world.

Alternatively, Microsoft will agree on a ANDF style standard for 
distributing applications, that can be compiled to native on install.
Then we should see Win32 implementations from Microsoft and others 
running on a wide variety of hardware, from PC's to consumer stuff.
Intel still rule the PC world.

Or Merced flops disasterously, Intel goes into shock, someone else
designs a new processor, NT and various Unixes move to it. Someone
else rules the world.

Java takes off, the point becomes moot.

But yeah, we should agree to differ. My point is that you have a
great chip like Alpha, which almost no one I know is supporting,
despite the fact the decent OS's are portable, and so is most code
and I'm trying to work out a reason why, and also to suggest some
easy way for DEC ( Compaq? ) to change this. It's in no ones interest
that Intel have such a vast market share.

Tom.

