young_r@eisner.decus.org (Rob Young) wrote:
>	Given that, and the fact that in a press release on the 26th Samsung
>	revealed 21264 will be at 1 GHz and 100 SpecInt95 (no time frame  
>	given but no doubt at or before Merced I).  Many reports Merced I
>	to be a 40 SpecInt95 part on intro.

Many chips have outperformed many others, but it hasn't been
performance that's come out on top (slowpokes SPARC and x86 are prime
examples).  If performance mattered, SPARC would have been deep-sixed
four years ago.

Dec couldn't even get the 21164PC -- explicitly aimed at getting low
cost Alpha systems into the marketplace -- out the door when Intel was
still non-agressively pricing the P2.  Now that Intel is very
agressively pricing the P2 (a 266+mb is around $600 -- throw on video
[$200], memory, monitor and a disk and you have a fairly nice x86
system for about half the price of a 21164PC system from the AXP
cloners).

Performance just doesn't matter in the mass market if you can't meet
the consumer's prices and Dec's share of the workstation market (also
not entirely dominated by performance) was not enough to keep a
company of that size afloat.




