Thanks all. As everyone has pointed out, the lower bytes are correct. I
know that, I was more concerned about the upper incorrect bytes. :-)

Printfs and couts yield the incorrect result, unless I do a printf with
"%h", since the printf function then only really does look at the two
bytes.

Assigning to a temporary short and then to an int works. The problem, as
it turned out, is that the first short came in through a parameter, and
that parameter was being marshalled by some third-party software.

I suppose I phrased my subject wrong. Ladebug is correct, but I wondered
why then the compiler was producing wrong results. When assigning a
short, why was it looking at the other bytes? I guess it assumes it is
packed into an int for the parameter. Anyhow, the third party has fixed
the code and now it works.

But I'm still curious... what are the restrictions on the upper bytes
when a short is placed on the stack?

PS: The SEGV is my alias. :-)

