NetBSD has been ported to VAX.  You'll want to check the hardware list,
but I'm certain it does support the MicroVAX II - don't know about the
VAXStation II/GPX.

> Also fitted is a couple of memory looking boards with the number NS630
> (not MS630), a CMD SCSI controller and an M7504 adapter.
> 

"Lucky dog!".  The CMD SCSI controller is a VERY nice card.

> The system has no tape or floppy drive, only a full height 5.25" 760mb
> SCSI drive with nothing on it.
> 

NetBSD can be MOP loaded into the VAX.  You'll need another machine to
do the MOP loading - some on the NetBSD mailing list use FreeBSD.

I think you can easily hook a CD-ROM drive to the CMD SCSI card and boot
from the CD to load OpenVMS.  The CD-ROM MUST support 512 bps though. 
Some CD-ROMs have a jumper (Plextor), some come set at 512 bps (DEC
RRD40), and some can be "MODE SELECTED" (Chinon and others).  You _MAY_
be able to use the CMD SCSI card to send mode select commands to the
CD-ROM drive.  I'm not too familiar with the CMD series cards.

> Armed with this can anyone advise me on my options.

1.  NetBSD for VAX
2.  Hobbyist VMS

> 
> Thanks
> Pierre
> 
> --
> Wanganui, home of me!

