Maybe the time came for Compaq to consider having something 'better'. Do 
not forget that Compaq and Intel were at odds several times since 1992, 
and Compaq was considering producing Alpha systems since 1995 - just at 
that time the chips were too expensive and there was too little sofware. 
This I can confirm, having spoken with people at very high (VP) level at 
Compaq at that time. In fact, the person who stopped Alpha support in 
Compaq last time, Gary Stimac, is gone long ago. Also, Compaq's server 
engineering people are extremely keen to use Alpha: again this is 
directly from them.

Don't forget that Compaq DID have some good system R&D in the early 
years: being the first with a 386 machine in 1986, for instance - all 
done with TTL's (and few PAL's). Too bad Intel's chipset strategy soon 
after 'discouraged' any real R&D in future PC systems, and got PC vendors 
stuck with 64-bit 66 MHz bus for the past 4 years alone (since P5-66)!

As you all know, last October AMD licensed 21264 Alpha bus for their K7 
processor with the new Slot A (size like Slot 1, but 400+ MHz mem bus). 
Compaq was surely encouraged by the clear opportunity of common 
mainboards for K7 and 21264 (EV-67, EV-68) systems, with BIOS and chipset 
that have to recognize both according to DEC-AMD agreement! Of course, 
Compaq would encourage Samsung, Mitsubishi & AMD to push Alpha further, 
and that will happen. By the way, 21264 is expected to reach 1 GHz 
actually in EV-67 version (EV-68 will be closer to 1500 MHz).

Finally, whatever happens to Merced, HP will always have an edge over 
Compaq in Merced systems simply due to its involvement in the chip. 
Having Alpha is an ideal answer for Compaq: all that Merced promises (and 
more) right now. Again, I can confirm that, having discussed Alpha with 
Compaq US and Asia people on various levels since 1995. Believe me, it 
took them time...

Regards,
Nova



