Phillip Helbig wrote:
> 
> In article <6aiuhr$obb$1@wolfman.xtra.co.nz>, eatonw@fp.co.nz () writes:
> 
> > Chris Moore (cmoore@nas.net) wrote:
> > :  How will Alpha, VMS, and Digital UNIX fare?  Any opinions?
> > : Let's see how long before VMS makes it to the PC platform now!
> >
> > : This is, of course, assuming they don't just kill the product completely.
> >
> > : Say goodbye to VMS (followed shortly by Digital UNIX and AlphaNT, just as
> > : soon as the IA64 is available)
> 
> Correct me if I'm wrong.
> 
> What is the purpose of VMS on a PC/Intel Chip?  Alpha has better
> price/performance; what you pay for is VMS.  Whether or not that is
> cheaper has nothing to do with running it on Intel.  Linux guys are
> happy that Linux runs on Alpha; why should we want VMS on Intel?
> 

What is the purpose of VMS on Intel? -> Management

We have a have a cluster of 12 VMS systems in 5 buildings.  Except major
power fails,  operational management of these systems is a snap.  One
person could handle a cluster
a 100+ machines.  Attempt doing that with WIN95/NT4.0.

What is the purpose of VMS on Intel? -> Stability

VMS systems don't crash on program bugs.  I have an NT server that stays
up for months
at a time, (on the NET)  but it is a file and remote access server, no
apps run on it.
Other NT servers/Workstations can consistently crash from apps.

What is the purpose of VMS on Intel? -> Multi-user

I'd love to run software on another machine.  No can do.  I either have
to install it on
mine, or get NCD's server software.  With VMS and X11, this is not an
issue.

What is the purpose of VMS on Intel? -> Ease of data sharing

With clusters, I can share/manage/process data much easier than on PC
environment.
I can processe data using both vax code and alpha code in parallel on my
cluster, 
controlling it all from one machine.  Since  mixed  mode works here, it
should work
Intel.  I have access to over a hundred Intel machines.  I could get a
lot of 
processing done.


The bottom line is, there are many places to use VMS but cost is a   big
issue.  
Until recently, the lowest priced NT Alpha systems were 2X Intel in
price.  
Typically a VMS machine (workstation) is $2K-$4K higher than equivalent
NT Alpha.  
If I paid $3K for VMS on a $2K Intel box, I'd be at least $5K cheapher
than the 
lowest priced VMS box.  

The problem DEC has (Compaq) is that VMS is too good.  For those of us
who started
on 11/780's and  then got microVaxes and workstations, we found out that
a $30-60K
microVax, ran VMS, apps, clustering etc. almost as good as 780's and
8000's but
at a whole lot lower price point.  The big systems had and still have
advantages of
certain I/O speed and fault tolerance, but in many instances the small
machines run
fine.  VMS on Intel would lower profit margin  even more - except now
deal with
thousands of different types of hardware out there and IRQ's etc.  Yes
DEC would
have sold alot of VMS, but I am sure they wanted the hassles.

It is a big problem for a company like DEC to take a high end product
and more it
to a lower end market and maintain profits.  Microsoft is going the
other way.
They are taking a low end product and moving it to the higher end.  The
other
issue is that everybody underestimated where the PC market would be 16
years ago.

Had they realized it, the market would be a lot different today.

My 2 cents.


-- 
Gary James Muswick
Image Processing Engineer
Radiology Department
University Hospitals of Cleveland
muswick@uhrad.com



