By de-emphasized I mean the lack of native Microsoft apps. If
it were as important as x86 to Microsoft you'd get simultaneous
releases on x86 and Alpha which you don't at the moment.


Consider the following (IMHO) history of Risc support on NT. 
Much of this is from Showstopper but I don't think it's 
entirely inaccurate.

1) NT was originally designed for Risc systems, tested on I860, 
and ported to x86 later. Microsoft make a big fuss about portability. 
I860 looks like it won't end up as a volume PC CPU. I860 dropped. 

2) Mips chip looks cool + possibly high volume. Mips support added.

3) Dave Cutler personally makes sure the Risc and x86 versions stay synched. 
Lots of people in Microsoft management suggest concentrating on
x86 support at the expense of Mips. Dave disagrees.
NT ships pretty much simultaneously on both platforms. Ace consortium 
is the Next Big Thing

4) PowerPC looks cool + high volume. PowerPC support added.

5) Ace consortium concerned that people don't seem to be 
rushing to develop Mips native apps. Ace consortium drops Mips. 

6) Microsoft find it hard to support several platforms for products
like Office 9x, ActiveMovie etc. Management insided Microsoft 
thinks Mips and PowerPc have low market share, not worth
releasing Microsoft apps for, both get dropped.

7) Alpha chip looks cool + high volume. Alpha support added.

8) Microsoft drops support for two platforms. Still not all of 
Microsoft apps run native. Alpha has small market share.

9) Merced look cool, looks like it will be high volume. 
Merced support announced for NT.

10) Guess what happens next, given that Microsoft don't seem
to like supporting more than one non x86 platform.

> 
> >I still maintain that there is a practical difference between needing
> >and needing one for each developer.
> 
> 
> Again, I hope I never implied each developer needs one at all times.

No, but you need more of them if you want to use them to build
than if you want to just use them for testing.

PS - I think my argument for cross compilers is pretty 
water tight, but you obviously don't. I still don't 
really see why. At the risk of repetition my argument
can be summed up as saying that you need to buy less
Alphas if you can do all you're builds on x86.

I also suspect that in this case you'd quickly develop
an instinct for deciding if the last change you made 
merits a test on Alpha, or just a test on x86. In an 
ideal world you'd need access to one just before you 
actually release the code to the outside world.

Also for the record I think Alpha is a good chip which
could have succeeded if it had been cheaper for 
software houses to support it.

