Yes, someone can come into a board meeting and make all sorts
of statements. Doesn't make them true. If someone wants to
do a lawsuit, in such a setting, lies and mis-statements can
be shown to be such.

While the "all software has bugs" statement is in the main
true, in some situations the bugs are inconsequential. In 
others they cost big-time.

One of the reasons for VMS' relatively good security, aside
from having been to the wars, is the process by which it is
developed. New functions get proposed in response to problem
statements (which are documents), have investigation reports,
functional specs, design specs, and coding and testing. These
get reviewed at all stages, and there are check-in norms that
code should be reviewed even for minor patches. Also, the
internal culture takes security and data integrity VERY
seriously and generally bugs in those areas are considered
show stoppers.

The image of VMS' being expensive is in part due to lazy
evaluations by folks who recall the performance of VAX11/780s
relative to, say, Sparcstation 2, and conclude that VMS
runs on huge, slow, clunky iron.

DEC has (unfortunately) been charging more for VMS licenses
than for NT ones on Alpha, but that difference is not an
order of magnitude. 

On the other hand, anyone who cares to think about what the
existence of bugs like the "getadmin" NT bug mean (kernel
argument checking not done, or done wrong) will realize that
it is a symptom of the effects of implementation quality.
(System crashes under load, btw, are another.) Ask around what
process for software development is followed at non-VMS OS
sites...

Then you can perhaps decide whether accepting marketing info
satisfies legal "due diligence". 

I don't really expect many such lawsuits, because most judges
or juries won't understand the issues. But woe betide the
IT manager who makes such remarks if a well informed board
member starts asking WHAT "tests" were done and WHAT (if any)
REAL analysis was done. Alas that well informed board members
are probably few also...

however, I see Compaq will continue to invest in VMS, and
(considering they do tend to tell folks about their stuff)
maybe they can help cure some of the ignorance, once they've
had a chance to learn themselves.

