I'm sorry, I'm really not getting through. Let me try again.

You suggested shipping a single, P-code image for both Alpha and
Intel. I asked you what problem that solved. You said:

1) You ship one image, it runs anywhere. Thus people buy Alphas
because they're quicker for the same price, and run all the Win32 apps
they have natively. Choosing a CPU would be as painless as choosing a
video card.

You seem to be mixing several issues. One is the number of binaries
that get shipped. Another is the number of platforms supported. You
seem to be proposing P-code because it seems to solve both problems
(only one binary, all platforms automatically supported). This is a
great idea.

First, please notice that media is not so expensive that we need to
save space by reducing th number of binaries shipped. If I really want
to ship a single piece of media, I'll ship a CD. If I run over a
single CD, I'll ship two and try to keep all the binaries for a
particular platform on a single CD.

Also, I could be mistaken, but I don't think there are many vendors
who ship P-code versions of their products. I also wonder how many
operating systems ship a P-code interpreter?

There are undoubtedly reasons for these things. I don't pretend to
know all of them. One of the obvious reasons is performance. Another
is the fact that you STILL need to test the resultant code on all the
platforms you're going to sell it on. You STILL need to support the
code on all the platforms you're going to sell it on. P-code saves you
nothing in this regard. Compared to the cost of testing and support,
the cost of a development environment for the target platform is
miniscule. And you have to debug any platform-specific bugs on the
target platform anyway!

Note that the same arguments seem to apply to shipping Java bytecode.

>You could almost get to this stage with cross compilers if
>you could guarrantee source code compatibility between
>platforms, but as you point out you'd probably need to test release
>builds on each platform. From what I've read the Alpha and
>x86 NT platforms are pretty much identical except for
>aligment issues and Fx32 hooks, so you could probably avoid testing
>most builds on Alpha.
>


I consider it irresponsible to not test all baselevel builds on all
platforms.

Assume you have fixed all the logic bugs on your primary (x86)
platform. This leaves platform-specific bugs. These could be bugs in
your code, in the compiler, in the runtime libraries or in the
operating system. Let's say you do no testing of baselevel builds. If
the bug rate is sufficiently low, you're all set. But what if you
have, say, one bug per baselevel? What if you have, say, ten baselevel
builds per release? This means that, just when you're ready to
release, once you've succeeded testing on your x86 platform, you'll
now suddenly find ten bugs! And you'll have to fix them all at a time
when you'd prefer to be shipping!

Most likely, there will be more platform-specific bugs or problems
introduced than you will find in your testing. So you're leaving it up
to your customers to find them for you. But that's ok. You've got
those test machines available, and you'll quickly fix that bug and
build and ship a patched version. Now, did you test that patch on all
platforms? No? Oh, that's a pity...


