No. As I've been trying to get across to you, you cannot develop
high-quality software for a platform without sufficient resources to
build, test and support that platform. Cross-compilers or even P-code
do not address this problem at all. Therefore, lack of cross compilers
is not damaging to Alpha.

If you buy into the myth that interpreted binaries implies no need for
frequent, incremental testing, then I suppose you feel you would need
very few Alphas in order to sell an Alpha product, given
cross-compiler or P-code technology. However, I believe this is a
myth, plain and simple. By the time you have what I consider to be an
adequate number of machines for incremental testing, you'd just as
well do incremental builds on those machines as well. Then, the ratio
of off-platform machines to developers can approach 1:1. After all,
how much is your developer's time worth to you? Do you really want
your developers queuing for test machines?

