My experience with supporting multiple platforms and multiple
operating systems seems to completely contradict yours. Your argument
for cross-compilers seems invalid to me, as you assume similar quality
between software that is "only tested" on Alpha and software which is
frequently tested on Alpha. If the quality were the same, then of
course you could reduce the frequency of the tests. Perhaps we could
even reduce the frequency of tests to the point that developers only
test (and debug) their Alpha code just before passing it to QA. Then
it might make sense to use a cross-compiler (not P-code) since there
would be no machines on which to compile natively.

If the quality is not the same then you will have a problem.

Of course, I'll have a different problem: convincing management that
we need more machines. This will be especially difficult if people
continue to buy into the mindset that little testing is necessary.


Let's just agree to disagree, and let's check back in ten years to see
who was right!

