In article <6ak8h2$8ft$5@probity.mcc.ac.uk> helbig@man.ac.uk writes:
>
>X-windows is not the same thing as a GUI.  X-windows is just a cheaper 
>way of having several VTs in your room.  A GUI means clicking 5 times 
>and navigating through menus to get the same effect---with much more 
>overhead---of typing a few keystrokes.

  X-Windows is an interface between a graphical display and a window
manager (olwm, twm, etc).  The look and feel of a particular system's
user interface depends almost entirely on the window manager.  I use
olwm on my workstation to do what you describe, manage a bunch of 
virtual xterminals.  There have been written window managers which 
use X-Windows to emulate very closely the MS-Windows and MacOS look 
and feel.  There are some kinds of graphical operations which are 
more inefficient when implemented through the X-Windows interface than 
they are through GUI's implemented at a more primitive level (closer
to the hardware, not through a more abstracted hardware-independent 
interface), but on the most part they does a good job, even with multi-
media apps.  XAnime manages to synchronize a high-res, rapid-refresh
graphics animation with audio playback very well.

  The fvwm95 window manager approximates the MS-Windows95 GUI.  I hate
it, personally.  But it shows that X-Windows can be used to implement
a "real" GUI.

  -- Bill Moyer

