  My information is a few months old, but last I checked a 533MHz
21164PC processor only cost $495.  That was almost half of what a
top-of-the-line PII cost at the time, but the Intel prices have 
come down since then.  I imagine Digital's have, too.

  *System* prices have been much closer.  A high-end x86-based
desktop system and an Alpha-based desktop system each cost about
$3000 right now.  The main differences are that the Alpha system
is marginally more powerful for most applications, while the x86 
system can run a wider variety of applications.  (~1700 native 
Alpha-NT apps vs who-knows-how-many native x86-NT apps.)

  I've asked my friends who have purchased new high-end PCs why
they chose Intel over Alpha, and every single one of them said
"Games".  There's no question that the Alpha systems are cheaper
and faster, but the lack of natively compiled games for the Alpha
is keeping it out of the home market.  Maybe it's just the company
I keep (young professional technogeeks with lots of money to throw
around) but it seems like gaming is the only reason people have 
for upgrading their home computers anymore.

  -- Bill Moyer

