This merger is the greatest thing that ever happened to Compaq. Now they
have access to DEC's marketing muscle and prowness and can continue the
mission DEC set out for itself in the late 80s of taking on IBM. Remember
Chic Shue's "CLEAR AND POWERFUL MESSAGES"!!

Market share should grow to great bounds. DEC's highly acclaimed Channels
organization can take the lead at Compaq and help them realize gains they
never thought possible. If I were in Compaq's Channel organization, I'd
begin to be really concerned about the future of my job. DEC clearly has
had years more experience with internal political battles (and DEC has
never lost one!).

Initially, Compaq's bottom line will be impacted as a result of acquiring
company cars for their existing sales personnel but that will be offset by
the savings realized by DEC's reprogramming the voicemail of all the
employees they have working in "home offices" resulting in dramatically
lower head count. Can you imagine the reaction of some of these people when
they return from the golf course to find that their dial tone is gone?!?!
Thousands of dollars of bar bills no longer have a way of being faxed in.
Any attempt to drive to Maynard will be thwarted by the emergence of the
Mill  as a nursing home (it's been so long since some of the DEC people had
been to the office that this will be news to them).

Beware though as hundreds of DEC's Legal Department realize they need to
find other sources of income. I'm sure we will see many more Lawyer
commercials being played during Saturday cartoon time.

Above all, everyone should eventually learn that, in this industry, you
should have as many girl friends as you can muster. Just don't fall in love
or the pimps will get you!



