Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com> writes:

>One of my friends also acquired a SMS 1000 model of some kind many
>years ago.  It also came with a hard drive and a floppy which in its case
>happened to be 8" RX03 hardware compatible - except that the floppy
>was accessed via the DU MSCP software device driver.  This arrangement
>was not too strange if the floppy was used only on one side - SSDD as
>per the DEC RX02 standard - fortunately there are index holes on an 8"
>floppy diskette and two sensors on an 8" floppy disk drive which determine
>whether the floppy media is single or double sided.  With the SSDD media,
>a transfer between a DEC (or compatible) RX02 and the SMS worked
>very well.  It was even possible to produce a bootable RT-11 SSDD
>diskette on the RT-11 side using the DEC RX02 drive by:
>"COPY/BOOT:DU   DY0:RT11FB.SYS  DY0:"
>as an example.  On the SMS system, the ":DU" option had switched
>the boot block to be the MSCP device and everything worked just fine.

>BUT, for double-sided 8" floppy media which had the index hole in the
>other position, everything was now very different.  Now there really
>has never been a standard for DSDD 8" floppy media on the RT-11, so
>this is VERY DEFINITELY NOT A COMPLAINT that on the
>one other RX03 drive my friends had from an DSD 880/30 that the
>cylinders were not interleaved as opposed to the SMS solution for
>the 8" floppy hardware in which DSDD 8" floppy media had the
>cylinders interleaved.  To be more explicit, for 8" SSDD media, there
>were 988 blocks in total for both DSD and SMS with only one side
>of the media used.  When the media was double-sided (the index hole
>in the 8" floppy media was in the other position), a DSDD 8" floppy
>media had 1976 blocks in total for both DSD and SMS.  But, with
>DSD, the whole first side of the media was read first followed by the
>whole second side of the 8" media.  For SMS, the cylinders were
>interleaved - first track was first head, first cylinder; second track was
>second head, first cylinder; third track was first head, second cylinder;
>fourth track was second head, second cylinder; etc.  This meant that
>a DSDD floppy from DSD was not compatible with a DSDD floppy
>from SMS since the order of the blocks was different when the
>blocks were read after the first track.  This difference had nothing
>to do with the nature of the device driver that was being used to process
>the I/O requests - DY in the case of DSD and DU in the case of SMS.
>Of course, the RT-11 DY device driver (a non-DEC modification)
>could have been re-modified to use the SMS definition, but by that
>time in the whole process, the 8" floppy was being phased out of
>use and no further effort was deemed to be worth while.  It could
>still be done if there is any interest - probably a simple change in
>the code to determine the next track.

>Sincerely yours,

>Jerome Fine
>RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict
>Year 2000 Solutions for Legacy RT-11 Applications
>(Sources not always required)



