In article <6aj8b2$fpu$1@nntp6.u.washington.edu>,
G. Herrmannsfeldt <gah@u.washington.edu> wrote:
>
>I have various RT11 formatted 8 inch floppies that I might want to read,
>including some from an SMS1000 DSDD, so it is nice to know how they are
>formatted.  I do have an RT11 system, though I haven't run it for a while.
>It has the DY driver, but no DSDD driver.  I believe the controller I have
>(non-DEC) can do DS, and I do have a DSDD drive.

Were the controllers MTI, Dilog, Sigma, DSD/Qualogy, or something
else?  Unfortunately, many implement DSDD slightly differently than the
SMS1000.

>I used to have a description of the RT11 file system, but I don't think
>I have that anymore.  I used to use it to edit disks to recover lost
>files, and related problems.

The big help in figuring out interleave issues is that all RT11 files
are contiguous in logical block number.  This makes it enormously easier
to figure such stuff out.

>I source for the DY driver available if one wanted to do this modification?

RT-11 ships with uncommented DY driver source.  Many third-party controllers
either came with DY.MAC patches or with a custom-written driver.  For example,
if you have a Sigma MXV22 floppy controller, the driver and documents were
contributed to DECUS and are available on the the 11S109 RT SIG tape.
You can also get these via ftp, from

 ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11
     /rt/decus/11s109/22bit_dev

Tim. 



