Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
|In <timleeEn9o25.26z@netcom.com> nobody@not.for.email (Timothy J. Lee) writes:
|
|>Bryan Althaus <bryan@panix.com> writes:
|>|I've always worked on UNIX, for a Fortune 500 type companies, and the
|>|need to run Windows software is a major reason for going with NT
|>|along with cost.  This would help.
|>
|>Actually, it is the (sometimes political) need to run MS Windows software
|>(e.g. "lock in" to legacy software) that pushes Microsoft Windows NT,
|>not cost.  Unix does not really cost significantly more than Microsoft
|>Windows NT on the same hardware (and may cost less), and costs less in
|>administration over the long term.
|
|It's not the cost of running the OS itself, it's the cost of running
|third party application software that pushes NT.  NT licenses are often
|*much* cheaper than Unix licenses for the same product.

I've often seen the opposite, since MS Windows licenses often are not
available as floating or otherwise sharable licenses.  Unix also has
a lot of good quality freeware that provides functional substitution
for many things that must be bought for MS Windows.

Still, the extra administration cost of MS Windows (including NT)
is likely to be a major, if hidden, factor -- consider also that most
MS Windows users have to do some system administration (computer
crashed, must reboot/reconfigure/reinstall), while most Unix users in
most well run Unix shops have no need or desire to use the root
password (obviously, those doing OS-level stuff or drivers or plugging
in hardware are an exception).

