steve.kappel@iname.com (Steve Kappel) writes:
|Another thing is that Sun, and many other people, believe that
|a complete computer under $2K just doesn't make sense because
|of the cost of ownership issues.  Their solution is the NC.
|If they offered a $1K workstation that wouldn't be in line with
|the paradigm they are pushing.

Is there any conceptual difference between an NC and a dataless or
diskless workstation?  If not, then the NC concept isn't new; many
organizations have been using it for years (since NIS and NFS; the
most common use of this model is probably student computer labs in
colleges).  $1000 or $2000 computers, set up for dataless operation
and connected to a network with a file server, can have quite low
maintenance and total ownership costs.  But, unlike a dedicated NC,
such computers could also be useful as standalone computers for home
or small office use.

(By "dataless", I mean having the OS and swap space and maybe a
few heavily used applications on local disk, but with user accounts,
data, and most applications stored on the file server and accessed
through NFS and NIS.)

