R!ch wrote:

> MY understanding is that the PCI drivers should be fairly straight
> forward to port, given the source code compatability betwenn x86 and
> SPARC, so I wouldn't be too surpised to see a whole bunch of x86
> support PCI hardware suddenly supported on the new Ultra systems.

As long as you only want to use that device once the OS is up, that's
true.  But if you want to use it at the boot prom - before and during
boot time - you're in trouble.  The bloody thing has to be in the
firmware, and supported in a multitude of different ways you can ignore
on a PC box because the BIOS handles it.

Thus the poor graphics adapter support.

BTW (and not something R!ch said), why are people going on about the
8-but graphics as standard?  These boxes are cheapo units to keep our
desktop Solaris customers happy.  They have 8 bit graphics now and are
quite used to it.  Colour flashing is annoying if you happen to have a
shakey mouse hand, but if you stay in a window to actually do some work,
who cares?

If they want better graphics, then they can add one of the supported
frambuffers to do that.  Why should we all have to pay for that when all
we do is develop and support?

I'm quite looking forward to replacing my US-1 with a US-10 soon, though
until I can get a second framebuffer in it, I *am* going to miss being
double-headed.

So why all the moaning?

