>size of Philips have an Alpha or two somewhere, but not enough to
>hand them out to developers. If I could build my code here an ftp the
>binaries across the site for testing, I'd probably try it.


I have been focusing my remarks on software vendors: companies which
create software for the purpose of selling it and making a profit.

1) I think most companies would have their sources and/or source
control system on a server. You don't have to move your project
anywhere to get it to build on the Alpha boxes, just point your Alpha
workstation at the sources the same way you would your x86
workstation.

2) I think most would give someone physical access to the machines. If
nothing else, the QA people probably want to be able to reconfigure
machines for different test scenarios. Maybe in your situation the
developers aren't physically close to QA?

3) If you're supporting multiple platforms - whether different CPUs or
just different OS versions, then you really had better do your "real"
builds on all of the platforms in order to avoid nasty surprises. I
agree that each developer does not need all platforms on his desktop,
and does not need to build all platforms every time he changes a line
of code. But before he checks his sources in, I hope he's built and
tested on all relevant platforms. Otherwise, QA is testing a large
amount of untested code each time. Sure, you've checked the logic on
the other platform, but it would be nice if QA didn't have to waste
their time finding trivial bugs the developer should have caught. The
developer knows what he's changed, and should point-test that change
on all platforms.

4) If you can't afford enough machines to build and test on, then
maybe you can't afford to support a new platform.

>Looking back at NT4.0, even if you were desperate to support all
>platforms, you'd be hard pressed to justify buying 4 workstations
>per developer to do it. Even now, no manager can justify buying an
>Alpha and an Intel platform per developer, but that is the only
>way you could support both platforms.


It is not necessary for each developer to have "n" machines. A
developer needs to be able to build code on platform A instantly. He
needs to be able to test and debug on platform A instantly. He needs
to be able to build on the other platforms quickly, once he's gotten
his code to work on platform A. He needs to be able to test on the
other platforms quickly, once he's tested on platform A. Whenever a
"full build" is done (nightly, weekly, per-baselevel) the build needs
to be done for all platforms. Whenever QA is done, it needs to be done
on all platforms (and maybe cross-platform if it's that kind of
software).

None of this implies "n" machines per developer. But you do need build
machines for each platform (maybe server-class machines if the
development environment supports "batch" builds), and you need enough
test machines for the multiple platforms that developers aren't
spending too much time waiting for a test machine.

>It's all very well to say that any company that wants to support
>Alpha will do it, but most companies could'nt afford to even if
>they wanted to.

Again, I'm talking about serious companies here. Perhaps I should work
out the math, but offhand, it seems to me that there is a large class
of companies who could expect to earn more money on their Alpha
version than it would cost to equip their developers to develop it.

> I suspect this sort of argument happened at Microsoft
>when NT was released, and this resulted in two Risc platforms being
>dropped and the remaining one appears to have been de-emphasized.


De-emphasized? By whom? When was it ever "emphasized"? And you might
want to take a look at some of the recent press. Among other things, I
think I just read about FX!32 being added to NT 5.0 integrated into
the system...

>I still maintain that there is a practical difference between needing
>a machine for testing ( testing departments already test on a variety
>of Intel machines, adding an Alpha wouldn't make much difference ),
>and needing one for each developer.


Again, I hope I never implied each developer needs one at all times.

