John Saunders <jws@jws.ultranet.com> wrote in article <6aikga$u8v$1@decius.ultra.net>...
> >There are two ways around this - one of them should have been part
> >of NT from the start, ie that Win32 binaries are a kind of
> >p code that gets translated at load or install time[1],
> 
> FX!32 didn't exist when Windows NT 3.1 was introduced.

No, I meant distributing the applications in P code as opposed
to native binaries. Something like Java but designed to be compiled
to native rather than interpreted. That way you'd distribute one
Win32 'binary' that would work on any Win32 platform.

> 
> >and one
> >could have been retrofitted, ie cross compilers for all architectures
> >are distributed with Visual C++.
> 
> Given a software company which has decided to support an Alpha version
> of their product, that software company would need Alphas to test on.
> So, why would they want a cross-compiler when they can have a native
> compiler running on the Alpha they need to support their product? And
> they still need to support and test on Alpha, regardless of the
> compiler technology they use.
> 

Ease of use. The fact that you don't need to move your C++ project
over to another machine. The fact that you don't need to have physical
access to the machine ( it could be on the other side of the world - if
you have a cross compiler you could do almost all of the development
on x86, occassuionally checking if the Alpha version is Ok. ). If 
you can trust the source compatibility, you don't need to own an
Alpha, just have time booked on one for testing. Most companies the 
size of Philips have an Alpha or two somewhere, but not enough to 
hand them out to developers. If I could build my code here an ftp the 
binaries across the site for testing, I'd probably try it. 

Looking back at NT4.0, even if you were desperate to support all
platforms, you'd be hard pressed to justify buying 4 workstations
per developer to do it. Even now, no manager can justify buying an
Alpha and an Intel platform per developer, but that is the only 
way you could support both platforms.

It's all very well to say that any company that wants to support
Alpha will do it, but most companies could'nt afford to even if
they wanted to. I suspect this sort of argument happened at Microsoft
when NT was released, and this resulted in two Risc platforms being 
dropped and the remaining one appears to have been de-emphasized.

I still maintain that there is a practical difference between needing
a machine for testing ( testing departments already test on a variety
of Intel machines, adding an Alpha wouldn't make much difference ),
and needing one for each developer.

Tom.

