Douglas H. Borsom wrote:

> In article <6ai9cm$n10@flonk.uk.sun.com>, andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com# wrote:
>
> >[...opinions of the Compaq -- Digital Equipment deal....]
>
> Andrew, how about giving  us an opinion of what this will mean for
> Sun (NT?  What's NT?) Microsystems.

Not a lot though having one less competitor is always a good thing.

I am not sure what your point about NT is.  Having bet your business
so comprehensively on NT like Digital looks increasingly like a really
bad idea.

NT 5 which in its 64 bit for would have offered Digital a pottential Marketing
lead over its other NT competitors is getting later and later.

Terrible beta reports have made Gartner and other analysts advise
corporates against NT 5.0 adoption until at least late 2000.

So the 64 bit NT window of opportunity for Digital is closing or has allready
closed. The window of opportunity being the time between the introduction
of 64 bit NT on Alpha and the general availability of Merced based NT
systems.

Digital will be left with trying to sell high end DU and OpenVMS systems
without the software catalogue of either HP, Sun or IBM and into a
conservative environment increasingly concerned about Alpha, DU
and OpenVMS's long term future.

Having a pure UNIX platform with very good NT integration products and
services has not done Sun any harm. As has been pointed out before
Sun's market valuation is twice Digitals.

But seriously very few people outside Digital or Compaq will really care
unless you are a Digital customer. Compaq will need to be very carefull
not to expend too much energy digesting Digital. There are plenty of
examples of what can go wrong in a merger/takover of this kind in the
computer business, Unisys, Prime/CV and HP/Appollo come to mind.

regards
Andrew Harrison
