In article <6agqt1$r8r$1@news.muenchen.roses.de>, ray@cult.ray.org (Ray) writes:
:I saved a VAXstation II/RC from being 'recycled' a few month ago. I meanwhile
:got the right cable to connect the thing to a keyboard and my VR260 Screen,
:and it seems to be OK on the hardware level. It has a TK 50 tapedrive and
:a Maxtor HD (model might be XT-2190). I have never used a VAXstation II
:(I've though got some experience with VAXstation 3100 and some DECstations)
:and the console (>>>) seems to be less powerfull than the ones I know,
:at least it seems not to have a 'help' or '?' command. Does anybody know
:a place on the net where I can find documentation?

  The major console commands are:

    BOOT
    HALT
    INIT
    UNJAM
    EXAMINE
    DEPOSIT
    TEST

:My first problem is: how can I see which devices are connected?

  You'll have to look in the Q-bus backplane -- I've posted various
  previous discussions of this (www.dejanews.com), but the only way
  you can verify the contents of the Q-bus, as well as the settings
  of the CSRs and interrupt vectors, is to pull the Q-bus modules and
  decode the switch settings.

:Neither
:'t -c' nor 's dev' work. 't' results in 'error.' and 't -c' in '?18 INV DGT'. 

  TEST requires a test number.

:I have no idea how the harddisk might be called? The tape is mua0 
:(what stands 'u'for? the controller?) 

  MUA0 and DUA0 are the tape and disk names -- "MU" is an TMSCP or
  compatible tape controller, while "DU" is an MSCP or compatible
  disk controller.  "A" is the first controller, and 0 is typically
  the firse unit.

:and I can successfully boot the ultrix
:4.4 installation tape from it, but it then says that no harddisk suitable for
:installation was found.  The HD seems to be connected correctly (looks like
:it's an MFM drive?), so I suspect it's an unsupported type?

  You'll need to specify what type of controller.

  Chances are, it is either unsupported or -- more likely -- it is
  set at the wrong CSR and/or interrupt vector -- most folks new to
  the Q-bus are unaware of the cascading nature of the settings, and
  the need to have each and every Q-bus board configured individually,
  based on the complete list of Q-bus boards present -- the required
  settings are based on the aggregate of all boards present, and the
  addition or removal of a board can cause cascading changes to the
  CSR and interrupt vector settings on one or more other Q-bus boards.

  You will need to tell us *every* Q-bus board present in the system,
  and *exactly* which slot the board is in.  Based on this, someone
  here can probably tell you what CSR and vector the board should be
  set at.  You'll also end up needing to know how to decode the CSR
  and interrupt vector switchpacks on each module, so that you can
  read off the current setting and then (if necessary) change it.  DO
  NOT OMIT ANY CONTROLLERS PRESENT IN THE Q-BUS DURING CONFIGURATION.

:How can I find out whether there's a system installed on the HD? Typing
:boot results just in '2..' and after a timeout 'RETRY' and '2..' again.

  This error -- effectively -- means that there is no correctly
  configured device controller with a visible operating system.

  Please visit one of the MicroVAX sites mentioned in the OpenVMS
  Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ), and please take a look at some
  of my previous postings on the Q-bus topic (via dejanews).

  Also be aware there is a hobbyist version of OpenVMS VAX V6.1 that
  is available via DECUS in many areas -- see www.montagar.com.

