David Hull wrote:
> There have been Compaq-DEC rumors for years.  I remember my DEC sales
> rep mentioning in February of '96 that Compaq would either buy DEC, or
> start selling Alpha workstations. 

My take on this is that Compaq did reach an agreement with Digital just
prior to buying Tandem. At that time, it looked as if Compaq had failed
to reach agreement with DEC and turned around to buy Tandem as a second
choice.

In hindsight, I beleive that DEC and Compaq had reached an agreement and
that Compaq had given directives to Palmer of what to do to make it
possible for Compaq to save Digital.

Specifically:
	-Selling off printers
	-Selling off other things
	-Finding a way to offload the semiconductors, use the fact that 
	 Intel had copied Alpha designs to irritate Intel into a legal
	 hassle which concluded in DEC selling the FAB plant, and Intel 
	 continuing to inspire itself from Alpha design except it now had to
	 pay for it.


> I really think it will have a
> positive effect, in terms of infusing cash into DEC, but I think the
> most important effect it could have is better management and, hold the
> phone,
> 
>       MARKETING!

I don't see cash infusion into this. DEC has tons of cash already. I do
not see many short term changes at Digital. I suspect that it will take
at least a year before we start to see substantial changes. As long as
the same folks remain at the helm of Digital, don't expect many changes.

And as far as MArketing, if DEC had wanted to attract attention, it
should have chosen another week to be eaten up, or Palmer should have
made claims to have slept with Bill Clinton.

It is still too early to look at the Tandem-Compaq example to gauge what
will happen to Digital in the longer term.

My biggest question is whether the Digital name will, in the long term,
survive or whether Compaq will be king. Will Tandem's name also survive
?

I think that the biggest looser in this is IBM. Compaq now has true
fault tolerance with Tandem, disaster tolerance and clustering with
Digital-VMS, it is 2 flavours of UNIX (Tandem's Flault Tolerant Unix (!)
and DEC's), as well as production facilities for PCs and servers running
MS stuff. 

In Compaq's statement, there is mention of Compaq seing value in
Digital's loyal installed base. I wonder if Compaq will find out how
many "loyal" customers are angry at DEC and if Compaq will take steps to
reduce irritants. Only time will tell.

Personally, I am very disapointed. Digital, as a name, is history. It
really should have been buying Compaq a "small" PC maker instead of 
Compaq buying Digital. But Olsen failed to change Digital in time, and
Palmer succeeded in downsizing Digital to extinction.



