> 
> Assuming Compaq keeps VMS around and intends to market it properly, VMS will be
> on a desktop Intel PC soon.  I give it 2 years.  Mark my words and note the
> date...1/26/98 at 12:24pm EST.
> 
> If I wasn't such a poor slob, I'd put money on that bet ;)

The problem is that most people want their computer experience to be
as simple as possible.  That's why Mac's were invented, and why MS
has ripped off so much of its own OS ideas from the Mac.
Heaven forbid they should have to actually type in a command!
Go over to comp.sys.ibm.pc.games.strategic and start talking about
DOS and you will get tons of hate mail about how evil DOS is and that
Win95 is great cause it is so simple and does everything for you and
you don't have to worry about drivers or remembering commands and
the like.  And strategy games are not for those with weak minds!
Even if VMS is "easy" for a command line driven OS, that isn't good
enough!  They need to be able to point and click!

So, it has *everything* to do with running VMS on an Intel chip.
There simply won't be enough of a demand for personal desktop PC's
running VMS to make it worth the effort of supporting.
DUNIX will die for similar reasons. 

They might live on a bit longer for servers, but not as a money
maker on personal PC's.  Not at home, not in the office.
What's the secretary going to do with VMS?!? or DUNIX?
And just forget about the pointy-haired boss types... ;P

Without the large customer base, VMS (and DUNIX) are restricted
to high end machines, which will keep the cost of them as high as
they are now (why should I spend $1000 for DUNIX for my home PC when
I can get Linux for free?) and therefore marginal, if they don't die
off completely.



