   I will eventually stop responding to the rumors regarding the
   demise of VMS, but I'd like to leave with one thought.

   Consider a case in which an enterprise opts to use not VMS,
   but Unix or perhaps NT for mission-critical applications
   and they experience a profound loss of service perhaps from
   a denial of service attack, the exploitation of a fundamental
   OS shortcoming, or from some other vulnerability which does
   not exist in VMS.  Whom would be held accountable?  Would the
   stockholders or if not public, the clients hurt (consider
   a hospital, for example) have some recourse?  Doesn't a company
   have an obligation to be reasonable secure? If they do,
   then it would seem to me that VMS, or a similarly secure
   system, would be _mandated_ as a prudent and ordinary measure
   of feduciary responsibility.

   Now, I'm not a litigous type, but consider that the groundwork
   is being prepared right now for lawsuits regarding Y2K failures.
   Why isn't an entity (company, whatever) similarly liable for
   accepting an inferior, vulnerable OS?

   VMS' nitch is security, stability, maturity and excellent design.
   It is Digital's responsibility to make that known to the people
   who make the big decisions 'upstairs.'



