	Like if Digital wanted all the Hobbyists to go take a
leap, they'd do it by allowing DECUS to issue you a license.  Right.

	People, when will you get it through some of your heads that
the problem isn't "Digital wants to sink VMS", but that "Microsoft
can market water to a drowning man."  Microsoft is doing EVERYTHING
it can to insure that their products only run on Windows NT (despite
the "Alliance", none of the BackOffice products are moving to OpenVMS),
getting poor PC users all over the world to BUY pre-release Beta
software and debug it for them.  By the time you buy all the "Developer"
stuff you need just to keep a working system, you've probably alread
spent more than you have if you just bought OpenVMS.  And you wouldn't
have to reboot so much and reload the OS numerous times.

	Yes, OpenVMS costs more.  But you get what you pay for, and
if you want a $9.95 operating system, then don't bitch when it isn't
as reliable as something else.  Sorry, folks, but the old rule of
marketing applies here as well as anywhere else:  good, cheap, fast -
pick any two.  OpenVMS is good and fast, and I'm not sure Microsoft
can get even TWO of the choices since they seem to be opting only for
cheap.  And wait until the heart failures when Microsoft announces
the pricing for WNT 5.0 Enterprise (which means that WNT is finally
out of Beta Test).  OpenVMS won't seem so expensive then.

	So if you want to bitch that OpenVMS is too expensive,
then why is the man that owns the cheap stuff the one at the top
of the "Richest Man" list?  Marketing, Marketing, and Anti-Trust.

	I showed a young WNT guy stupid stuff I could do with 
OpenVMS (X-windows and alternate display locations, AMDS, bring
things up/down without rebooting, quick DCL commands for common
things, etc), and he thought it was the neatest stuff.  It was
like all new to him, but we've been doing stuff like this for
10 years.  Marketing, my friends, pure marketing.

	Just today, I had a PC freak out and almost destroy a
document on a floppy, have incompatible MSWord files that I
couldn't use, an NT system that scrambled itself.  The wonders
of modern technology.  For all those people that are afraid
of Y2K, relax.  W95/WNT problems, reboots, and crashes will
likely occur more often than any of your Y2K glitches.  And at
least you can fix your Y2K glitch without reloading your
entire machine from scratch (again).

