John Saunders <jws@jws.ultranet.com> wrote in article <6a6f4f$ujk$1@decius.ultra.net>...
> Also, recompiling an application for a new platform isn't the only
> cost involved in shipping the application on that platform. There are
> the costs of shipping multiple copies of the executables, the cost of
> changing the install procedure to install the correct version, the
> cost of documenting all of this, and the cost of supporting the users
> who somehow manage to get it all wrong despite your best
> documentation.
> 
> This is not to mention the cost of testing the product on all
> platforms for all tests you ever perform on the Intel platform. Of
> course, you have to buy and maintain the machines you use to do this
> testing. As well as the extra developer seats on the other platforms.
> You may even need to test interoperability between the various
> platforms.
> 

There are two ways around this - one of them should have been part
of NT from the start, ie that Win32 binaries are a kind of 
p code that gets translated at load or install time[1], and one
could have been retrofitted, ie cross compilers for all architectures
are distributed with Visual C++. Dec could just give away a cross
compiler now, if they could guarrantee that Alphas will run 
any Win32 code that runs on Intel[2]. As you say the current 
source code compatible / no [supportted] cross compiler model
for NT inevitably means that non Intel platforms get ignored 
by most ISV's.

[1] Here I'm thinking of the output from the front end of a 
C compiler designed specifically to be compiled to native, 
as opposed to something like Java which is designed to be 
interpreted.

[2] AFAIK alphas handle misalignment faults transparently and
this is the only problem I can see with cross compiling code.

It'll be interesting to see if Intel handles thinks any 
better with Merced.

