Organization: DECUServe
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.alpha



Dear Michael,

	Recently, I penned a missive I called:  "The Massively
	Muddled Alpha Message" right here in Usenetland.  Comp.sys.dec
	and vmsnet.alpha to be specific.  Funny, it didn't take long
	at all for an example of what I was talking about to rear
	its ugly head . . . again.  In:

http://www.news.com/News/Item/0,4,18431,00.html

	you talk about Wednesday's upcoming Digital/Microsoft Enterprise
	NT announcement.  You start out well but then spin off like many of 
	your fellow writers have done.  Specifically:

                "In a massive legal settlement last year, Digital
                transferred its chip fabrication plant to Intel and
                said it would adopt Intel's upcoming 64-bit chip,
                code-named Merced. Those terms, according to
                many, signaled a slow death for Alpha. "

	There is no empirical evidence to support a "slow death" claim.  In 
	fact, Digital is set to open up a very substantial performance lead
	OVER ALL and appear able to sustain that lead for at least 4 years.
	Mr. Bob Palmer, leaves much to imagination but does hint at that
	in his remarks regarding the quarter just ended:

	"We are in an excellent position to maintain and enhance Alpha's 
	performance advantage over all other architectures far into the future."

	There are many here in comp.sys.dec that would agree that is
	an accurate statement.  So while you say: "according to many", I
	too would say: "according to many" Alpha will have a very nice
	growth curve over the next 4-5 years.  Much greater than today
	due to very high performance.  Terry Shannon writes to Usenet
	in December 1997:


FIRST, Intel does not OWN the Alpha chip. When the deal goes through,
Intel will own DEC's Fab-6 plant.

SECOND, Intel is obligated to produce Alpha chips for some period of
time. Reliable sources claim the timeframe is ~7 years.

THIRD, Intel is not DEC's only Alpha Fab partner. Mitsubishi and Samsung
also produce Alpha chips. I would keep a close eye on Samsung; they are
ahead of the curve on CMOS7 process.

FOURTH, which "generation" of Alpha are you referring to?
Third-generation EV6 is in production at DEC and Samsung,
fourth-generation EV7 is prototyped, and fifth-generation EV8 is in
advanced development. That takes us beyond Y2K. Beyond EV8, DEC isn't
very specific, but word has it that EV9 and EV10 are on the advanced
development roadmaps.

As long as DEC can maintain a significant performance differential
vis-a-vis IA64, there's no reason that Alpha will die. That is, of
course, if DEC finally decides to market the architecture!

Cheers,

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC
http://www.acersoft.com


	The EV7 he talks of has been hinted at being a 150 SpecInt95 part.
	At 150 SpecInt95 and coming on at the end of 1999, you might want
	to ask yourself how that would compare to known Merced I performance
	targets.  I have read 35-40 SpecInt95 for Merced I.  In your 
	estimation, how would you compare the two?  Would you give Alpha half 
	a chance at least through 2000?  

	At best, Alpha has about a 60% integer performance premium over Intel 
	today.  Given what we can expect with enhanced performance, the 
	differential should be growing to 200-300% or better.  I know this is 
	hard to believe because Digital has been very quiet about EV7 
	(aka Arana).  Maybe you would be interested in finding out more
	about EV7, it is obviously a tremendous engineering achievement given
	the performance targets.

			Sincerely,

				Rob Young

	<< Attachment:  The Massively Muddled Alpha Message >>

cc: michaelk@cnet.com
    comp.sys.dec
    vmsnet.alpha




