Jim Griffin wrote:
> 
> Hello.
> 
> I have a net with a mix of machines - Alphas, SGIs, Suns running SunOS
> 4.1.x, Suns running Solaris 2.5, and some older Encore real-time machines
> running a somewhat old version of Encore's proprietary MPX OS.
> 
> I've run into a stumbling block.  From SunOS 4.1, I can telnet and ftp to
> the Encore machines.  However, the Alphas and Solaris 2.5 machines hang at
> the connection.  All of the Unix boxes run from the same NIS server, so
> it's not an addressing issue.
> 
> Using EtherPeek on a Mac (Etherpeek is a Mac packet sniffer), I've noticed
> a difference in the IP header between the Alpha and the SunOS machines
> when attempting the connection.  The Alpha sets the IP Don't Fragment
> flag, which apparently the Encore can't handle.
> 
> Is there a way that this can be turned off?
> 
> TIA
> 
> --
> Jim Griffin - TNS/AANR
> griffin@vol.com
> "You can fool all of the people some of the time ---
>                and that's sufficient.

This most likely is a result of Path MTU Discovery.   

If the Alphas are running TCPware, then try:

	$ NETCU START/TCP/NOPATH_MTU_DISCOVERY

If you are running Multinet, then try:

	$ MULTINET SET/KERNEL TCP_DO_MTU_DISCOVERY 0

I don't believe UCX will do Path MTU Discovery...

If the Alphas are running Unix instead of VMS, then find the equivalent 
Unix commands.

