Are you referring to Netcom (your ISP), the ISP that no longer sells
user accounts with shell access?  Or Concentric (mine), which does
still sell accounts with shell access but has a highly modified
environment (security reasons) such that it bears little resemblance
to what you would have if you owned a machine with SunOS installed?

Or are you talking about a smaller ISP, perhaps one that doesn't sell
any "unlimited accounts for $19.95"?

I don't know what the actual price difference is, but I guess that
setting up a shell server on a Sun with SunOS is *far* less expensive
than setting one up on a DEC Alpha with Dec Unix.  Why pay more, will
the shell account users pay a significantly higher monthly fee *just*
to be on Dec Unix machines?  This is why you *can* rent a Corvette,
but not the Lamborghini.  People are only willing to pay so much,
after that the market evaporates because the price you have to charge
causes the customers to say "isn't worth it" and you can't afford to
offer the service.
