In article <6a36rs$2u$1@svr-c-01.core.theplanet.net>, <@prestel.co.uk> writes:
>
>I would like some information on the old Alpha series
>the type of servers available in the UK between
>1985 to 1990 could anyone help?

This is easy.  Since asked I'll assume that you're thinking of
systems based on the Alpha CPU chip...

There are none.  Using my old Systems and Options catalogs as
guide, the first Alpha systems were released between April-June
1991 and November 1992, probably late-middle 1992.

You could be thinking of systems based on the MIPS CPU chips
used in our DECsystem and DECstation line.  While RISC, these
were certainly Alpha based systems.  The system in the line
were:

DECstation 3100 - The first one, a workstation using a 16 Mhz
R2000A supporting a maximum of 24 MB of memory.  It had a
built-in SCSI adapter and LANCE based Ethernet adapter.  The
graphics subsystem was your choice of color or monochrome
frame buffer.  By removing the frame buffer, plugging a terminal
into one of the serial ports and adding a loop back for the
mouse, you turned it into a server.

DECstation 2100 - The same basic system as the DS3100, but with
a slower CPU and lower price.

DECstation 5000/200 - The first of the TURBOchannel based systems,
this used the R3000 series CPU at 25 Mhz.  The maximum memory was
eventually 480 MB using a 32 MB memory module.  It had three
TURBOchannel slots for option expansion.  At least one of these
was used for the graphics adapter on the workstation versions.
The base system included a SCSI adapter and LANCE based Ethernet.

The TURBOchannel was a 32 bit bus using a clock speed of 25 Mhz.
Some later models used a half-speed TURBOchannel.  Among the
options available with ULTRIX were a SCSI adapter (single channel
Slow, Narrow, Single-ended), Ethernet adapter, Fiber and Copper
FDDI and some 3rd party devices.  There was even a TURBOchannel
based CI adapter.

DECsystem 5400 - This used a variant of the S-box used for the
MicroVAX 3000 family.  I don't recall if the CPU/Memory subsystem
included an I/O module.  If not all the I/O was through the
Q-bus.  I heard of a board-set upgrade being considered to
convert suitable MicroVAX systems, but I never saw one.  I
suspect this was one of the first systems that included a
VAX CPU of some flavor for running low-level diagnostics.  This
saved having to write all the diagnostics.

DECsystem 5800 - This used the VAX 6000 series cabinet.  The CPU
was two XMI boards and also included some flavor of VAX for running
the diagnostics.  It allow multiple CPUs, but at 2 slots per CPU
quickly used up the available XMI slots.

DECsystem 5500 - The 2nd generation mid-sized system, this used
the cabinet similar to the VAX 4000 family.  It had an integrated
I/O module which included SCSI, DSSI, Ethernet and was rumored to
include a VME bus or some way to connect one.  For additional
expansion it had Q-bus slots.  This was one of the first systems
to offer Prestoserve NVRAM.

DECsystem 5100 - I never actually saw one of these, but it was
in a table-top enclosure very similar to that of the later
MicroVAX 3100 family.  Expansion cabability was limited as
I recall, but it also either included or had the option of
a Prestoserve NVRAM.

DECstation 5000/100 - A family of systems using a mother-board
daughter-card design for the CPU.  CPUs running at 20, 25 and
33 Mhz were available.  Eventually an R4000 family CPU was
available.  This system used the half-speed TURBOchannel.  The
base system included the usual built-in SCSI, Ethernet and
serial interface.  This was the first of the DECstation family
to have a SCSI adapter supporting DMA because it cost less than
the shared-memory version of previous systems.  It had three
TURBOchannel slots.  This used the same memory as the DECstation
3100, but also support a high density memory module, allowing
upto 128 MB of memory.

DECstation 5000/240 and 5900 - This was also a mother-board/
daugher-card design using a 40 Mhz R3000 family CPU.  An R4000
family CPU was eventually offered.  It used the same memory as
the 5000/200.  It had a full speed TURBOchannel and its SCSI
adapter also supported DMA.

The DECsystem 5900 used a /240 enclosure for its CPU, but was in
a larger cabinet that supported a number of I/O expansion drawers.
A Prestoserve NVRAM board was either part of the base system or
an option. Some versions of system were sold with the CITCA TURBO-
channel CI adapter.  I believe the CITCA based system included the
TURBOchannel extender that allowed physically large devices to
need only one system TURBOchannel slots.  The CITCA was three
slots wide, so this allowed the remaining two lots to be used
for something else.

The I/O drawers were wired to support two SCSI busses and room for
four or so half-height 5.25" form factor device and five or six
full height 5.25" devices.  Empty, the drawer is pretty heavy.

DECstation 5000/xx - This was the Personal DECstation.  I think
it included a built-in graphics adapter and offered only one or
two additional TURBOchannel slots.  The memory configuration was
also limited, but it was a relatively inexpensive system.  I
think it also used the same CPU daughter cards as the Model 100
family.

Well, as best as I can recollect that's all of them.

