:In article <34C65155.1D31B3E7@wesmed.com>,
:John Thomas  <jthomas@wesmed.com> wrote:
:>Matt,
:>    in you experience, how many drives can you put on a  channel before
:>the
:>performance degrades? I have heard that when you put more than 6 drives
:>on a
:>channel, the performance goes away.
:>
:>John

    Assuming you aren't doing bandwidth-intensive work the performance
    will degrade as you run up against the transaction limit of the SCSI 
    controller.

    An adaptec or ncr pci controller can do around 600 complex
    transactions per second across multiple targets.  A typical hard drive 
    will hit its seek limit, assuming semi-random operations, at around 
    100 transactions/sec.  Thus you can typically put five or six drives
    on a SCSI bus without serious performance degredation.

    Note: one 'transaction' is not equivalent to one sector.  One transaction
    can transfer many sectors.   An Ultra-Wide SCSI bus has a bandwidth of
    40 MBytes/sec, but a typical semi-random disk load doesn't come close to
    saturating it from a bandwidth point of view.

    Also note: when doing small linear accesses, both the controllers and 
    the drives can do better then 7000 transactions/sec.  This is not 
    a realistic statistic for a multi-target semi-random-access system,
    however.  Special-purpose systems may have different requirements, the
    above description refers to disk subsystems used by general purpose 
    servers.

