In article <gurman-1901982356290001@goodgulf.nascom.nasa.gov>, gurman@ari.net (Joseph B. Gurman) writes:
>
>P.S. I thought I should mention that some colleagues in California
>recently decided not to order more Digital workstations (of any flavor)
>after they found out Digital failed to support the flavor of ATM on which
>their gaggle of SGI O2's run. They are considering instead simply buying
>fast Intel boxes and running Linuxon them --- somebody evidently writes an
>acceptable (and free) ATM driver. I realize the potentially catastrophic
>costs such a decision could entail, compared to a supported OS and layered
>software, but any vendor of >$5K workstations had better realize that
>cost-driven customers are going to be looking at this alternative.

Exactly.  I refer you to the story of the folks who rendered most of the
scenes for the movie Titanic, at:

  http://www.ssc.com/lj/issue46/2494.html

It's a very interesting article.  For instance, for this project they 
purchased 160 433Mhz Alphas from Carrera, and loaded 105 with Linux, 55
with WNT.  From Digital's business perspective this hardly represents a
success story.  They made whatever they made selling the hardware to
Carrera (anybody want to guess? maybe $1k/box? does Carrera buy
processors from Samsung or Mitsubishi?), but they didn't make whatever
extra they would have made selling their own workstations, or their own
software.  Microsoft made around $350 * 55 on WNT sales, and Red Hat made
$50 * 105. (I just called Red Hat, and the price for the Alpha version is
the same as for Intel.)  Digital's vaunted "premium" Unix was completely
blown away by Linux.  Probably this was primarily due to the $2000 * 105
difference in cost, although in the article the company also cited
technical reasons for preferring Linux.  Which compiler did they use?  It
didn't say.  Since they apparently did not put DU on a single machine, it
was probably gcc, which is demonstrably slower than DEC C.  Let's see how
big that efficiency difference would have to be to make up for the cost of
DU. 

 105 * 5000 = $ 525000  (approximate total cost of workstations)

 525000 = 7000 * X      (X is number of DU workstations they could have had,
                         assuming DU costs $2000 per machine)
 75 = X

 105/75 = 1.4

By not buying DU they were able to purchase 40% more workstations, and
40% more compute power than they would have had with gcc.  (I don't know if
DEC C would have been 40% faster than gcc on their code, which might have 
tilted the balance the other way.)

Interestingly, they did purchase WNT (instead of Linux) for 55 of these
machines, which most likely indicates that the availability of certain key
pieces of software for that platform drove those sales. 

Anyway, this case certainly fits the idea that commercial Unixes are going 
to be in big trouble on workstations, not just against WNT, but also 
against Linux.  It also fits the model that for many jobs Linux beats out 
WNT (they *could* have put WNT on everything.)

Regards,

