Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: kelleyg@mail.dec.com
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Digital Equipment
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


The system is upgradeable in the following way:

1. You can upgrade the amount of RAM to 128MB (althought it is almost impossible
to find 64MB SIMMs)
2. You can upgrade the amount of DISK storage by adding additional hard drives (up
to the maximum allowed at the time - 2.0GB per drive - this is the Windows95
limitation 2GB partition)
3. You can upgrade the CPU to 133Mhz (at the time that the Starion 300i was
designed, the Pentium 133Mhz was the fastest Intel Pentium available and MMX was a
wet dream in some designers mind).
4. The desktop case that the 300i uses was modified from a Digital design to a
more open industry accepted form factor called Western Digital LPx. The LPx form
factor was the most widely used at the time for OEMs and systems integrators. The
LPx form factor specified the size of the MLB, the location of the riser card, the
placement of the IO connectors, and the placement of the standoffs and the screw
holes on the MLB. You should be able to find LPx form factor MLBs.
5. You can upgrade the system by installing additional peripherals such as faster
modems, better sound cards, better video cards, network adapters, video capture
cards, etc., etc., etc.

At the time that the Starion 300i was designed and marketed in the Retail market,
it offered one of the best performing systems at its price as compared to the
other brands offered in the same market (ie. Compaq, Packard Bell, Acer, IBM).
After 3 years, its somewhat out of date. It also was not designed to run with
technology that was not available at that time.

regards,
glen kelley
digital

