    Interesting.  Keep in mind that the bottlenecks you are going to hit
    are going to occur in the I/O and memory subsystems - i.e. the disk 
    drives will max out long before the cpu even comes close to saturation,
    and each service you run will require a certain amount of memory to
    operate smoothly.

    One of the reasons why FreeBSD on PPro 200's work out so well for us is
    precisely the fact that the overall limiting factor in the system is
    disk seeking.  The drive's transactional capability maxes-out long before
    the SCSI controller's or the cpu's transactional capability and means,
    roughly, that you can put five or six drives on a single ultra-wide SCSI 
    bus without effecting per-drive performance.

