>Getting back to computers, even proud Sun has given in and accepted
>PCI bus. How long before all Sun workstations take basically PC
>graphics cards? AGP for SPARCs? How about I2O? Are they going to
>ignore DDR-SDRAM, RAMBUS?

Don't believe because Sun has subscribed to PCI they will take
on every PC-style technology. PCI is faster than SBus, I heard,
and Sun would not have been able to establish their own IO bus
again. At the time the SBus was first implemented (1987? 1988?)
the alternatives were Nubus or MCA. Maybe EISA. Neither of these
could match SBus speeds.

Now, it's leapfrog time again. A non-proprietary bus is taking
over again (before SBus, we had Multibus and VMEbus, neither
a proprietary bus). Maybe the same thing will happen again
in three years, who knows?

BTW, while I do not like the fact that the new low end Sun machines
have EIDE disks, I understand the reason behind this. EIDE has
a very attractive price for almost the same capabilities as SCSI.
(It needs shorter cables, so can't have external drives, it can
only do disks of various sorts, the address space is very limited.)

>... The move to services (harking back to
>my original point) is a positive thing for DEC which Casper was
>denying. ...

I understood he wanted to point out why DEC is selling less
computers even though their revenue is bigger than Sun's.

>Right, _so far_ Sun is looking good because it has preserved it's
>loyal customer base. However it will have to spend a lot of money to
>replace the current big servers it sells in its product lineup,
>especially the Starfire which is based on Cray technology. UltraSPARC
>will need a lot of investment to even standstill in relation to
>competing architectures. What did MPR call SPARC? The slowest high-end
>chip? A friend of mine at SGI said SPARC "is nicely balanced, it does
>everything slowly". And meanwhile Microsoft, Compaq et al have their
>tanks firmly parked on the workstation lawn and are looking rather
>meaningfully at the server middle-ground.

The Starfire is based on *Cray Business Systems* technology. That
part used to be FPS. They built SPARC machines based on a bipolar
version of the SPARC chip (BIT?). When FPS folded, Sun enticed
Cray to buy a good part of the assets. Sun contributed the Dragon
technology (later known as E2000) to allow the new Cray division
to build a bigger version of it, using FPS and Cray Know How
in the big machine area. The result was the 6400.

Later, Cray Business Systems probably got/bought UltraSPARC
server technology and again built a bigger version, with mainframe
style mounting, etc. When Cray sold out to SGI, Sun had little
chioce but to buy the CBS part from SGI, and SGI had little
choice but to sell the bastard child.

I would not call this "Cray technology". It's a blend of Sun,
FPS and Cray technology. With FPS I mean mostly the work of
the people who work in Beaverton, under the names of FPS,
Cray, and Sun.

As for SPARC being slow, it looks like it's getting faster, when
I see the projected speed of the UltraSPARC III. Looking at SPEC
numbers, the gap seems to be closing. With TPC figures, the big
bandwidth and low latency of the UltraSPARC memory interface makes
them faster than most, plus the fact that they can put more CPUs
in a single box, on a single bus.

>There are no grounds for smugness on the part of Sun in my view.

I support this. Sun has to be running to keep up. So far they are
running quite fast, though.

>I work for a firm that has X thousand sparcstations in our building
