From: "Mark Jordan" <mcj2412@ggr.co.uk>
Subject: Re: VMS system hangs while booting.
Date: 1998/01/21
Message-ID: <01bd2652$e9421dc0$08e4b893@ukd07312>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 317962363
References: <884946096.523028@terra.ultra.net.au>
Organization: Glaxo Wellcome
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


If you're running DECnet-OSI you may be suffering from the DNS$ADVER accvio
problem.  The system starts DNS$ADVER and then waits a while for it to be
up and running so it can communicate with it.  If the process dies then the
startup waits forever, or until you halt it.

Try rebooting minimum and checking accounting for a process accvio'ing at
about the right time, status should be 0000000C.  You may also find the
file sys$manager:dns$adver_error.log contains messages about resizing the
cache.  If this does look like the answer then backup and delete the files
DNS$CACHE.* from sys$system.  Shutdown and reboot normally.  New cache
files should be created.

Additionally review the OSI ECO level you're at.

This problem often occurs when DNS$ADVER thinks the amount of memory in the
box has changed.  You haven't got some memory going bad by any chance?

Mark.




