Wishing for affordable VMS is fine and dandy. But please wake up and
smell the coffee: DIGITAL DOES NOT WANT CHEAP VMS. It might dilute from
their all too important revenues from their few remaining large
customers.

Digital is NOT a large volume low margin operation , it is a low volume
high margin operation. We may not like it, but such is the case.

Please face the fact that the new guy at Digital has been around long
enough that he is no longer a "new guy". He has had plenty of time
to shape and re-shape Digital to the way it will be in the next
foreseable future and the lack of any action to make VMS price
competitive in all these years should be taken as a corporate policy. It
is no longer the case of "when Palmer gets around to it, he will fix
it". If Palmer hasn't gotten around to fixing something by now, we MUST
accept the fact that it is because PALMER sees no reason to change
something which is , in his opinion, not broken and remains corporate
policy.


Most of use would LOVE to see VMS and DIGITAL succeed in this world. We
would love to see small Digital battle the Microsoft Golliath and win.
Digital certaintly has the quality goods which are by far superior to
MS's.
But it won't happen. Apple and Oracle have more guts than Digital has.

And lets not forget that Digital is bound and tied by its agreements 
with MS. The only option DEC has is to keep a low profile and not make
too much noise. Making Bill Gates angry would have devastating effects
on Digital which has bet so many of its eggs on Bill Gates.

So, let Digital do business as it wishes, and if Digital does not offer
products which are competitive, go elsewhere.

If we had not been so loyal to Digital during all those years where
Palmer was trying to hide all DEC products such as VMS, DEC sales would
have dropped so much that Palmer would have been ousted and maybe
Digital would be much better today. Palmer survived because of our
loyalty and he , in fact, abused it at our own expense to play with his
pet projects of sucking up to Bill Gates.

Had DEC's VMS customer base not been so loyal, Digital would have been
forced to make VMS price competitive right from the start. What our
loyalty did is allow Digital to toy with other systems while we were
continuing to blindly pay the big bills. And once DEC engaged itself
with others such as Bill Gates, it could no longer go back and become
the independant company it used to be.

Palmer has not bleed Digital enough to open Wall Street's eyes. If we
all lost our loyalty and went elsewhere, it might hurt DEC enough that
Palmer would be ousted and someone else could get in and rebuild
Digital.

Sorry to sound so negative. But dreaming doesn't do much and I see 
way too much dreaming here. We all know VMS *could* be great. We 
all know that Digital *could* be great, but why raise hopes when 
it is very unlikely things would change ?

All non-MS systems are threathened. But Digital's inability to keep VMS
prices in check, or arguably, Digital's delay in making VMS follow
price trends (if you are optimist) are making it next to impossible 
for VMS to really realise its potential.

Digital, a division of Microsoft Inc.



