NT , because of its strong assocation with Windows, is considered "free"
because Windows is basically free. (Perception counts, reality doesn't)

By default, folks go NT.

But because LINUX is also "free", it costs next to nothing for a company
to run a pilot with LINUX and when/if it works out, it can build on it.

The lack of cheap VMS prevents this incursion into this growth market
in which Digital has little presence. Only those with big budgets
and clearly identified needs for VMS go VMS.

BUT at least VMS has a diffenciator. It is different, has abilities
such as clustering which others do not have.

DEC UNIX on the other hand does not have much to differentiate against
LINUX, except the price. Perhaps DEC would be better off dropping its
own Unix and adopting LINUX big time and start to offer true support for
those who need it.

OS2 failed because it did not really offer anything that different from
Windows (although it was more reliable). 

How big a niche VMS gets depends on how big a niche DEC wants this
better quality product to get.



