Message-ID: <1998Jan19.022852.1@eisner>
X-Deja-AN: 317282567
X-Nntp-Posting-Host: eisner.decus.org
X-Trace: news.decus.org 885194949 29708 YOUNG_R [192.67.173.2]
Organization: DECUServe
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.alpha



Reading the Press Release reporting earnings at www.digital.com we
find the following snippet:

"This agreement [Intel-Alpha] is very positive for both companies, customers
and especially for our Alpha technology," Palmer said. "It reinforces and
supports our commitment to Alpha. We now can focus our resources on the design
of Alpha microprocessors and systems while benefiting from Intel's
state-of-the-art manufacturing process technology. We are in an excellent
position to maintain and enhance Alpha�s performance advantage over all other
architectures far into the future."

While I agree that what Mr. Palmer says is true, I am sad to report that I 
fully believe the Alpha Message has been ineffectively communicated  and 
seriously twisted as we shall see.

Is the mis-communicated Alpha Message a recent phenomena?  There is strong
evidence to suggest it is a long-standing problem.  Writing in a September 1994
ComputerWorld, Charles Babcock pens:

		"Alpha may not win, but it's the best"

His insightful close:

"Despite its many strengths, Alpha may be a case of superior technology
that does not win out. It is carrying the millstone of Digital's
business problems as it tries to win new adherents. But win, lose or
draw, the principles around which Alpha was built will continue to
influence computer design for years to come.  [Yeah, like Pentium Pro...
zing!!!]

Digital needs a no-nonsense display of good business judgment. If it were
purely a technical matter, Alpha would stand tall."

Pardon me if this bounces all over and lacks flow.  It isn't an official
white-paper and after all , this is Usenet.  I don't get paid for this and so
it is up to you the reader to take it for what it is worth.  With that, look at
what Michael Slater of MPR fame has to add� in:

http://www.mdronline.com/slater/perspective/1115sp.html

"Digital executives claim that the company's plans for Alpha remain unchanged,
and that they still hope to drive it into the mainstream PC market (see MPR
11/17/97, p. 1)--but this just doesn't seem realistic. Alpha has made little
headway against x86, and it will have a lot more difficulty making headway
against x86 and IA-64. The publicity surrounding the Intel/Digital deal has
been damaging, and Digital's Alpha customers must be very skeptical. Digital's
system business will find itself increasingly torn between Alpha and IA-64."

Digital has very skeptical Alpha customers,� "Alpha is at best, going
to be around for a short while.  Alpha is a risk proposition."  

But let us take a look at a recent piece that his clone penned. I made
reference to this recently here in Usenetland.

Linley Gwennap writes in:

http://www.mdronline.com/report/editorials/edit11_16.html

"While the uncertainty surrounding Digital's Alpha plans provides an
uncomfortable backdrop for these efforts to push Alpha into the PC market, it
isn't likely to have a tangible downside. The next-generation 21264 design is
nearly ready for production, and Digital is working with Samsung on a PC
derivative of that processor. With process shrinks to 0.25- and 0.18-micron
CMOS, the 21264 core should satisfy the needs of the PC market for years to
come. Even if Digital decides it doesn't need the 21364, it could develop that
chip under contract for Samsung and Mitsubishi."

Again� we see a pattern here:

"Uncertainty...�i.e.  clouded future.  Very much in doubt."

What is most troubling however is this finishing sentence because it is what
other wanna-be analysts and writers that are digging for background tee-off on:

"Even if Digital decides it doesn't need the 21364, it could develop that chip
under contract for Samsung and Mitsubishi."

"Even if" doesn't take much for "even if" to become the following:

"It looks very much like Digital is today weighing the future of Alpha to
decide whether or not it should continue development.  Digital'�s next
generation processor seems to be in doubt at this time."

You get the idea.

But why not a bit of their own medicine?  Why can'�t Digital break out of the
mold of going on the offensive?  Why should everyone (or most everyone it
seems) continue to marginalize Digital?  How can Digital get writers to become
marketers for Alpha as many are marketing Intel even as we breath? Case in
point.  Return to the Slater piece above and you will find:

http://www.mdronline.com/slater/perspective/1115sp.html

"RISC-based systems won't disappear instantly. The RISC systems have unique
software and customer bases that will take time to migrate. In addition, it
probably won't be until the second-generation IA-64 processor ships in 2001
that the architecture will really shine. It will take time for the compilers
and operating systems to mature, as well as for applications to get ported. But
eventually, unless there turns out to be a fundamental flaw in the IA-64
approach, the architecture is likely to develop the same kind of momentum that
drove the x86 architecture to dominance."

You see, RISC won'�t die immediately.  But the second generation of Merced will
really prove IA-64.  Here is a situation where futures are being sold 3 or more
years in advance.  Why not let Michael know more about Arana?  I mean it is
more than a little ridiculous that IA-64 futures can be trotted about with no
mention of Alpha futures.  Alpha does have a future doesn't it?

But is Alpha dead?  Is it hopeless?  What does a PCWeek writer think?

Writing in:

http://www.zdnet.com/pcweek/news/1027/31ealpha.html

"Having been touted as Digital's beacon to the future for the last five years,
the Alpha processor will take a backseat to Intel's IA-64 architecture and the
forthcoming 64-bit chip, code-named Merced, that Intel is developing with
Hewlett-Packard Co."

And from that same article:

"A Digital official said the company is on track to deliver the next-generation
Alpha 21264 in the first half of 1998, and an executive-level Digital insider
said Intel will guarantee Alpha production for 10 years.

However, as part of the settlement, Digital becomes Intel's only customer for
Alpha, calling into question the future of the Alpha clone business and such
companies as Enorex Microsystems Inc. and DeskStation Technology Inc."

Ms. DiCarlo shows that the Alpha clone market is on shaky ground.  Why?
Well Alpha is on shaky ground.  Where is Samsung and Mitsubishi?

What prompted me to pen all this was this recent article in Red Herring:

http://www.redherring.com/mag/issue50/reload.html

That piece which I will quote shortly was *apparently* a knee-jerk
reaction following the first in-depth piece Red Herring wrote covering
Digital:

http://www.redherring.com/mag/issue49/being.html 

From issue50:

"But in the long term, Intel wins much, much more. Alpha has been one of the
few chips that kept Intel "honest," so to speak. By taking over production of
the Alpha chips and paying Digital to develop Merced-based systems, Intel has
effectively put the boot into its last source of competition, other than Sun,
for the PC and high-end-system markets. As in its relationship with Microsoft,
Digital is partnering with a company that means to put it out of business.
Moreover, Digital's StrongARM license could give Intel a quick entry into the
market for chips for nonstandard appliances."

Alpha is going away!!! "Alpha gets the boot!" . . . I thought to myself:  
"it isn't getting any better, in fact it is getting MUCH worse."

Is all that I have quoted above in all the articles true?  Is some of it true?
Is there a plan afoot to counter-act some of this drivel?  The real danger in
all of this is a confused customer base.  "Shall I purchase an Alpha machine? 
Will Alpha even be around in 2 years?  My Alpha investment will be worthless in
2 years, etc."  Is this Digital's fault?  Partly.  Why?  Little is being done
to counter-act such nonsense.  What you see above is just part of what I have
found.  I have on several occasions exchanged e-mail with writers in hopes of
straightening their thinking.  Writers don'�t understand 64-bit computing, they
don'�t understand Alpha, they don'�t understand Digital.

What can be done?  A few suggestions:

1)  A real-time campaign.

		When Digital announces a press release/product, several dozen
		important newsprint organizations need to see a clear message 
		in real-time so there isn't any confusion.  Phoned in, and 
		e-mailed in, and faxed in, and followed up days later.  Maybe
		this is already taking place.  But if it is, how can so many
		be getting it wrong?  Am I missing something here?  But who
		to phone/fax/e-mail?  See 3) below.

2)  Take some chances.

		The aloof laid back New Englander approach may make for
		a polite conservative company but it isn't winning mind-share.
		Mind-share matters muchly.  How about a "line in the sand",
		"put up or shut-up" campaign?  Digital challenges anyone to 
		outperform it ON December 31, 1998.  Digital is willing to
		donate $5 million to charity if anyone can come within 1/2
		the performance of their high-end config running the
		TCP-x benchmark.  A shoot-out, fully audited, that
		takes place at the stroke of midnight, etc.  Any takers
		out there?  I didn't think so.  Chickens.  Little risk
		in a campaign like this and would give substance to the
		fact that AlphaServers ARE high-end and others are pretenders.

3)  Educate/Cajole the Writers 

		When different groups attempt to get bills passed, they lobby 
		Congress.  It is called influence peddling.  I suggested this
		before.  Treat them to lunch.  Let them in on cool information.
		Make friends.  It has been my experience that several of these
		folks are ill-informed.  However, attacking them just doesn't
		work (trust me).

I hope this isn't too over the top but I get frustrated when I see the finest
in the world once again misinterpreted/debunked.  Paraphrasing Babcock from
above:  "Digital needs a good no-nonsense display of Marketing/PR.  If it were
a purely technical matter, Alpha wouldn'�t be so marginalized."

				Rob




