David Hull <david_hull@notes.pw.com> writes:

>DEC did $13.1 billion in business last year (not sure how much of that may or may
>not have been in China), compared to Sun's $8.6 billion.  And, they rank #1 in
>market share in high-end SMP decision support systems, according to IDC, and #1 in
>low-end UNIX servers, according to Dataquest.  If you can't find any Alphas, it's
>because you're either blind, or hanging out at Sun.



Well, with the latest numbers in, we can see that Sun does $9.2 billion/yr
and DEC $13.3 billion/yr.  (Funny, the stock market seems to think Sun is
worth much more at $16B against DEC's $6B)

On closer examiniation, however, we find that 45% of Digitals revenue
is "service" as it is very much a service oriented company
(servicing non-DEC equipment as well as their own).

So we have about $7.3 billion in DEC "sales" plus about $750m from sequent;
(which includes DEC PC sales, NT and OpenVMS; hm, no OpenVMS on Merced yet?)

No, the $9.2 Sun revenue is not all "non-service" either, but to
say that DEC is "much" bigger is simply not true.

Even the #1 position in any Unix segment market seems highly unlikely.

And Sequent is, of course, only a minor player in the Market at $750million;
Their NUMA-Q performance numbers dont' look compelling either.

