Oh come on Casper, I think your postings are normally very good, but
saying DEC isn't bigger than Sun because some unquantified amount of
its revenues are merely more services than Sun sells is a little
lame. Give us the non-services Sun revenue at the very least.

As for share price well one could make the opposite point - Why is a
16 billion dollar company like Sun only selling $9.2B when poor old
$6B DEC is selling about that much in your "real sales" or $13.3B if
you count those pesky serice revenues. What's wrong with Sun?

I'm not seriously asking that btw, merely taking issue with your
statements being wholly Sun-boosting.

You will no doubt point to the historic differences in size between
the two firms. That's fair enough, unless you believe that
SPARC/Solaris is merely Sun's turn to have VAX/VMS style proprietary
blow-out before falling to the commodisation of the computer market
which has so ravaged DEC. Unfortunately for Sun I do think this is
quite likely the case. When you make great products like VAX/VMS in
its day or SPARC/Solaris right now and the customers keep snarfing
ever-increasing quantities of the big ticket high margin big boxes,
it's not hard at all to grow lots, hire loads more people and have a
great share price. Ask DEC. The trick is keeping it all looking so
rosy. We shall see. Perhaps Sun-of-Starfire (if such a product exists)
will take Sun into the 21st Century, but you might care to wonder if
it might not also be the VAX9000 white elephant style monument to
vanity that seriously damages the company. You probably know this, but
DEC poured immense resource into a high-tech mainframe class VAX. It
won awards for its manufacturing techniques and everything. But almost
nobody bought one.

FWIW although I am an admitted DEC fan I also really want Sun to
continue to do well. In fact my livelihood sort of depends on it at
the moment.

Chris
