steve.kappel@iname.com (Steve Kappel) writes:

> 
> In article <87iurgl9ty.fsf@ix.netcom.com>,
> 	Chris Morgan <mihalis@ix.netcom.com> writes:
> >You will no doubt point to the historic differences in size between
> >the two firms. That's fair enough, unless you believe that
> >SPARC/Solaris is merely Sun's turn to have VAX/VMS style proprietary
> >blow-out before falling to the commodisation of the computer market
> 
> So now Unix and SPARC are "proprietary"?

I shouldn't have said proprietary, that was a mistake. I
apologise. However Sun does have plenty of proprietary urges just like
many other large engineering concerns. Witness them keeping all the
hot technology away from Sequent and only offering them the less
successful Solaris/X86 technology. Or the rather shoddy treatment of
Microsoft over Java - suing them and not Netscape was very very
lame. Yes I know MS behaved much, much worse but I don't expect any
better from them.

> 
> Has M$ once again consumed yet another buzzword that used to mean
> something?  I suppose only M$ makes open/non-proprietary software
> and only Intel makes open processors.  I guess the definition of
> Unix is no longer controlled by a standards body.  I guess having
> an independent processor consortium for SPARC isn't any good either.
> Let's just flip the traditional meaning of open and proprietary!!

As I said it was a mistake and I take your point about the "openness"
of the Wintel hegemony. Look I'm really sorry ok. If I just said
"single source OS plus minority processor architecture blowout" it
would be closer to what I meant, still not totally accurate. Yes you
can get SPARCs from other vendors, but basically the SPARC hardware
market is Sun (plus fab partner) and the little guys. For whatever
reason the move to UltraSPARC has severely dented Ross for example.

Perhaps I should have just said "VAX/VMS style blowout" and leave it
at that.

And of course I don't accept that Solaris is controlled by a standards
body. Solaris threads don't quite fit with Posix threads for example.

> 
> There remains a huge market for non-commodity computers.  Seems
> IBM is still selling lots of proprietary mainframes and AS/400s.

Am I supposed to respect that? Most of the mainframe market depends on
software lock-in.

> There are companies that do just fine selling non-commodity
> products.  Or are we all driving Yugo's and I just didn't notice?
> You can do everything with a Yugo, right?

Actually many many parts of the entire world auto market have been
commoditised. How else could it be that at times the UK export of
parts to VW manufacturing plants in Europe has exceeded the UK import
of VW cars by value. How else could Jaguars use Mercedes gearboxes?
How else could the lowliest Ford have a paintjob every bit as good as
a BMW? You drive a car with no commodity parts? My sympathies if that
is the case.

Getting back to computers, even proud Sun has given in and accepted
PCI bus. How long before all Sun workstations take basically PC
graphics cards? AGP for SPARCs? How about I2O? Are they going to
ignore DDR-SDRAM, RAMBUS?

> 
> Maybe if DEC would have stuck with OpenVMS it would have had a
> chance.  Or switched all cylinders to Unix at the right time.
> This begging people to buy *anything* - OpenVMS, Digital Unix, or
> NT - isn't helping them at all.

Probably true. As I was trying to point out, I am basically saying DEC
was very big, producing some great products, had a good share price
etc, but got hit amidships by the Wintel tidal wave. Could some of
that have been because of corporate arrogance and engineering pride?
Some armchair pundits (e.g. me) think there's some chance Sun is just
the "before" to DEC's "after". The move to services (harking back to
my original point) is a positive thing for DEC which Casper was
denying. But my use of proprietary has you thinking I'm some Wintel
bigot when nothing could be further from the truth.

> So how do you explain Sun's success with the Starfire?  Maybe being
> in the right place at the right time with the right product with
> the right strategy has something to do with it?  Seems to me Sun has
> a long history of doing things this way.  DEC *used* to be like that

Right, _so far_ Sun is looking good because it has preserved it's
loyal customer base. However it will have to spend a lot of money to
replace the current big servers it sells in its product lineup,
especially the Starfire which is based on Cray technology. UltraSPARC
will need a lot of investment to even standstill in relation to
competing architectures. What did MPR call SPARC? The slowest high-end
chip? A friend of mine at SGI said SPARC "is nicely balanced, it does
everything slowly". And meanwhile Microsoft, Compaq et al have their
tanks firmly parked on the workstation lawn and are looking rather
meaningfully at the server middle-ground.

There are no grounds for smugness on the part of Sun in my view.

I work for a firm that has X thousand sparcstations in our building
(X>5). Chances are they will get replaced by WindowsNT
workstations. This will be a grave mistake in my view but they wont be
asking me, there's almost no discussion. Sun gouged us so badly and
for so long with SunOS hardware pricing that Solaris is going to get
skipped (my prediction). At the same time we were being charged well
over 10k for sparc20s we were buying Pentium Pro200s which were
laughably superior in raw power for a third the price. Intel took CISC
to 200MHz in volume before Sun managed it with RISC. I know that
WindowsNT soon soaked up all the difference and more but that's not
the point. There will be some Sun servers still in the data centers to
run the old apps, and of course PC X-Servers to display them,
occasionally we might even spend the odd 100k on some replacement
hardware, but Suns gravy train in my office is looking exceptionally
shaky. How will they feel joining IBM and DEC in the backroom legacy
vendor retirement home? Let's hope they prove me wrong.

Chris
