The fact is, hundreds of thousands or millions of these drives were
manufactured, mostly being installed in high load servers.  Of all
tape drives, only the Colorado Jumbo 250 and 350 were manufactured in
greater numbers.

The 250 and 350 were (and still are) mainly used for personal
computers, while the 5525 was (and still is) mostly used in servers
and business computers, UNIX boxes, and Sun and Apollo systems and
mainframes.

Subjected to frequent, daily use, usually in multiple tape sessions,
over the years time and heat takes its toll, and any component subject
to such conditions and heavy use will eventually develop problems, or
die altogether.

Thanks to Murphy's Law, one usually doesn't find out the problem
exists until they need to restore a backup dues to a system crash.  In
cases where the problem gets noticed during a backup session, a strong
purchasing consideration is the existence of a large tape library of
DC6525 tapes that would be made useless if a new tape drive system
were to be installed.  Many times, because of this, the decision is
made to replace the problem tape drive with an exact replacement,
rather than moving up to a newer tape drive.

For server applications that perform daily backups, the bad reputation
of DAT is spread all over USENET.  While the DAT tape drives are nice,
the tapes themselves do not last, and have to be replaced far more
often than a QIC tape; the QIC tape can be reused a far greater number
of times than a DAT tape, and DAT tapes are more expensive.  This is
another consideration that leads many to stick with their existing QIC
