Now if you were referring to the drive's internal BIOS software, and
pointing out that there are three different versions of that, it would
only show your ignorance.  There are no significant differences, in
different BIOS versions except as regards data throughput and
efficiency improvements.  The SCSI controller and ASPI driver are
never aware of any differences.  All versions respond identically to
the same SCSI or ASPI commands.  Those with improved capabilities over
prevsious versions might support advanced SCSI or ASPI commands that
the previous versions did not.  If your backup software was
incompatible with the different drives, it was a programming error.
You should know this, if you are as knowledgable as you claim to be.

Intelligent and responsible ASPI / SCSI interface programming requires
a thourough interrogation of the tape drive in order to determine it's
capabilities and characteristics (the SCSI MODE SENSE command, I'm
sure that you in your great and vast experience must surely be
familiar with it) before performing any data I/O.

Given your supposed great and vast knowledge and experience of SCSI
