seem to have difficulty with because it interferes with your crusade.

The BIOS revisions in a tape drive are no more outwardly apparent to
the controller card than they are in a hard drive to it's controller.

The Western Digital Caviar 420 meg hard drive.  There were a fair
number of BIOS software revisions, and at least two circuit board
revisions.  Some had 256K cache, others had 512K.  But to the
controller card, all the drives look the same, whether they report
themselves upon interrogation to the controller card as WDAC2420AF or
WDAC2420AH.

To the PC BIOS, they all autodetect the same way, and the only
differences in operation are improved throughput speed in the later
revisions.  You obviously lack an understanding of the reasons drive
manufacturers upgrade their BIOS on a regular basis.  BIOS version
upgrades are a good thing!  I hate to be the one to have to tell you
all that, but someone obviously has to.

