In browsing dejanews to witness some of J.T. Blocksom's 350+ posts,
gatering material to strike back after he attempted to flame me
without due cause, justification, or regard to the facts, I found some
interesting insights.

To be fair:

It does actually appear that he usually attempts to do a good job in
his self appointed role as "USENET cop".  For example:

>On 1997-02-25 gowden@asylumbbs.com said:
> --> I have an old 386sx-33 for sale. If anybody is interested in this
> --> for any reason (i.e. use in a computer, spare parts, backup,
> --> research, etc.) just email me.. I was thinking of selling it for
> --> around $50 (INCLUDING S&H) Email me at:

JT replies:

>Good luck!

>Any number of quite usable (if hardly SOTA) '486DX boards/CPUs can be 
>had for less than that.  Check the back posts on this NG -- you'll see 
>several of them advertised.  Try for $15-20 (incl. S/H, which shouldn't 
>run you more than about $5) -- but take $10 if you can get it.  The 
>thing is essentially worthless.

>-- Jay T. Blocksom
>   Appropriate Technology, Inc.
>   76657.3375@!compuserve.com

Even last February, you would have to be a chump to fork over $50 for
a 386DX40 motherboard, let alone a SX-33 board.

And there are many other occurances where some fool or ignorant person
tries to sell something for 3 or 4 times its real market value, where
JT is seen to jump and stoutly defend the USENET consumer, like a
regular knight in shining armor, showing us the true path to the holy
grail, helping us all avoid the pitfalls of the trail along the way..

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Other times JT's true intent isn't so clear.  Notice the date of the
next post.  2 months after this date I sold a used Western Digital
WDAC2250 250 meg IDE drive for $26 on Auctionweb, and I had seen the
going price there and on USENET ranging from $25 to $35.  It appears
his intention might be to get the drive for $10 less than it's going
market value.  I'll admit, the $80 asking price for the drive below is
totally and completely outrageous, but nevertheless...

>On 1997-04-22 cwaters@iu.net (Charles Waters) said:
> CW> I have a Quantum LPS 270AT hard drive for sale. Barely used. This
> CW> is a 270 meg IDE hard drive.
> CW> $80.00 + shipping. Will ship via USPS COD.

>Get real!

>Brand new drives are selling for _under_ $0.10/MB.  Why would anyone pay 
>almost $0.30/MB for a several-year-old *USED* drive?!?

>Try asking $25-30 -- but be ready to take $15-20, if you can get it.

Be ready to take $15-$20?  (if you are a chump, he night have added)

>-- Jay T. Blocksom
>   Appropriate Technology, Inc.
>   76657.3375@!CompuServe.COM  (<--- Remove the "!")

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Now here, at a date when it would still truly be a rare opportunity to
buy a 430 meg drive for $30, he claims the price is a little high.
The average going price at the time was much closer to $50.  This is
regardless of what you could buy new for $100 at the time.

Of course, it's still not very obvious yet, but we're getting slightly
closer...

>On 1997-10-25 "Brandon Vogel" <bvogel@commtech.net> said:
> BV> Newsgroups: misc.forsale.computers.storage
> BV>
