This has no connection with any kind of reality.  The seller would
have been lucky to get $440.  Selling the drives singly, at the time
there was no question he would have gotten $100 each. 2/28/97. was the
date, remember...

For someone claiming to be knowledgeable in such matters, how the
going price of around $100 for that size drive could have escaped JT
to the point  where he believed it should be $52.50 each is unknown.

If I would have found that sucker JT selling 540s at that price back
then, I surely would have bought all the drives he could sell me.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Now here, he tries telling someone who is asking a compleely
reasonable price, although not a once in a lifetime, unbeleivable
price, selling a 386 laptop, that their laptop is only worth $25...

Now us normal people might have attempted to haggle the seller down to
$200, in the hope of getting an excellent deal, where all one had to
do would be to upgrade the hard drive.  It's now almost ten months
after the post date, and we would still be lucky to get the laptop
offered for $200.  We realize that while this laptop isn't state of
the art, it isn't by any means useless, and would suit many business
and personal purposes quite admirably.

We also notice that it not being state of the art, the seller isn't
asking $700 or $1700 for it.  We realize that 286 (4M, HD) laptops are
still seen going for around $150 - $175 on a fairly regular basis, and
we also realize that if we only want to pay $200 for a laptop, then we
likely cannot run DOOM in full color on an active matrix TFT screen.
Many of us would have gladly and immediately forked over $200 for this
laptop, if we had known of its being offered.

We realize that even today, we could not expect to buy a
stolen-from-an-airport 286 laptop (mono,4M/40M HD) for $25.

$50, maybe...

Note that a rebuilt battery pack is part of the seller's deal.

