Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Reading about the appalling markup on OSes charged by DEC it suddenly
occurred to me that their prices are actually very good these
days. Running VMS or Digital Unix on an Alpha workstation these days
gives me an awesome combination of hardware and software at prices
that only a few years ago we'd pay just for a RAM upgrade to our VAX
or some extra VMS points for a new CPU.

What's happened in this time, however, is that prices have gone
through the floor. Even the Enterprise version of WindowsNT costs
peanuts compared to all other real operating systems, except of course
for the freeware Unixes which can be had either for very little fully
supported (RedHat $50) or almost nothing (CheapBytes Redhat $1.99).

So my thought now is : can any proprietary operating system survive?
What do others think. It seems to me that all the hardware in
something like a 21164PC type machine (PC parts through and through)
is fully a commodity until one tries to run VMS on it. VMS is sort of
a super-premium brand - pricing is unlike the bulk of the OS market
and only supported by very specialised customer needs. I can't imagine
how this can be supported for much longer unless cross-subsidised by
other DEC products.

Are we going to see DEC trying to soften up its Unix and VMS customers
for a WindowsNT transition?

Chris
