> rather than moving up to a newer tape drive.

  That would be nice, except that there are 2 completely different revs of
the 5525ES logic board and 3 sets of software, none of which have full com-
patibility with each other. It's better than the 5099EN 60MB QIC-36 days,
though, when everybody had their own "proprietary" extensions to the command
set for copy protection. I had an Everex controller (also sold by Wangtek as
the PC-36) with 4 different ROMs stacked up, with a rotary switch and a reset
button so I could run 4 different sets of tape software (Everex, Colorado,
Core, and Sytos).

  The thing to do if you want long-term archival of tape contents is to
restore the data and put it on some higher-density media. I set up a customer
of mine with a system that reads 60, 125/150, and 525MB QIC as well as DAT,
and he makes DATs which I burn onto CD-R for him. He's converting his records
for the last 10+ years to CD.

> For server applications that perform daily backups, the bad reputation
> of DAT is spread all over USENET.  While the DAT tape drives are nice,
