  I disagree. I've found DAT drives to be more reliable than QIC ones. I know
of several sites that run multiple tapes per day, and after a year or so I
say "when was the last time you used a cleaning tape?" and they go "what's a
cleaning tape?" and they've never had any problems.

  Also, new DAT tapes are *not* more expensive than new QIC tapes on a per-
tape basis, and *much* less expensive on a $/MB basis.

> Now the going rate to repair a 5525ES tape drive is $250 dollars.
> Thousands of network administrators who use this tape drive on a
> regular basis realize full well that this is true.

  Really? I never repaired 5525's, though I *did* personally repair over
5,000 5099Ex's and 5125/5150Ex's. I did have a flyer from some outfit that
repaired QIC drives saying "any Wangtek QIC drive fixed for $79 with this
coupon" a number of years ago. As a joke I built them a mega-dud which had
an open head, defective stepper, binding motor, defective logic, bad motor
driver, and frame that Wangtek had forgotten to add any mounting holes to,
and they "fixed" it for the $79. That was a 5525ES, by the way. Of course,
as this is now an "obsolete specialty item", repair prices have probably
gone up (to encourage users to move to a newer media format).

  If you can get your asking price, fine, but I don't think that many of
the points you raise to justify that price hold water.

