In article <eECGhwoI9GA.120@nih2naab.prod2.compuserve.com>,
Kelvin Smith  <74654.3313@CompuServe.COM> wrote:
>I'm running into difficulty trying to teach an old line printer new
>tricks, specifically running off a PC.  I have a Centronics E-series
>model III band printer (1400 lines/minute), vintage c. 1984, which
>I've been using with my RSTS system.  It's hanging off a standard
>DEC LS11 interface card (card numbered M7258), with a DB25 connector.
>I'd like to use it with an ordinary PC, either the serial or the
>parallel port, but my attempts to wire it up have so far been
>fruitless, and Genicom (who bought out Centronics' printer division)
>has no information on such old hardware.

It's been a long time, but I thought the M7258 was the LP11,
a parallel-type interface card.  The "Dataproducts interface" implemented
by this card is very close to the standard "Centronics interface",
with a few small differences.  It's possible that your current
cable has some modifications in it already to handle the
Dataproducts <-> Centronics conversion, and this is what has been
hampering your efforts so far.  Or there may have been a modification
made to the Centronics printer you're using to make it more amenable to
the LP11.  Can you examine your current printer cable, as well the
input connector on the Centronics, and try to determine if they're
completely standard or a bit modified?

>Using a breakout box, I was surprised to see that almost none of the
>lines lit the box's lights when the box was connected to the printer;
>only 22 went red, none green.  When I connect printer and PC, the
>lights as a rule stay off, or are just barely lit (depending on how
>I connect the pins on both sides).  With the several different ways
>I've tried to connect my PC's COM port to the printer, the printer
>always gives the response it gives when you try to put it online and
>the computer isn't turned on.

Well, if you've been trying to hook it up to the COM port, it's not
surprising that you've been having problems.  I don't expect that
this combination would work without a serial <-> parallel converter.

A while ago, Alan Frisbie posted some very useful information about
hooking up modernish PC-oriented printers to the LPV11, the Q-bus
equivalent of the LP11.  With some luck, his cable pinouts ought to
help you figure out what you've got.  Here's Alan's article:

In article <18282057@zl2tnm.gen.nz>, 
don@zl2tnm.gen.nz (Don Stokes) writes:

> Does anyone know what I have to do to attach a Centronics printer to an
> LPV11?  I have this dim & distant memory that the LPV has the ablity to
> do something that looks close enough to a Centronics port to support the
> olde LA180 parallel port.

       People often ask me if it is possible to connect a printer
    with the Centronics (tm) parallel interface to a DEC computer.
    Not only is it possible, but DEC used to sell them!

       Although the signals for the Data Products line printers and
    the Centronics printers are different, they are similar enough
    that by connecting one wire in the cable to +5 or to ground
    the same interface can be used with either.   The interface
    simply switches its control logic from Data Products mode to
    Centronics/LA-180 mode.   This works with the Q-Bus LPV11 and
    I believe it is also true with the Unibus LP11.   The trick
    to remember is that the DEC LA-180 uses timing and control
    signals similar to the Centronics printers.

       The DEC part number for the Centronics-compatible cable is
    70-09087.   Unfortunately, this has been an "obsolete" part
    for several years, so you may only be able to find it through
    third-party cable vendors.   If you wish to build your own,
    the following table should tell you everything you need to know.
    You might also consider taking an LA-180 cable and replacing the
    Berg connector with a Centronics-style connector.

    These pin-outs were determined by examination of the schematics,
    and not from an actual 70-09087.   If anyone has an actual
    70-09087 cable, I would appreciate knowing if there are any
    differences between it and my list.

    I have been using this for two years with my PDP-11/73, an
    LPV11 and a Toshiba P1351 printer with no problems and great
    results.   A quick test with an AST/Camintonn printer failed,
    although it works fine with a PC.   The reason has not been
    determined.   If you have any problems (or solutions), please
    let me know.

--  Alan E. Frisbie               Frisbie@Flying-Disk.Com
--  Flying Disk Systems, Inc.
--  4759 Round Top Drive          (213) 256-2575 (voice)
--  Los Angeles, CA 90065         (213) 258-3585 (FAX)





        Connections for DEC LPV11 (M8027) to Centronics printer

        Ribbon   DEC (Berg)     Centronics      Signal
        Cable    Connector      Connector        Name
        wire #    pin #           pin #

           1        A
           2        B
           3        C              27           Ground
           4        D               9           Data 8 (MSB)
           5        E              29           Ground
           6        F              11           Busy
           7        H
           8        J
           9        K
          10        L
          11        M
          12        N              13           On Line
          13        P
          14        R              32           Fault L
          15        S              16           "LA180" (Ground)
          16        T
          17        U
          18        V              14           "Connected"
          19        W              28           Ground
          20        X              10           Demand (ACKNLG L)
          21        Y
          22        Z
          23        AA              22           Ground
          24        BB               4           Data 3
          25        CC              26           Ground
          26        DD               8           Data 7
          27        EE              23           Ground
          28        FF               5           Data 4
          29        HH              20           Ground
          30        JJ               2           Data 1 (LSB)
          31        KK              21           Ground
          32        LL               3           Data 2
          33        MM
          34        NN
          35        PP              25           Ground
          36        RR               7           Data 6
          37        SS              24           Ground
          38        TT               6           Data 5
          39        UU              19           Ground
          40        VV               1           Strobe L

                     DEC (Berg) connector pins
                (Looking at male connector on board)

              Pin "A" connects to ribbon cable wire #1

         B  D  F  J  L  N  R  T  V  X  Z BB DD FF JJ LL NN RR TT VV
         A  C  E  H  K  M  P  S  U  W  Y AA CC EE HH KK MM PP SS UU

         In case of trouble, check the orientation of the ribbon cable.
         DEC often has pin #1 (red stripe) on the opposite side (pin VV).
         Also, check to make sure Centronics pin 16 is grounded.   If it
         is not, the LP11/LPV11 will try to use Data Products style
         signals.





