Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel


To me the Merced port and the Sequent collaboration make sense when
considered in the light of DEC's existing positioning of Digital Unix
and Alpha. They offer Digital Unix and Alpha as the high-end mission
critical Unix system emphasizing performance, reliability, scalability
and so on. Well adding Sequent into the picture buttresses them at the
high-end in terms of reliability and scalability. Adding Intel and
Merced into the picture buttresses them in terms of fab capacity and
future-proofing hardware decisions. Really I don't think a business
committing to Digital Unix now need worry about the very long term
future of Alpha, as they can buy the current performance champ with
the certain knowledge that future hardware for their chosen OS will
continue to be competitive. If Alpha stays ahead and even possibly
extends its lead then great Intel and Samsung will sell them all the
chips they could need and DEC and Sequent will continue to offer some
tasty boxes to put them in. If however Merced II plus $3 billion in
clown adverts sways people towards the Intel chip, well your
investment is still secure as you'll just add in Merced boxes into
your cluster and run old Alpha native executables that you can't
recompile under "FX!64". Where's the problem?

Chris
