In article <87yb0sm803.fsf@ix.netcom.com> Chris Morgan <mihalis@ix.netcom.com> writes:
>Secondly the MVI set on
>Alpha is newer than VIS (although you say VIS has been extended) and I
>don't think anyone knows how effective it is compared to MMX and VIS,
>so in particular you really cannot possibly state with any credibility
>that it "toasts" the 21264. If 21264 were such a weedy design for
>image work where would the MPEG2 _encoding_ in real-time come from?

While I'm not exactly known for giving Andrew Harrison an easy time,
this particular statement is not an option. VIS is an instruction set
that contains a number of instructions that are very well suited to
things like image convolutions. VIS is large and complex (and seems to
become more so if Sun indeed extends it). The MVI instruction set
in the 21164PC (now shipping) and the 21264 (now sampling) is just
that (Motion Video Instructions). There are no complex instructions,
only two simple partial word shifts (pack/unpack) and the PERR instructions
which exists in almost exactly that form in VIS (I believe it's called
PIXEL there). 

Digital did extensive analysis on what would help general purpose
visualization codes and other such things. MVI is very specialized
for motion video encoding/decoding and not really applicable to things
such as image convolution. The 21264 *does* have a fully pipelined integer
multiplier, which helps throughput at lot for image convolution (and
this from normal C code, not from special VIS assembly). Again, such
stuff is a tradeoff, i.e. how many applications do you think people
will special-code for your extensions (MMX or VIS) and how many are
more helped by improving the overall performance (i.e. 21264 style
investment in integer performance).

