With the introduction of the Celebris GL a couple of years back, we
changed the power switch from the typical AC power switch to a DC power
switch. This allowed the migration to Microsoft's PC9x spec which called
for the OS to have the option of controlling the power on/off. This change
meant that the circuit for the power switch wasnow on the motherboard. The
actual power switch changed to a monentary switch (the switch does not
have a set position for on/off). When power is removed and then restored,
the power on circuit doesn't know the position of the switch.

The power adapter that we're talking about solves this problem by saving
the state of the power on switch in a non-volatile register. When power is
restored, the register is in the last known position and if the power was
ON, the system will power on.

Gary's problem may be something else. Even with the power adapter, the
system does not power up. This could be caused by a printer connected to
the parallel printer port. Some printers when powered on provide
sufficient current back through the cable to prevent the power on circuit
from working correctly. We've identifed a number of these printers. The
solution is to turn on the power to the printer after the system powers
up. In the case of a power failure, some kind of a power on delay would
solve this. I've seen a number of power consoles from Curtis that cycle
the power for different devices in a particular order. I've seen these at
CompUSA.

regards,

glen kelley
