In article <bland-060198122857@host-251.subnet-13.med.umich.edu>, bland@umich.edu (Peyton Bland) writes:

>Can anyone help with the magic to put in the config file?

You never have to rebuild the kernel to add a device to an
existing SCSI bus.  For LUN 0 disks and tapes this has never
been *necessary* on any released version of DEC OSF/1 or Digital
UNIX (I start counting at V1.2).  I think there were some early
versions where you needed configuration file lines to get I/O
stats, but this may have been limited to non-LUN 0 devices of
array controllers.  I'm also not sure whether you needed device
lines for non-LUN 0 disks just to see them, but I don't think
so.  This has been fixed in recent versions.

You do need to build a new kernel to add a SCSI controller,
but you don't need anything for the disks or tapes on that
controller.

>anything I need to know about running MAKEDEV?

(bus * 8) + SCSI-ID

That's the essential bit of information.  If you're
creating a disk preceded the "unit" number from that
by "rz".  For tapes use "tz".  If creating disks for
array controllers that use LUNs other than zero it
gets a bit more complicated.  MAKEDEV won't create
non-LUN 0 tape devices at all. 

