As Rob has pointered out, the Pentium Pro can not be upgraded to other types of
processors, only the Pentium Pro. This is because the Pentium Pro Intel chip
uses the socket 8 (not Digital's idea). The fastest Pentium Pro made was the
200MHz and Intel has not planned any follow-on chips or an "OverDrive" upgrade.

Rob Stow wrote:

> Ragnar wrote:
> >
> > I have a Venturis GL running a Pentium Pro 200Mhz processor. I am unsure
> > of the specs on the motherboard, but I certainly would love to upgrade to
> > a 266Mhz Pentium II processor.
> > I have searched the Digital web site, and have found nothing that says I
> > can or can't do this. Any insights into this would be great. From what I
> > understand, the Celeris and the Venturis are similar in architecture.
> >
>
> You won't be able to upgrade your processor outside
> of the P-Pro processors:  a P-Pro uses a processor
> socket ("Socket 8") and chipset that is incompatible
> with plain vanilla Pentiums (which use "Socket 7")
> and with the Pentium II's (which use "Slot 1" to hold
> a cartridge containing the processor and cache).
>
> A motherboard upgrade is your only option for
> upgrading your processor to a Pentium II and is
> almost undoubtedly too expensive relative to the
> price of a complete new computer.  Digital has
> a long history of non-standard motherboard form
> factors, which results in them being the only
> source for upgrades to Digital computers and they
> charge an arm and a leg for their motherboards.
>

Digital's PC are designed in ways similar to Compaq , HP or IBM: the form
factor is determined by cost as well as features. Any motherboard from Compaq,
HP or IBM will be "non-standard".

The current shipping systems from Digital use a form factor called NLx, which
is a design standard by Intel, Compaq and Digital. The form factor is an
"industry standard" (as defined by Intel, Compaq and Digital).

Dell and Gateway use to design their systems using the AT or other "standard"
because it was in their business model to purchase MLBs from other
manufacturers and the AT was a standard that was widely used (and still is).
Now that they make their MLBs, some models have non-standard MLBs.

The only exception to this was the Digital retail line (Starion) which used the
Western Digital LPx form factor (at the time, this was "the" standard).


