It was written...

>      > I challenge you to find one of these benchmarks where
>      > Digital has a performance lead.
>      >
>      > TPC-C, Third behind Sun and HP
>      > TPC-D, never published a 1 TB or 300 GB number 
>      > In the 100 GB tests they are 4th behind NCR, DG and Sun

> Why have Digital not put in any good numbers?
> 
> resources ? does not seem to stop much smaller companies like DG.

DEC is pretty resource-constrained these days. You won't see any new
benchmarks out of them until EV6-based systems are shipping. 

> performance ? more likely.

See above. The performance delta between a 440MHz and a 612.8MHz EV56 is
far less than the delta between an EV56 and an EV6 running at ~500MHz.
Why bother doing EV56 benchmarks (at as much as a quarter million bucks
per pop) when they will be superceded in three months or so?

> lack of interest internally ? possibly but then why buy their boxes if
> they can't be bothered.

Huh? Don't buy a box simply because the vendor chooses not to spend a
quarter of a million bucks on tpcs? In DEC's case, the bucks are better
spent accelerating the WildFire rollout.
> 
> Are you going to wait untill they give you the benchmark information
> that will influence
> your server decision or do you go to a vendor who has done the work
> and if you
> are prepared to wait, how long will you wait.
> 

If you're smart, you take ALL benchmarketing bluster with a big grain of
salt (a 50 pound salt block works real nicely) and benchmark YOUR OWN
APPLICATIONS on the competitive platforms in question.

Before you get dazed and confused by the "my $tpmC is better than your
$tpmC," take a close look at the configurations used to produce audited
benchmark results. They bear little resemblance to real-world production
configurations.

