In article <1998Jan9.153149.1@eisner>, young_r@eisner.decus.org (Rob Young) writes:
>
>    Here ... look at what some heavyweights say Re: Alpha and Intel and
>    FTC:
>
>    http://www.infoworld.com/cgi-bin/displayStory.pl?98018.wdeci
>

I would have, except that the URL above is invalid.

>"One observer expressed surprise that the FTC reportedly has antitrust concerns
>of that nature. The Alpha chip is also currently produced by Samsung and
>Mitsubishi, and Alpha's market share is so small compared to Intel's that it is
>unlikely that Intel's purchase of Digital's fabrication facilities and its
>intent to manufacture Alpha chips could affect competition with Intel's own
>chips. according to Terry Shannon, editor of Shannon Knows DEC, a newsletter in
>Ashland, Mass."
>
>"I don't see how the presence or absence of that fab facility is really going
>to impact the popularity of IA-64," Shannon said. "Intel is not further
>marginalizing Alpha by acquiring the foundry in which Alpha is built." 

I agree with Terry on that, but it would seem to be a bit of a red herring.

The real FTC issue is whether or not the language in the agreement in some way 
includes Digital bailing on Alpha in some manner which is not immediately 
apparent from what (little) we know of the settlement, since Alpha is one 
of the primary competitors to Merced.  Digital is of 
course denying that they are writing off the Alpha, but Digital has not
always been an altogether reliable source of information on its own future
actions (as DECstation owners can attest).  One must wonder then, why
Digital and Sequent are off to port to Merced when Digital is currently
shipping Alphas comparable (if not faster) than the projected speed of the
first version of Merced, with the projected speed of Alphas of that era
being *much* quicker than Merced.  Moreover, Digital of recent years has
had a peculiar relationship with Microsoft which may, or may not, account
for the noncompetitive positioning of Digital's own operating systems
against Microsoft's own WNT in many market segments.  (A particular sore
point for me, since it has effectively forced me to plan a migration off of
my platform of choice.)  Now Digital has developed another peculiar
relationship with another should be competitor, and then turns immediately
to porting DU to Merced.  Inquiring minds at the FTC are doubtless trying
to understand exactly which market this Merced version of DU is aimed at,
since the Alpha version should be vastly faster, and on all but the
smallest of workstations, any difference in price due to the processor
itself will be a negligible factor in the purchase decision.  Also, since
part of the deal is apparently a price subsidy on the Alphas from Intel, it
isn't altogether clear that the Merced will have much of a price advantage
either.  How then to rationalize DU/Merced? Is Digital planning another of
its strategic retreats from key markets?  In this case, positioning the
DU/Alpha as a "premium" alternative to the DU/Merced?  (Just as they have
positioned OpenVMS/Alpha as a "premium" alternative to WNT/Intel or Alpha.)
From the FTC viewpoint an agreement between Digital and Intel to "position"
in this manner would be bad thing, since it indicates an intent to suppress
competition in a given market.

Anyway, there are other arguments you can make, but it is perfectly 
reasonable that the FTC should be considering whether Digital will give
Intel more competition with, or without, the settlement.  Still, I suspect
that in the end they'll let the settlement go through unmodified. 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



