} 
}I can only hope DEC isn't so foolish as to select something like
}ADVANTAGE-UNIX, or DURAWARE. Believe it or not, said monikers were in
}the running back in the OSF/1 days.

DURAWARE?

I think I have some of that sitting in my refrigerator holding leftovers.

If you put Digital and Sequent together you can get almost get "Sequential".
I can't say that calling a multitasking multiuser OS "Sequential" is a great
thing, but it would beat the heck out of DURAWARE or ADVANTAGE-UNIX. (What
is it with the all capitals thing, anyway?) "Sequential 64" is even
alliterative.

On the other hand, if combining the two names DEC marketing would probably go
for DIGIQUENT UNIX, which isn't much better than ADVANTAGE-UNIX (although I'd
say it is still noticably better than DURAWARE).

I could also see "Digital DYNIX" since Sequent's current IA-32 Unix is
called DYNIX/ptx, although that might displease both parties since to
Digital it would sound like it is based on DYNIX instead of being DU
incorporating some of the technology from DYNIX (such as its NUMA technology)
and the people from Sequent might notice that Digital's name appears in the
name and theirs doesn't.

--- Carl



