On 23 Dec 1997, Edward Lee wrote:

+In article [cut] Richard Tomkins <neuron@cyberus.ca> wrote:
+>The use of the command prompt window to manipulate Windows NT and Windows
+>95 programs and stuff is problematic.
+>
+>The command prompt only understands 8.3 file names and will truncate stuff
+>and also delete stuff, unbeknownst to the innocent.
+>[....]
+
+The Windows NT command prompt does understand long file names.  When

 Exactly as Win95. Rihard -be aware, that the "like DOS" mode where you
get when start the system as a "command line only" is *not* the same
in function as the command box: it really doesn't know about the long
names - but the windows box *does*.
 Also: in the box command line you can can use *eighter* the long
name *or* the mangled compatible-dos-8.3-name. If you do a DIR
you see TWO names for any file/directory !

+you need to change the directory, for example, you have to enclose a
+long directory name in double quotes [snip in-line]

 Only when the name contains a special character like space etc.
 For "normal" name you can use the long name without any problem !
CD aLongNamedDirectory
 is correct (also the lower/upcase is ignored).

+                                      or use a wildcard.

 *WHAT* ??
 Yes, no, er... I have see that behavior useful many year ago
and my CD procedure for VMS does that (in the meaning: "set the
default to first matched directory") but haven't see it implemented
in any OS !! (and yes, it's inconsistent: why directory "A" may be
a "best" than named "B" ?).
 Definitely (including Win95 OSR2 Polish edition) doesn't work.

[the same for COPY is correct, no doubt]

+I was concerned about the possibility of file name truncation, but found
+to my satisfaction that no file name truncation occurred.

 To be precise: when in use is FAT (or FAT32) filesystem then
the truncation does occur, but in the directory is stored *both*
the truncated (and converted to upcase) and full name.
 And with simple DIR one may see both :)

[cut]
+
+The HP SureStore 7110e drive uses a non-standard file system which
+preserves long file names.

 Hm... I suspect you would say: "the program where I have
will store the filesystem on a JOLIET structure [where is a Microsoft
(I think) propertiary extension] *as default*". Probably you use
the EasyCD pack ? Look somewhere to set-up and you see that you *can*
select the filesystem !
 The device itself can only distinct between the 2048, 2352 and 2356
bytes long sector for data and audio in differrent format *and*
may be responsible to some type of storing (b.ex. not all CD-writers
can do a Disk-At-Once write), but not the filesystem itself.

+                           The drivers take care of the interface
+with the operating system.  The drive is also capable of creating
+regular ISO 9660 compatible CD-R disks, but you must buy separate
+third-party software to do so.

 Look in them what you have - and you probably will find the
ISO 9660/HighSierra format. But yes, I've seen that some program
where declare a write in ISO 9600 will *not* properly *check*
the data to write and you *can* write a disk where are in general
looks as ISO but because not checked have some incompatibility
because file names (b.ex. a national character in it, a extension
in the directory name [ISO will not allow !!] etc.) and you can
have problem reading the CD - because noone have check - on some
OSes: probably not in Windows... but under VMS, Mac'OS etc.

+               I have read articles where people
+mentioned the EZ CD Creator software from Corel.

 Have seen that definitely has, but have seen at least three
program (also the EasyCD and one where can't remind: this was
in past) and all have the possiblity to write ISO). But will
it *properly* check to all ISO limitation ?
 For test create a directory with extension and see what the program
will say: as I have say ISO will not allow that name :>

