>
> Perhaps I should have only quoted the tail end of your post :
>
> You used the present tense when saying the Ultra-III toasts the 21264
> in things like image convolutions. You cannot possibly know this as
> firstly the Ultra-III does not exist, so when it does exist it will
> have to compared to the Alpha that exists at that point, as a minimum
> I would say 700-800MHz in 0.25 micron silicon. Secondly the MVI set on
> Alpha is newer than VIS (although you say VIS has been extended) and I
> don't think anyone knows how effective it is compared to MMX and VIS,
> so in particular you really cannot possibly state with any credibility
> that it "toasts" the 21264. If 21264 were such a weedy design for
> image work where would the MPEG2 _encoding_ in real-time come from?
>

Well, firstly Digital are only claiming to be able to Encode MPEG-1and they are
claiming to be able to decode MPEG-2, not encode MPEG-2
since an UltraII  can do MPEG-1 encoding and MPEG-2 decoding now using VIS
we can assume that whatever Digital are claiming for the MVI instruction
set in the 21264 is only comparable to the current UltraII capabilities
at least for MPEG.

BTW The UltraIII will be at least 3 times as fast as the UltraII for VIS
the clock will be faster, the memory interface will be faster and VIS will
benefit from the additional FPU in the UltraIII. VIS instructions are
executed in the FPU.

But comparing MVI with VIS is a mistake, MVI is only targeted
at video encoding and decoding and in that sense VIS matches
MVI function for function, but VIS does a whole load of other
things that MVI does not do, which is why it can give huge performance
wins for things like Smith Waterman, Photoshop, Block load and store
for TCP/IP accelaration, none of these are applicable to MVI.

MVI for example would be not usefull for accelerating the fluid flow
products from Exa

http://www.exa.com/Press/ExaNEWS_111296.html



