Peyton;

	The easiest way to add your tape (or any device) is NOT to
edit the kernel config file (unless you understand the syntax).

	Try this process for your new tape (or anything else).

	1.  Shutdown system and add your new hardware.
	2.  Powerup and verify that the computer knows it's there.
            You can do this at the console firmware prompt.
	3.  Boot the system using the GENERIC kernel.
	4.  Execute "doconfig -c {yoursystemname}."  When asked if
	    you want to "replace" the existing config file, say "yes."
	5.  Backup old kernel if needed; copy new kernel to root.
	6.  Re-boot.

When you boot off the generic kernel and the tape is present, the OS
knows about it.  So, when you run doconfig and tell it to replace the
config file for your system name; doconfig will make a new config file
based on everything in your current hardware configuration.



