In article <34B14B75.B994E396@pacifier.com>, Scott Heynderickx <sheynde@pacifier.com> writes:
:OK tech heads, I've got the opportunity to purchase 4 DEC 19-inch
:monitors. Two are the VR299 model with the RGB connectors in back and
:the other two are VR 319 model with what looks like a coaxial cable
:hookup. The guy said they are for Mac. Are these two color--Would they
:work with IBM based machines?

  IBM has made a number of quite different machines and graphics display
  terminals over the years.  Mind narrowing it down a bit?

  If you are refering to a typical PC graphics controller, forget it.
  (If you want the details on why I say "forget it", please visit the
  http://www.dejanews.com/ site and try a few searches -- there have
  been many previous discussions -- search for synch-on-green -- of
  various older DIGITAL monitors, monitors that follow a design that
  predates the appearance of the commodity video signalling systems.)

  DIGITAL monitors of this vintage require the horizontal and vertical
  synchronization signals on the green wire -- this is often refered to
  as synch-on-green -- and very few standard PCI, EISA, ISA, or IBM
  MicroChannel graphics controllers can generate this signalling scheme.
  Typical PC graphics controllers generate three-wire RGB, with seperate
  synchronization signals on two additional BNCs.  (One also sees a d-sub
  (DB15?) connector design on more recent graphics systems, but I digress.)

  The VR319 is a monochrone with synch-on-green (1 BNC), while the VR299
  is color RGB synch-on-green (3 BNC).  ("Green" is a misnomer when working
  with a monochrome monitor, but I digress... Again...)

  I won't bother looking up the monitor resolutions and the Hz, unless
  you actually have a synch-on-green-capable graphics controller...

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman@xdelta.ZZenet.dec.com
