>Jay West wrote:
>I have a micro PDP11/73 with an RD52 and RX50 internal. I also have
>a second RD52 I'd like to temporarily put internal to the cpu (in place
>of the RX50). There are connectors built in for this, but I was wondering
>if anything needs to be changed on the RQDX2 controller?

Jerome Fine replies:

I am cross-posting this reply since it may still be of interest to other
PDP-11 users.

If are willing to do a tiny bit of soldering, you can duplicate the functionality
of a 6 button front panel.

I normally try to do this in such a manner that there is no permanent alteration
to the original wiring.

Also, I believe that either solution would apply to all three controllers:
RQDX1,  RQDX2  and  RQDX3  although the solutions have only even
been in use for the first two.  Also, they have been used only for RD51,
RD52 and RD53 drives.  There is no reason why they would not also
function for an RD54.  The specific situation when I lost all the information
on both hard drives was for 2 RD52s - Jay West - be careful - don't say
you were not warned - it takes only a few seconds late at night when you
may be tired to set both drives as DS3 and you are then lost and have to
re-format both drives.  If you always have the WRITE PROTECT on
at this point, you may be safe, but don't count on it.  I remember. I did it.

In one solution, I inserted an "extension" cable into the 10 flat wire cable to the
I/O distribution panel for the hard drives and floppy drives.  This alteration
required only jumpering two of the wires.  Of course, now both hard drives
must be in the same status (both either WRITE  PROTECTED or neither)
and if you really wanted to, both off line (I would suppose - since I have
never found the need for that option and have never tested it).  The one
drawback I seem to remember experiencing was that after pushing the
single  "WRITE  PROTECT"  button (remember it controls both now),
I could not un-write-protect either drive unless I re-booted.  It seems
that the loading of two drives hung the status and it would not release
until an init of the controller.

The other solution which is much more extensive is the construct your
own substitute front panel for just the hard disk drives and/or floppies.
This is simple if you do not want indicator diodes since just switches
are required.  And I did not bother with the READY button since I
have never had occasion to want a drive "not ready".

THE  ONE  THING  YOU  MUST  MUST  MUST  MUST  MUST
BE  AWARE  OF  IS  THAT  THE  DRIVE  SELECTION  JUMPERS
MUST  BE SET  PROPERLY  IF  YOU  WANT  TO  RETAIN
FORMATTING  ON  THE  DRIVES  (ESPECIALLY  IF  THEY
ARE  NOT  WRITE  PROTECTED).  I forgot ONCE  (it was enough -
when the write protect switch is off) and lost ALL  THE  CONTENTS
of  BOTH  drives.  As other respondents have noted,  the first drive
is normal enough and is set at DS3.  BUT  THE  SECOND  DRIVE
MUST  MUST  MUST  MUST  MUST  BE  SET AT  DS4.!!!!!!!!!

By the way,  if you put both of your hard drives into the BA23, you
can still run the RX50 from a long cable from outside the BA23 box.
All three of the RQDX1,  RQDX2  and  RQDX3  do allow 4 units
on line at a time.

A word of advice.  When using a close to full, and especially a full
backplane of boards,  check how much power you are drawing.
Normally, I  ALWAYS  used a spare PC power supply for the
2 hard drives and the floppy rather than load down the BA23
box internal power supply with that large a power drain.  The
hard drives and floppy will be quite happy with the 5 volt and
12 volt from the PC power supply.  And the BA23 box will
thank you for your consideration and love in not having to
also start those hard drives at power up by giving you many
more years of useful trouble free service.  This word of advice
seems to have worked very well in my case.

At one point before I shifted to other types of drives, all of the above
was working and tested.

OR!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

Buy or beg or look for a BA123 box which is twice as large, almost
always sits on the floor, has twice the power, has 12 slots rather than
8 (the first 4 are ABCD rather than the first 3 for the BA23)  AND
can hold up to 4 hard drives at once with the special extender card
for all three controllers that normally comes as a standard bit of
hardware with each BA123.

One other bit of advice.  RD5n drives are no longer very reliable.
If you get a year or two from each drive, be happy.  ALWAYS be
sure that you have ALL of your files backed up.  Have a few
extra RD5n drives on hand.  RD54s might be a bit better than
RD53s, but I never had the bigger bucks for them before I found
a very inexpensive alternative.  But, while I am still using RD53s
on occasion, I never take a chance (well almost never) and I have
never been sorry (well almost never:)-.  Also, I am advised that
you should spin up those spare drives every so often so the heads
do not lock up from lack of use.  I don't know if this is true.

Happy RD52ing.  Please send me a note as to how it works out.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict
