In article <rubiconEMBw5q.HwL@netcom.com>, rubicon@netcom.com (James Birdsall) writes:
:   I have just acquired a MicroVAX II in a nice BA23 case. I'd like to get
:it back in service, but after opening it up, I have a lot of questions.

  The first mistake folks make is assuming that the CSR and interrupt
  vector addresses can be set in isolation -- this is not the case.
  The addition or removal of a Q-bus module into a Q-bus can cause a
  series of cascading CSR and interrupt vector address changes, on one
  or more additional Q-bus boards present in the system.

  For details, try some www.dejanews.com searches -- I've posted some
  basic CSR and interrupt vector information multiple times, as have
  others.

  (There are Q-bus boards -- the DEQNA, DELQA, and RQDX3 are among
  these -- that are at a fixed CSR address and interrupt vector for
  the first board present in the system, and the addition and removal
  of one of these fixed-address boards does not cascade.  Additional
  boards do cause the cascade, and can be at various addresses.)

  (For configuration information, see the SYSGEN> CONFIGURE command
  under OpenVMS.  Under other operating systems, I do not know how
  the correct Q-bus device addresses are configured...)

:   Background: the boards present in the cardcage (Micro-11 backplane,
:HA9278A) are: M7606, which is obviously the CPU with 1M memory onboard;
:M7609, 8M memory; M7608, 4M memory; a DEQNA; a M7555; an RX50; and an
:RD53.

  Support for the DEQNA was removed around V5.5, with the DELQA
  replacing it in this enclosure.

:   Question 1: The CPU is connected to a serial port on the rear of the
:chassis. The baud knob is pretty obvious, but I have no idea about the
:other two controls. One is a three-position knob, with the following
:choices: an arrow pointing away from the knob, a stylized face in profile
:with mouth open, and a 'T' in a circle with an arrow pointing clockwise.
:The other is a two-position switch, generically marked with a triangle
:inside a circle; the two positions are: circle with dot inside, and circle
:with dot outside.

  Please see "VAX1.   Please explain the back panel of the MicroVAX II"
  in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ).  FAQ URLs:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/vms
    ftp://rtfm.mit.edu/pub/usenet/news.answers/dec-faq/vms

:   Question 2: I have an RX50, RD53, and M7555, which according to various
:net resources should all play together. How do I hook them up? The
:card-edge connectors on the M7555 don't have enough conductors for all
:those signals and the bus; there's also a big header connector on the
:M7555, but it's not obvious what that connects to. I assume that when it
:is hooked up correctly, the connectors on the back of the drive bay area
:are active?

  The RQDX controller connects to the signal distribution panel in
  the BA23 -- basically located behind the Q-bus backplane -- with
  the ribbon cable routed beside the Q-bus and the metal of the BA23.
  Once connected to the signal distribution panel -- remove a couple
  of screws at the back of the drive bay, remove the cover, and you'll
  be able to see the signal distribution panel itself -- the RX50 and
  RD5x drives are connected to the marked sockets.

:   Question 3: Where can I get a mounting sled for my RD53?

  Scrounge it -- I'd recommend scrounging an RD54, and keeping the RD53
  as a spare.

:   Question 4: It doesn't look like NetBSD will boot from an RX50, and
:anyway I have no way to write a boot floppy for it. It looks like my best
:bet is to boot a minimal system diskless, then use that to transfer a full
:system to the RD53. Am I overlooking any easier alternatives?

  Donno about NetBSD.  There is a hobbyist OpenVMS license for V6.1
  available via DECUS -- see http://www.montagar.com/ for details.

