        If you were in the  SF  Bay  area,  I  have a couple of overflow
    VS3100/76  Owner's  Guides.   That's  not the  same  as  a  hardware
    reference, but it would have answered many or most of your questions
    so far...

> Anyway, two questions:  On the back of the VS3100-76, on the far right as
> you're looking at the back, there's a little bank of led's, apparently
> diagnostic type things,  Next to them on the left is a recessed switch. 
> What is the function of this switch?

        You've found the S3, or "Alternate Console" switch. :-)  Down is
    the normal position, slide it  up  for  using  a  VT terminal as the
    console.

> Second, I only have one monitor, but I've got a couple of VT type
> terminals  around.  Is it possible to make the VS3100 use one of it's
> serial ports as  as the console?

        Yes, precisely.  Shut the system  down  and  power it off.  Move
    the S3 switch into the UP position.  Plug a VT terminal into the MMJ
    socket  (RS423) with the _Printer_ icon above, it's one removed from
    the S3 switch, _not_ the one next to the switch.  Set  the  terminal
    to 9600 baud, 8-bit no parity.  Power up and you've got your console
    terminal.  :-)

        Actually, App.A of this manual adds this note:

        "9 If the  console  prompt  (>>>)  does  note  appear on the
           alternate  console  screen,  press the break key  on  the
           alternate console terminal keyboard."

    This is followed by a discussion of "terminal emulation mode", etc.,
    which I _believe_ is  irrelevant  if  you  only  have a terminal and
    don't  have  a graphics head, etc.  Note that the S3 switch is  only
    read at power-up, so you do need to power down the system  in  order
    to addect any change.

        -Ken
