> Well, what I was talking about was scale (ie. splitt a job/task on serval CPU's).
> Thats a important issue for large coperations, thats running huge databases and do datawarehousing.
> If Digital will be sucessful in that marked they will need an OS that can handle many processors.
> Solaris can easly handle servers with 64 or 128 CPU's. I dont know how good Alpha do scaleing.
> (I have only seen these nifty quad boxes.. :)

I've seen 14-way DEC Alpha boxes, and they seem to scale pretty damned
well (the biggest problem DEC faces with TLaser-class machines is I/O).
The 32-CPU crossbar switch-based WILDFIRE server ought to be out by the
end of the year. WILDFIRE ultimately could scale to 256 CPUs, but that's
some years away. Figure at least 64 CPUS by Y2K, though.

DEC's OVMS folks claim that the Galaxy Software Architecture will
provide near-linear scalability. The DUNIX folks claim that Digital UNIX
will scale well to at least 64 processors.

Information on Galaxy is available at the SKD web page on 
http://www.acersoft.com

