In article <34b09ba6.0@rainbow.hrz.uni-siegen.de>, engel@numerik.math.uni-siegen.de (Michael Engel) writes:
:Dave Walton (walton@en.com) wrote:
:: Can I use IDE drives on an old AXP-150 running NT4?

  In general, no.

:The AXPpc150 aka Jensen doesn't have an IDE controller onboard and I
:don't think the ARC firmware supports booting from IDE (heck, it doesn't 
:support booting from floppy ! ;-)). You might try putting an IDE controller
:board in one of the EISA slots, but I'm afraid NT won't be happy with it.

  You'd need to install and configure an IDE controller under Windows NT,
  loading and enabling the ATDISK driver -- there was no IDE controller
  shipped with the standard Jensen package, and there was thus minimal
  (no?) testing in this area...

:The Jensen usually has an Adaptec 1742A EISA SCSI Controller, any standard
:SCSI disk drive will work with it.

  I'd not say "any" in the context of SCSI -- in my experience, that's
  not a safe assumption to make.

  A number of SCSI drives will work with the Adaptec controller used in
  the Jensen, but the controller is rather sensitive to the particular
  SCSI configuration -- I would keep all drives internal, or all drives
  external, and I would keep the SCSI as short as possible.  If using
  any internal drives, beware of the cooling requirements of the 7200
  RPM drives -- I'd tend to use an external enclosure rated for cooling
  the 7200 RPM drives.  (I don't know that any 7200 RPM drives have been
  tested in the Jensen enclosure -- one could end up "cooking" a disk.)

