James Birdsall <rubicon@netcom.com> writes:
>    Question 1: The CPU is connected to a serial port on the rear of the
> chassis. The baud knob is pretty obvious, but I have no idea about the
> other two controls. One is a three-position knob, with the following
> choices: an arrow pointing away from the knob, a stylized face in profile
> with mouth open, and a 'T' in a circle with an arrow pointing clockwise.
> The other is a two-position switch, generically marked with a triangle
> inside a circle; the two positions are: circle with dot inside, and circle
> with dot outside.

  Those are the infamous Icons With No Decipherable Meanings 8-) They are:

  A) Normal operation
  B) Language Inquiry (only on DEC MCS terminals - VT220 and up)
  C) Continuous test loop

  The flip switch is the console break enable/disable switch.

>    Question 2: I have an RX50, RD53, and M7555, which according to various
> net resources should all play together. How do I hook them up? The
> card-edge connectors on the M7555 don't have enough conductors for all
> those signals and the bus; there's also a big header connector on the
> M7555, but it's not obvious what that connects to. I assume that when it
> is hooked up correctly, the connectors on the back of the drive bay area
> are active?

  You need a 50-pin cable between the M7555 and the backplane assembly -
if you look in the front where the disks go, there's a metal cover about
3.5" x 1" held on w/ 2 screws. The other end of the 50-pin cable goes
there. Note that RD53's had an ECO to change out that cable for a shielded
one - otherwise they sometimes corrupted themselves.

>    Question 3: Where can I get a mounting sled for my RD53?

  Any used DEC dealer - they're pretty worthless now (the sleds, not the
dealers 8-). I don't think DEC has any anymore.

>    Question 4: It doesn't look like NetBSD will boot from an RX50, and
> anyway I have no way to write a boot floppy for it. It looks like my best
> bet is to boot a minimal system diskless, then use that to transfer a full
> system to the RD53. Am I overlooking any easier alternatives?

  Look on ftp.spc.edu for "third-party-disks.txt" or similar. It shows how
to modify a number of common PC 1.2MB floppies to work as RX33's, the DEC
1.2MB format.

