Aaron Spink wrote:

> Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com#> writes:
>
> > You seem to be too badly informed to be speaking for Digital and to
> > suggest
> > that the UltraSPARC III is simply a process shrink is ample proof of
> > this.
> >
> Well, lets see, I said I wasn't speaking for dec.  Can I assume you
> speak for sun?

No I don't speak for Sun any more than you speak for Digital.

> Yet there are differences between the U2 and U3.  Yes,
> it is still vary simular to the ultra 2 though.  The differences that

> you quote below are fairly evolutionary.

But not the "only a die shrink" that you claimed.

> The big problem because and
> will not go away in the near future, is that a 600+ mhz in-order micro
> processor is not a very good idea.

This is your opinion, but lets just examine some of the issues. The
UltraIIIhas 9 execution units, the 21264 has 6 this makes OOE more
necessary to
meet your projected 6 issue number.

The 21264 has a longer cache miss latency than the UltraIII (according to
the papers
at Microprocessor Forum) having an OOE core will compensate for this.

At the end of the day its a set of design tradeoff's. The UltraIII has a
lower cache miss
latency and higher memory bandwidth and Ecache bandwidth than the 21264,
it has
more execution units and a much more complete Visual Instruction Set that
lets it
toast Alphas on things like image convolutions, gene sequencing and a
whole host
of graphical and non graphical codes than can use VIS.

> LD/ST start to be heavily dominate
> when you get up to these speeds.  The question I have is this.
> Everything I have seen on the U3 says that it is an IOE design.  Is
> this true?  If it is, I'd say that the performance estimates for a
> 600Mhz UIII which I have seen quoted at 30Int95 and 60FP95 are a
> little bit agressive concidering the estimates for a 600mhz OOE alpha
> with the same cache sizes and the same issue rate are the same.  All I
> am saying is that I don't see the USIII reaching the performance
> estimates that have been quoted for it.
>

Only time will tell, but simply having OOE is not going to guarantee that
the21264 is faster. Cache bandwidth, latency, TLB design, etc etc all play
an
equally big part.

BTW people don't buy CPU's they buy systems and these need to be
engineered
as well putting the 21264 into an 8400 will not be a good idea unless you
only
want one CPU.You have also misquoted our estimates they are 35+ Int95 and
60+ fp95.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK






