:   shipped with the standard Jensen package, and there was thus minimal
:   (no?) testing in this area...

Some experience with our Jensens (running Linux) make me think that the I/O
system is totally different from standard PC-compatible EISA hardware re.
address decoding. I'm not sure if NT/Alpha will support an IDE controller
in an EISA slot at all as I assume all (E)IDE controllers on more recent
systems are PCI-IDE controllers. 

: :The Jensen usually has an Adaptec 1742A EISA SCSI Controller, any standard
: :SCSI disk drive will work with it.

:   I'd not say "any" in the context of SCSI -- in my experience, that's
:   not a safe assumption to make.

You're right. _Most_ drives _should_ work ...

:   A number of SCSI drives will work with the Adaptec controller used in
:   the Jensen, but the controller is rather sensitive to the particular
:   SCSI configuration -- I would keep all drives internal, or all drives
:   external, and I would keep the SCSI as short as possible.  

I wasn't aware of the fact that the Adaptec 1742 is that sensitive. Could 
that be a problem of the DEC-specific controller firmware (G2 ?) that was
used instead of the standard Adaptec one ? I must admit that my Jensen here
was a blow-out sale from our local Vobis shop (a big German PC distributor)
and I used a standard Adaptec 1742A in it. Works fine with Linux and NT,
but not with DEC Unix. VMS probably wants the DEC-specific controller 
firmware, too.

Btw., does anyone know what to do with a Jensen with a defective flash rom ?
There is an empty 32-pin PLCC socket near the flash on the Jensen board,
might that be used for a firmware EPROM ??? It's not documentend AFAIK.
We have two Jensen boards with defective flash roms here that should 
otherwise work fine.

regards,
	Michael Engel	(engel@unix-ag.uni-siegen.de)




