If it's a MicroVAX 2000 it may have a block about 3"x3" in the back fastened
to the lip/apron of the box.  The block will have 3 MMJ type connector jacks
on id underside.  The plastic should be numbered  1-2-3.  The number 1 jack
is the OPA0: or console.  Connect a terminal via a DECconnect cable and plug
it into this port.  The terminal must be 9600 baud.  THere's no way to change
this speed.

IF you box does not have this 3"x3" block, you'll need a BCC08 cable.  Plug
the 9 pin side into the 9 pin jack and the 25 pin side into your terminal.
Again, the terminal must be 9600 baud.  I'm not certain of the pinout of the
BCC08 however a IBM 9 pin to 25 pin cable WILL NOT work.

If you need the pinout for the BCC08, reply back here and I or some other
collector of VAX antiquities should be able to get that for you.

Once you've gotten the terminal connected, a self test should ensue when the
box is powered up and will eventually result in the ">>>" prompt (unless it
autoboots that is).  Test B tests memory and may take a bit of time -- wait.

At the ">>>" prompt, you can type "T 50" and should get a synopsis of the 
tests performed.  Copy this information down because, if you continue to 
have further problems, that information posted here will help me or one of
the other collectors of VAX antiquities diagnose your problem(s).

