Aaron Spink  <spink@kraftwerk.pa.dec.com> wrote:
>> Sounds like Ultra-III will exceed Alpha. [...]
>> 
>Not really.  Not to harp or anything, but, do your really think that
>sun will get a 2x clock speed improvement from basically a shrink.  Do
>you think that a processor that is in-order running at a slower speed
>than an OOE processor will be faster.  Do you think digital is
>standing still?  As far as I am concerned, UIII is pure marketing hype
>until sun can actually release a product.  

By that definition, 21264 and 21364 are pure marketing hype;
admittedly, the 264 is marketing hype with nearterm delivery,
but where are machines running it? 8-)

This discussion is really sort of silly.  Will the Ultra-III exceed
current Alphas and early 21264s?  One would hope so, given the
timeframe it will be noncompetitive if it does not.  Will it exceed
late model 264s and 364s at the time it's introduced?  Probably not.

Will Sun get a 2x speed improvement from a process shrink?  No.
Is Ultra-III just a process shrink?  Also no.  600mhz clocking is
pretty much standard for well done 0.25um CPU designs.  Remember that
the Alphas have been getting 600mhz from very aggressive 0.35um chips.
People are getting pretty good about squeezing more speed from their
pipelines without even doing shrinks, in all chip development lines.
With the shrink and somewhat less speed-demonish chips 600mhz is going
to be normal in 99.  It's really nifty what you can do with 40 picosecond
gates if you can keep interconnect distances and wiring manageable.

A slower in-order processor could be faster than a faster-clocked
OOE chip, if the in-order CPU has more or more efficiently fills
its pipelines.  There is nothing magic about OOE, its another tool
to try and speed things up, but making it work requires a lot of
silicon that in-order doesn't need.  As with any architecture choice
you have to look at all the details and how they interact to see if
it's a win or loss.  IMHO, OOE on Alphas is a win, but that doesn't
mean that all CPUs must go to OOE immediately or that it's the only
path to fast chips.

Finally, obviously, Digital isn't going to be a stationary target,
and they should not be.  The more aggressive 21264s (the shrink to
0.25 ought to speed up more than 600->700mhz, and will eventually)
and 364s we'll be seeing in a couple of more years are going to
be a lot faster than what Digitial is shipping today.

It's appropriate to defend DEC from silly claims about SPARC,
but it's also appropriate to credit the next-gen SPARCs appropriately.


-george william herbert
gherbert@crl.com




