The question of "solaris on an alpha" seems to me to point to the real weakness
of the unix boxes (sun, hp, sgi, ibm, dec) against MSoft.

Msoft has a unified product, even if people don't always love it.  They are
being fought by a ragtag bunch of companies that can't unite on a common front.

Sure, the whole theory behind Unix was that it would be easy to port from
platform to platform, but things are never that easy.  Consider a big company
paying a total cost of 150k/yr to support each sys-admin.  Easy big software
package (ansys, cadence, etc) is box specific.   Each system has its own
pecularities, and it is much simpler to have all wintel boxes.

Consider our company, we have macs, pc's, and unix workstations (sun and hp). 
A hot wintel box is about 70% as fast as an ultra workstation, and cost much
less, and you can buy an engineer just one box.    Not all of the software is
available yet, but it is rapidly moving.

As an analogy, if the (worse) english system of units was challenged by  not
one, but 5 different, non-compatible metric systems, each with slightly
different standards, could they prevail? I doubt it.

If every workstation maker ran solaris (or any other decent unix) I think it
would help all the players.   Probably too late anyway . . .

For ~6k you can get a pentium II box loaded to the gills with big monitor.   

Andrew



