Warren Marts wrote in message
<01bd1575$e8e1a1e0$a4038a81@pengo.tcct.nmt.edu>...
>Is there much difference between the different OEM motherboards based on
>the 164LX and SX designs - are they generally equivalent to each other
>and DEC's?

No, the SX is for the low cost 21164PC processor, where as the LX(or UX from
Samsung) is
for the 21164A processor. The SX only have 1MB cache onboard, vs min. 2MB on
the LX.

>
>Microway seems to offer more cache (up to 4MB) on their LX boards, but
>that's about the biggest difference I've noticed.  Slots for more than
>6-8 DIMMs might be a feature worth having, but I'm not sure anyone offers
>more than 4 on a board with an SDRAM memory system.

The PC164-UX from Samsung offers 6 DIMM slot's.

>
>Some vendors seem to mention 256 bit paths between main memory and cache
>- is this for real? Reading the DEC product sheets I had thought the DRAM
>interface was 128 bits, just like the CPU <--> off-chip cache links.  My
>back of the envelope calculations suggest (256 bits * 66 Mhz * 2/3 = 1.4
>GByte/sec) this might be needed for the 1.2-1.3 GB/sec figures quoted in
>some places. Interleaving two 128 bit banks would also work, although it
>would seem to require clocking the data lines at 100-150 Mhz.
>Interleaving would at least get you out of having to add DIMMs in
>increments of four for maximum performance.
>
The old PC164 board uses the 21172 chipset which support's 4/8 SIMM's for a
128/256 bits
wide DRAM bus.
The PC164SX/LX/UX mobo's uses the 21174 chipset, which support's 2/4/6 SDRAM
DIMM's
for either 1/2/3 banks 128 bits data bus(100MHz min).


>Technical questions aside, can someone recommend a good source (support
>as well as price) for new Alpha systems to run Linux or NT?  I'd also be
>interested in opinions on the 21164a vs. 21164pc for a workload of
>general software development and some floating point (linear algebra).
>The cost increment in similar final systems looks to be proportionately
>somewhat greater than the increment in SpecINT 95, but less than the
>SpecFP increment. (Given that I'm spending my own money I'll probably be
>buying the 21164pc anyway...)

Personally I use the LX board with great satisfaction. Some of my colleges
at work
uses the old PC164 which is quite ok, but slightly slower than my LX at the
same
processor clock- speed.
I haven't had a change to try the SX yet, but I think it will give the same
performance
as the PC164, given the fact that it runs at 533 vs. 500Mhz, and have the
21174 vs. 21172
chipset, plus of coarse the MVI instruction set. All in all this should put
up for the loss of
the 96KB second-level cache in the 21164PC processor.
If I had to choose today, I'd get the UX board from Samsung, which supports
up to 800MHz
processor speed and according to Samsung will be out in mid. jan.

Have a look at:

    http://www.sec.samsung.com/Products/alpha/alpha-page1.html

Good luck, Anders Blicher Petersen
