Buyer beware!

I recently had an experience that demonstrates that DEC does not stand
behind the products they sell.

Just two years ago I bought their top-of-the-line 133 mHz Starion 940
personal computer.  Recently, I installed Microsoft's Direct X 5.0, which
has become *the standard* software for 3D graphics.  I discovered that
the drivers that came with my Starion 940 are incompatible.  "No
problem", I thought, "I'm sure a large, reputable company like DEC will
have updated drivers for this extrelemely popular software."

So I contacted DEC (by phone -- abysmal web site, no email contact that I
could find), asking where I can find the updated drivers, and I was told
that there would be no updated drivers.

DEC is no longer making Starions, they have no need to sell them, and so
anyone who bought one is just going to have to find a way on their own to
make their PC compatible with modern software.

Come on!  I bought their top of the line PC, for $4,000. I feel that, for
that kind of money, DEC should stand behind their product for the
reasonable useful life of the machine, not abandon their customers the
moment they move on to the next generation of PC's.

What kind of business practice is this??  I'm very disappointed, and from
now on, I'm buying from Dell, who are renown for excellent customer
service. Their website is outstanding, and they have been extremely
supportive, answering all my technical and sales questions *by email*,
with a turnaround of just a few hours.

John Reynolds

